\id ACT Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Acts
\toc1 The Acts of the Apostles.
\toc2 Acts
\toc3 ACT
\mt1 The Acts of the Apostles.
\is THE ARGUMENT OF THE ACTS OF THE APOSTLES.
\ip THE Gospel having shewed, how the Jews most impiously rejected Christ (as also Moyses and the Prophets had foretold of them:) and therefore deserved to be rejected themselves also of him: now followeth this book of the \it Acts of the Apostles\it* ( written by S. Luke in Rome the fourth year of \f - \ft * Hier. in Catal.\f* Nero, An. Dom. 61.) and sheweth, how notwithstanding their deserts, Christ of his mercy (as the Prophets also had foretold of him) offered himself unto that unworthy people, yea after that they had Crucified him, sending unto them his twelve Apostles to move them to penance, and so by Baptism to make them of his Church: and whiles all the Twelve were so occupied about the Jews:
\ip how of a persecuting Jew he made an extraordinary Apostle (who was S. Paul) and to avoid the scandal of the Jews (to whom only himself likewise for the same cause had preached) sent him, and not any of his Twelve by and by, who were his known Apostles, unto the Gentiles, who never afore had heard of Christ, and were worshippers of many Gods, to move them also (for, that likewise the Prophets had foretold) to faith and penance, and so by Baptism to make them of his Church: and how the incredulous Jews every-where resisted the same Apostle and his preaching to the Gentiles, persecuting him and seeking his death, and never ceasing until he fell into the hands of the Gentiles: that so (as not only he every-where, but also the Prophets afore him, and Christ had foretold ) the Gospel might be taken away from them, and given to the Gentiles: even from \f - \ft \+it Act. 13, 46. 18, 6. 19, 9. 28, 28. Mat. 21, 23. Esa. 1. Luc. 13, 33.\+it*\f* Hierusalem (whose reprobation also by name had been often foretold) the head-city of the Jews, where it began, translated to Rome the head-city of the Gentiles. All this will be evident by the parts of the book: which may be these six.
\ip First, how Christ Ascending in the sight of his Disciples, promised unto them the Holy Ghost, foretelling that of him they should receive strength, and so begin his Church in Hierusalem: and from thence dilate it into all that Country, that is into all Jury: yea and into Samaria also, yea into all Nations of the Gentiles, be they never so far off. \it You shall receive\it* (saith he) \it the virtue of the Holy Ghost coming upon you: and you shall be witnesses unto me in Hierusalem, & in all Jury, and Samaria, and even to the utmost of the earth\it*. Chap. 1.
\ip Secondly, the beginning of the Church in Hierusalem, accordingly. Chap. 2.
\ip Thirdly, the propagation of it consequently into all Jury, and also to Samaria. Chap. 8.
\ip Fourthly, the propagation of it to the Gentiles also. Chap. 10.
\ip Fifthly, the taking of it away from the obstinate Jews, and giving of it to the Gentiles, by the ministry of S. Paul and S. Barnabee. Chap. 13.
\ip Sixthly, of taking it away from Hierusalem itself, the head-city of the Jews, and sending it (as it were) to Rome the head-city of the Gentiles, and that, in their persecuting of Paul so far, that \f - \ft \+it Act. 25, 11.\+it*\f* he appealed to Caesar, and so delivering him after a sort unto the Romans: as they had before \f - \ft \+it Luc. 23, 1.\+it*\f* delivered to them also Christ himself. Whereas S. Peter's first coming thither, was upon another occasion, as shall be said anon. Of which Romans and Gentiles therefore, the same S. Paul being now come to Rome (the last Chap. of the Acts ) foretelleth the obstinate Jews there, saying: \it Et\it* \f - \ft \+it Act. 28, 28.\+it*\f* \it ipsi audient\it*: You will not hear, but, \it they will hear\it*: That so the prediction of Christ above rehearsed might be fulfilled: \it And even to the utmost of the earth\it*. And there doth S. Luke end the book, not caring to tell so much as the fulfilling of that which our Lord had foretold (Act. 27. 24.) to S. Paul:
\ip \it Thou must appear before Caesar\it*. Because his purpose was no more but to shew the new Hierusalem of the Christians, where Christ would place the chief seat of his Church: as also indeed the Fathers and all other Catholics have in all Ages looked thither, when they were in any great doubt: no less than the Jews to Hierusalem, as they were appointed in the old Testament. Deut. 17, 8.
\ip And so this Book doth shew the true Church, as plainly, as the Gospel doth shew the true Christ, unto all that do not willfully shut their own eyes. To wit, this to be the true Church, which beginning visibly at Hierusalem, was taken from the Jews, and translated to the Gentiles (and namely to Rome) continuing visibly, and visibly to continue hereafter also, \it Until the fullness of\it* \f - \ft \+it Rom. 11, 25.\+it*\f* \it the Gentiles shall be come in\it*: that then also \it All Israel may be saved.\it* And then is come the end of the world. For so did Christ most plainly foretell us: \it This Gospel of the Kingdom shall be preached\it* \f - \ft \+it Mat. 24, 14.\+it*\f* \it in the whole world, for a testimony to all Nations: and then shall come the consummation.\it* For the conversion of which Nations and accomplishing the fullness of all Gentiles, the foresaid Church Catholic, being mindful of her office, \it to be Christ's witness even to the utmost of the earth\it*, doth at this present (as always) send preachers to convert and make them also Christians: whereas the Protestants and all other Heretics do nothing else but subvert such as before were Christians. \f - \ft Tertul. de praes.\f*
\ip And this being the Sum and scope of this Book, thus to give us historically a just sight of the fulfilling of the Prophets & Christ's prediction about the Church: it is not to be marveled at, why it telleth not of S. Peter's coming to Rome: considering that his first coming thither was not, as S. Paul's was, by the Jews' delivery of him, working so to their own reprobation, but upon another occasion, to wit, to confound Simon Magus. \it Eus. Hist. li. 2. c. 12. 13.\it* For who also seeth not, that it maketh no mention of his preaching to any Gentiles at all, those few only Act. 10. excepted, who were the first, and therefore (lest the Gentiles should seem less cared for of God, than the Jews) Peter being the Head of all, was elected of God, to incorporate them into the Church, as before he had done the Jews. \it God\it* (saith he) \it among us chose, that by my mouth the Gentiles should hear\it* \f - \ft \+it Act. 15, 7.\+it*\f* \it the word of the Gospel, and believe.\it* And S. James thereupon: \it Simon hath told how God first\it* \f - \ft \+it Act. 15, 14.\+it*\f* \it visited to take of the Gentiles a people to his name.\it* But otherwise (I say) here is no mention of Peter's preaching to any Gentiles: no nor of the other eleven Apostles. Will any man therefore infer, that neither Peter, nor the other Eleven preached to any Nation or city of the Gentiles? No, the meaning of the Holy Ghost was not to write all the Acts of all the Apostles, no nor the preaching of Peter and his, to the Gentiles, but only to the Jews: thereby to set out unto the world, the great mercy of Christ toward those unworthy Jews, and consequently their most worthy reprobation for contemning such grace and mercy. As also on the other side to shew, how readily the Gentiles in so many Nations, were converted by one Apostle only, who \it From Hierusalem\it* \f - \ft \+it Rom. 15, 19.\+it*\f* \it even to Illyricum replenished the Gospel of Christ.\it* And this parting of the work so made by S. Peter with the rest doth S. Paul himself touch: \it That we unto the Gentiles, and they unto the\it* \f - \ft \+it Gal. 2, 9.\+it*\f* \it Circumcision.\it* Nevertheless before his coming to Rome, not only was the Church come to Rome (as it is evident Act. the last chap.) there planted by Saint Peter and others (as likewise by Saint Peter it was planted in the first Gentiles, before that S. Paul began the taking of it away from the multitude of the Jews, and the translating of it to the multitude of the Gentiles) but also so notable was the same Church of Rome, that S. Paul writing his Epistle to the Romans, before he came thither, saith: \it Your faith is renowned in the whole world.\it* And therefore they with the rest of the Gentiles, be \f - \ft \+it Rom. 1, 8.\+it*\f* that Nation whereof Christ told the Jews, saying: \it The Kingdom of God shall be taken away from\it* \f - \ft \+it Mat. 21, 43.\+it*\f* \it you, and shall be given to a Nation yielding the fruits thereof.\it*
\ip As before we noted the Gospels, as they are read both at Matins and Mass, throughout the year, in their convenient time and place: so the books following (as also the books of the old Testament) are read in the said Service of the Church, for Epistles and Lessons, in their time & place, as hereafter shall be noted in every of them. \it See the very same order and custom of the primitive Church, in S. Ambrose ep. 33. S. Augustine Serm. de Tempore 139, 140, 141, 144. S. Leo Ser. 2. & 4. de Quadrag. & Ser. 13. & 19. de Pass. Domini. S. Gregory in his 40 homilies upon the Gospels.\it*
\c 1
\cd Christ now ready to ascend, biddeth the Apostles to expect the Holy Ghost which he had promised, foretelling where (being strengthened by him) they should begin his Church, and how far they should carry it. 9. After his Ascension they are warned by two Angels to set their minds upon his second coming. 14. In the days of their expectation, 15. Peter beginneth to execute his vicarship, giving instruction and order, by which Mathias is elected Apostle in the place of Judas. 83. 93. \f - \ft The CHURCH readeth this book at Matins from Low Sunday unto the 3. Sunday after Easter: even as in S. Augustine's time. See serm. & de Diversis. to. 10.\f* \f - \ft The 1. part. The expectation of the Holy Ghost between the Ascension of Christ, and the beginning of the Church.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc The\sc* \x - \xt Lu. 1, 3.\x* \f 2 \fr 1.1 \ft The Epistle at Mass upon Ascension day.\f* first treatise I made of \f 3 \fr 1.1 \ft Not all particularly, (for the other Evangelists write diverse things not touched by him) but all the principal and most necessary things.\f* all things, O Theophilus, which \sc Jesus\sc* began to do and to teach,
\v 2 until the day wherein giving commandment by the Holy Ghost to the Apostles whom he chose, he was assumpted. \ef - \fr 1.2 \fq Giving commandment. \ft He meaneth the power given them to preach, to baptize, to remit sins, and generally the whole commission and charge of government of the Church after him and in his name, stead, and right. The which Regiment was given them together with the Holy Ghost to assist them therein for ever.\ef*
\v 3 to whom he shewed also himself alive after his passion in many arguments, for forty days appearing to them, and speaking of the Kingdom of God.
\v 4 And eating with them, \x - \xt Lu. 24, 49.\x* he commanded them, that they should not depart from Jerusalem, but should expect the promise of the Father, which you \x - \xt Io. 14, 26.\x* have heard (saith he) by my mouth:
\v 5 for John indeed baptized with \x - \xt Lu. 3, 16.\x* water, but \f 2 \fr 1.5 \ft John's Baptism gave not the Holy Ghost.\f* you shall be \f 3 \fr 1.5 \ft The aboundant pouring of the Holy Ghost upon them on Whitsunday, he calleth Baptism.\f* baptized with the Holy Ghost after these few days.
\v 6 They therefore that were assembled, asked him, saying: Lord, whether at this time wilt thou restore the Kingdom to Israel?
\v 7 but he said to them: It is not for you to know times or moments, which the Father hath put in his own power: \ef - \fr 1.7 \fq It is not for you. \ft ¹ It is not for us, nor needful for the Church, to know the times & moments of the world, the coming of Antichrist, and such other God's secrets. This is enough in that case, to be assured that Christ's faith shall be preached, and the Church spread throughout all Nations, the Holy Ghost concurring continually with the Apostles and their Successors for the same. \fq ¹ \ft The times and moments of things to come pertain not to us.\ef*
\v 8 but you shall receive the \x - \xt Lu. 24, 49. Act. 2, 1.\x* virtue of the Holy Ghost coming upon you, and you shall be witnesses unto me in Jerusalem, and in all Jewry, and Samaria, and even to the utmost of the earth.
\v 9 And \x - \xt Mar. 16, 19. Lu. 24, 51.\x* when he had said these things, in their sight he was elevated: & a cloud received him out of their sight.
\v 10 And when they beheld him going into Heaven, behold two men stood beside them in white garments,
\v 11 who also said: Ye men of Galilee, why stand you looking into Heaven? This \sc Jesus\sc* which is assumpted from you into Heaven, shall so come as you have seen him going into Heaven. \ef - \fr 1.11 \fq Assumpted from you. \ft ¹ By this visible Ascending of Christ to heaven and like return from thence to judgement, the Heretics do incredulously argue him not to be in the Sacrament. But let the faithful rather give ear to S. Chrysostom saying thus: \+it O miracle, he that sitteth with the Father in heaven above, at the very same time is handled of men beneath. Christ ascending to heaven, both hath his flesh with him, and left it with us beneath. Elias being taken up, left to his Disciple his cloak only: but the Son of man ascending left his own flesh to us. Li. 3. de Sacerd. Ho. 2. ad po. Ant. in fine. Ho. de divit. & paup. in fine.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Christ is ascended, and yet really in the B. Sacrament.\ef*
\v 12 Then they returned to Jerusalem from the mount that is called Olivet, which is by Jerusalem, distant a Sabboth's journey.
\v 13 And when they were entered in, they went up into an upper chamber, where abode \f 1 \fr 1.13 \ft This visible company was the true Church of Christ, which he left, & commanded to keep together till the coming of the Holy Ghost, by Him to be further informed & furnished to gain all nations to the same Society.\f* Peter & John, James and Andrew, Philippe and Thomas, Bartholomew and Matthew, James of Alphæus and Simon Zelotes, and Jude of James.
\v 14 All these were persevering with one mind in prayer with the \f 1 \fr 1.14 \ft The Heretics, some in the text, other in the margent, translate, \+it wives,\+it* to wit, of the Apostles, most impudently, knowing in their consciences that he meaneth the Marys & other holy women that followed Christ as \+it Lu. 8, 2. 24, 10.\+it* See Beza & the Engl. Bible 1579.\f* women and \sc Mary\sc* the mother of \sc Jesus\sc*, and his brethren. \ef - \fr 1.14 \fq MARY the mother of JESUS. \ft ¹ This is the last mention that is made in holy Scripture, of our B. Lady. For though she were full of all divine wisdom, and opened (no doubt) unto the Evangelists and other writers of holy Scriptures, diverse of Christ's actions, speeches, and mysteries, whereof she had both experimental and revealed knowledge: Yet for that she was a woman, and the humblest creature living, and the pattern of all order and obedience, it pleased not God that there should be any further note of her life, doings, or death, in the Scriptures. ² She lived the rest of her time with the Christians (as here she is peculiarly named and noted among them) and specially with S. John the Apostle, ³ to whom our Lord recommended her. Who provided for her all necessaries, her spouse Joseph (as it may be thought) being deceased before. The common opinion is that she lived 63 years in all. ⁴ At the time of her death, (as S. Denys first, & after him S. Damascene \+it de dormit. Deiparæ\+it*, writeth,) ⁵ all the Apostles then dispersed into diverse nations to preach the Gospel, were miraculously brought together (saving S. Thomas who came the third day after) to Hierusalem, to honour her divine departure and funeral, as the said S. Denys witnesseth. Who saith that himself, S. Timothee, and S. Hierotheus were present: testifying also of his own hearing, that both before her death and after for three days, not only the Apostles and other holy men present, but the Angels also and Powers of heaven did sing most melodious Hymns. They buried her sacred body in Gethsemani, but for S. Thomas' sake, who desired to see and to reverence it, they opened the sepulchre the third day, and finding it void of the holy body, but exceedingly fragrant, they returned, ⁶ assuredly deeming that her body was assumpted into heaven, as the Church of God holdeth, being most agreeable to the singular privilege of the mother of God, and therefore celebrateth most solemnly the day of her Assumption. And that is consonant not only to the said S. Denys, and S. Damascene, but to holy Athanasius also, who avoucheth the same, \+it Serm. in Evang. de Deipara.\+it* Of which Assumption of her body, S. Bernard also wrote five notable sermons extant in his works. \fq ¹ \ft Our B. LADY. \fq ² \ft Her life. \fq ³ \ft \+it Io. 19, 26. 27.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Her death. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Dionys. ep. ad Timotheum.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft Her ASSUMPTION.\ef* \ef - \fr 1.14 \ft ¹ But neither these holy Fathers, nor the Church's tradition and testimony, do bear any sway now a days with the Protestants, that have abolished this her greatest feast of her Assumption. Who of reason should at the least celebrate it as the day of her death, as they do of other Saints. For though they believe not that her body is assumpted, yet they will not (we trow) deny that she is dead, and her soul in glory: neither can they ask scriptures for that, no more than they require for the deaths of Peter, Paul, John, and other, which be not mentioned in scriptures & yet are still celebrated by the Protestants. But concerning the B. Virgin \+sc Mary\+sc*, they have blotted out also both her Nativity, and her Conception: so as it may be thought the Devil beareth a special malice to this woman whose seed brake his head. For as for the other two days of her Purification & Annunciation, they be not proper to our Lady, but the one to Christ's Conception, the other to his Presentation, so that she by this means shall have no festivity at all. ² \fq ¹ \ft The Protestants have no feast of her at all, as they have of other Saints. \fq ² \ft How the Primitive Church & ancient Fathers honoured our B. Lady.\ef* \ef - \fr 1.14 \ft But contrarywise, to consider how the ancient Church and Fathers esteemed, spake, and wrote of this excellent vessel of grace, may make us detest these men's impiety, that cannot abide the praises of her ¹ whom all generations should call blessed, and that esteem her honours a derogation to her son. Some of their speeches we will set down, that all men may see, that we neither praise her, nor pray to her, more amply than they did. ² S. Athanasius in the place alleged, after he had declared how all the Angelical spirits and every order of them honoured and praised her with the \+sc Ave\+sc*, wherewith S. Gabriel saluted her: we also, saith he, of all degrees upon the earth extol thee with loud voice, saying, \+it Ave gratia plena &c. Hail full of grace, our Lord is with thee pray for us o Mistress, and Lady, and Queen, and mother of God.\+it* Most holy and ancient ³ Ephrem, also in a special oration made in praise of our Lady, saith thus in diverse places thereof, \+it Intemerata Deipara\+it* &c. \+it Mother of God undefiled, Queen of all, the hope of them that despair, my lady most glorious, higher than the heavenly spirits, more honourable than the Cherubins, holier than the Seraphins, and without comparison more glorious than the supernal hosts, the hope of the Fathers, the glory of the Prophets, the praise of the Apostles.\+it* And a little after. \+it Virgo ante partum, in partu, & post partum. by thee we are reconciled to Christ my God, thy son: thou art the helper of sinners, thou the haven for them that are tossed with storms, the solace of the world, the deliverer of the imprisoned, the help of orphans, the redemption of captives.\+it* And afterward, \+it Vouchsafe me thy servant to praise thee. Hail lady \+sc Mary\+sc* full of grace, hail Virgin most blessed among women.\+it* And much more in that sense which were too long to repeat. ⁴ \fq ¹ \ft \+it * Lu. 1, 48.\+it* \fq ² \ft S. Athanasius. \fq ³ \ft S. Ephrem. \fq ⁴ \ft S. Cyril.\ef* \ef - \fr 1.14 \ft S. Cyril hath the like wonderful speeches of her honour, \+it hom. 6. contra Nestorium. Praise and glory be to thee o holy Trinity: to thee also be praise, holy mother of God, for thou art the precious pearl of the world, thou the candle of unquenchable light, the crown of Virginity, the sceptre of the Catholic faith. By thee the Trinity is glorified and adored in all the world: by thee heaven rejoiceth, Angels and Archangels are glad, devils are put to flight, and man is called again to heaven, and every creature that was held with the error of Idols, is turned to the knowledge of the truth: by thee Churches are founded through the world: thee being their helper, the Gentiles come to penance.\+it* and much more which we omit. Likewise ¹ the Greek Liturgies or Masses of S. James, S. Basil, and S. Chrysostom, make most honourable mention of our B. Lady, praying unto her, saluting her with the Angelical hymn, \+it Ave Maria\+it*, and using these speeches, \+it Most holy, undefiled, blessed above all, our Queen, our Lady, the mother of God, \+sc Mary\+sc*, a virgin for ever, the sacred ark of Christ's Incarnation, broader than the heavens that didst bear thy creator, holy mother, of unspeakable light, we magnify thee with Angelical hymns. All things pass understanding, all things are glorious in thee o mother of God. By thee the mystery before unknown to the Angels, is made manifest and revealed to them on the earth. Thou art more honourable than the Cherubins, and more glorious than the Seraphins. To thee, O full of grace, all creatures, both men and Angels do gratulate and rejoice: glory be to thee, Which art a sanctified temple, a spiritual paradise, the glory of virgins, of whom God took flesh and made thy womb to be his throne.\+it* &c. ² \fq ¹ \ft The Greek Liturgies of S. James, S. Basil, S. Chrysostom. \fq ² \ft S. Augustine.\ef* \ef - \fr 1.14 \ft And S. Augustine \+it Serm. 18. de Sanctis to. 10\+it*: or (as some think) S. Fulgentius: \+it O blessed \+sc Mary\+sc*, who can be able worthily to praise or thank thee, receive our prayers, obtain us our requests, for thou art the\+it* ¹ \+it special hope of sinners, by thee we hope for pardon of our sins, & in thee, o most blessed, is the expectation of our rewards.\+it* And then follow these words now used in the Church's service: ² \+it Sancta Maria succurre miseris, iuva pusillanimes, refove flebiles, ora pro populo, interveni pro clero, intercede pro devoto foemineo sexu. Sentiant omnes tuum iuvamen, quicunque celebrant tuam commemorationem. Pray thou continually for the people of God, which didst deserve to bear the redeemer of the world, Who liveth and reigneth for ever.\+it* ³ S. Damascene also \+it ser.\+it* de dormitione Deiparæ. \+it Let us cry with Gabriel, Ave gratia plena, Hail full of grace, Hail sea of joy that cannot be emptied, hail the\+it* ⁴ \+it only ease of griefs, hail holy virgin, by whom death was expelled, and life brought in.\+it* See ⁵ S. Irenæus li. 3. c. 33. and li. 5. circa medium, & S. Augustine \+it de fide & Symbolo. & de agone Christiano.\+it* Where they declare how both the sexes concur to our salvation, the man and the woman, Christ and our Lady, ⁶ as Adam and Eve both were the cause of our fall. Though Adam far more than his wife, and so Christ far more excellently and in another sort than our Lady: who (though his mother) yet is but his creature and handmaid, himself being truly both God and man. In all which places alleged & ⁷ many other like to these, if it please the reader to see and read, and make his own eyes witnesses, he shall perceive that there is much more said of her, and to her, than we have here recited, and that the very same or the like speeches and terms were used then, that the Church useth now, in the honour and invocation of the B. Virgin: to the confusion of all those that willfully will not understand in what sense all such speeches are applied unto her, to wit, either because of her prayer and intercession for us, ⁸ whereby she is our hope, our refuge, our advocate &c. or because she brought forth the author of our redemption and salvation, whereby she is the mother of mercy, and grace, and life, and whatsoever goodness we receive by Christ. \fq ¹ \ft \+it * unica spes\+it* \fq ² \ft Sancta MARIA succurre miseris, &c. \fq ³ \ft S. Damascene. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it * unicum levamen\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft S. Irenæus. \fq ⁶ \ft As Adam and Eve, so Christ & our Lady. \fq ⁷ \ft \+it See S. Greg. Nazian. in fi. Trag. Christus patiens.\+it* \fq ⁸ \ft The meaning of the titles and terms given to our B. Lady.\ef*
\v 15 \f 1 \fr 1.15 \ft a The Epistle upon S. Matthias day \+it Febr. 24.\+it*\f* In those days Peter rising up in the midst of the Brethren, said: (and the multitude of persons together, was almost an hundred and twenty.) \ef - \fr 1.15 \fq Peter rising up. \ft ¹ Peter in the mean time practised his Superiority in the company or Church, publishing an election to be made of one to supply Judas' room. Which Peter did not upon commandment of Christ written, but by suggestion of God's Spirit, and by understanding the scriptures of the old Testament to that purpose: the sense whereof Christ had opened to the Apostles before his departure, though in more full manner afterward at the sending of the Holy Ghost. And this act of Peter in prescribing to the Apostles and the rest, this election, and the manner thereof, is so evident for his Supremacy, that ² the Adversaries confess here that he was, \+it Antistes\+it*, the chief and Bishop of this whole College and company. \fq ¹ \ft Peter beginneth to practise his Primacy. \fq ² \ft \+it Beza in no. Test. Græcol. an. 1565.\+it*\ef*
\v 16 You men, brethren, the \x - \xt Ps. 40, 9. Io. 13, 18.\x* scripture must be fulfilled which the Holy Ghost spake before by the mouth of David concerning Judas, who was the \x - \xt Luc. 22, 47. Io. 18, 3.\x* captain of them that apprehended \sc Jesus\sc*:
\v 17 who was numbered among us and obtained the lot of this ministry.
\v 18 And he indeed hath possessed a \x - \xt Mt. 27, 5. 7.\x* field of the reward of iniquity, and being hanged he burst in the midst, and all his bowels gushed out.
\v 19 And it was made notorious to all the inhabitants of Jerusalem: so that the same field was called in their tongue, \it Hacel-dema,\it* that is to say, the field of blood.
\v 20 For it is written in the book of Psalms: \x - \xt Psa. 68, 26.\x* \it Be their habitation made desert, & be there none to dwell in it. \it* \x - \xt Ps. 108, 8.\x* \it And his Bishopric let another take.\it*
\v 21 Therefore, of these men that have assembled with us, all the time that our Lord \sc Jesus\sc* went in and went out among us,
\v 22 beginning from the Baptism of John until the day wherein he was assumpted from us, \f 1 \fr 1.22 \ft No small mystery, that the number of the twelve Apostles must needs be made up again.\f* there must one of these be made a witness with us of his resurrection.
\v 23 And they appointed two, Joseph, who was called Barsabas, who was surnamed Justus: and Matthias.
\v 24 And praying they said: Thou Lord that knowest the hearts of all men, shew of these two, one, whom thou hast chosen,
\v 25 to take the place of this ministry & Apostleship, from the which Judas hath prevaricated that he might go to his own place.
\v 26 And they gave them lots, and the lot fell upon Matthias, and he was numbered with the eleven Apostles. \ef - \fr 1.26 \fq Lots. \ft ¹ When the even or fall of the lot is not expected of Devils, nor of the stars, nor of any force of fortune, but looked and prayed for to be directed by God and his holy Saints: then may lots be used lawfully. And sometimes to discern betwixt two thins mere indifferent, they be necessary, as S. Augustine teacheth \+it ep. 180. ad Honoratum.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Casting of lots.\ef*
\c 2
\cd The Holy Ghost coming to the Faithful upon Whitsunday, 5. Jews in Hierusalem of all Nations do wonder to hear them speak all tongues. 14. And Peter to the deriders declareth, that it is not drunkenness, but the Holy Ghost which Joel did prophesy of, which JESUS (whom they crucified) being now risen again and ascended (as he sheweth also out of the Scriptures) hath poured out from heaven, concluding therefore that he is CHRIST, and they most horrible murderers. 37. Whereat they being compunct, and submitting themselves, he telleth them that they must be baptized, and then they also shall receive the same Holy Ghost, as being promised to all the baptized. 41. And so 3000 are baptized that very day. 42. Whose godly exercises are here reported, and also their living in state of perfection. The Apostles work many miracles, and God daily increaseth the number of the Church. \f - \ft The 2. part. The coming of the Holy Ghost and beginning of the Church in Hierusalem.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \x - \xt Act. 1, 4.\x*\f 2 \fr 2.1 \ft The Epistle upon Whitsunday.\f* when the days of Pentecost were accomplished, they were all together in one place: \ef - \fr 2.1 \fq The days of Pentecost. \ft ¹ As Christ our Pasch, for correspondence to the figure, was offered at the Jews' great feast of Pasch, so fifty days after (in Greek, Pentecost) for accomplishing the like figure of the Law-giving in Mount Sinai, he sent down the Holy Ghost just on the day of their Pentecost. Which was always on Sunday, as appeareth \+it Levit. 23, 15.\+it* Both which days the Church keepeth yearly for memory of Christ's death and Resurrection, and the sending down of the Holy Ghost: as they did the like for record of their delivery out of Ægypt, and their Law giving aforesaid. The said Feasts with us containing, besides the remembrance of benefits past, great Sacraments also of the life to come. \+it Aug. ep. 119. C. 16.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The feasts of Pasch & Pentecost.\ef*
\v 2 and suddenly there was made a sound from Heaven, as of a vehement wind coming, & it filled the whole house where they were sitting.
\v 3 And there appeared to them parted tongues as it were \x - \xt Mt. 3, 11.\x* of fire, and it sat upon every one of them:
\v 4 and they were all replenished with the \x - \xt Io. 7, 39.\x* \sc Holy Ghost\sc*, and they began to speak with diverse tongues, according as the \sc Holy Ghost\sc* gave them to speak. \ef - \fr 2.4 \fq All replenished. \ft ¹ Though the Apostles and the rest were baptized before, and had thereby received the grace of the Holy Ghost to sanctification and remission of sins, as for diverse other purposes also: Yet as Christ ² promised them they should be further endued with strength and virtue from above, so here he fulfilleth his promise, visibly pouring down the Holy Ghost upon all the company and upon every one of them, thereby replenishing the Apostles specially with all truth, wisdom, and knowledge necessary for the government of the Church, and giving both to them and to all other present, the grace and effect of the Sacrament of Confirmation, accomplishing, corroborating, and strengthening them in their faith and the confession of the same. And lastly for a visible token of God's Spirit, he endued them all with the gift of diverse strange tongues: all (I say) there present, as well ³ our Lady, as other holy women and brethren, besides the Apostles. Though ⁴ the Heretics fondly argue, for the desire they have to dishonour Christ's mother, that neither she nor they were there present, nor had the gift of tongues, contrary to the plain text that saith, \+it They were all together\+it*, to wit, all the 120 mentioned before c. 1, 15. \fq ¹ \ft The sending of the Holy Ghost on Whitsunday and the effects thereof. \fq ² \ft \+it Lu. 24, 49.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Our B. Lady. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Beza Annot. in hoc cap. v. 1.\+it*\ef*
\v 5 And there were dwelling at Jerusalem Jews, devout men of every nation that is under Heaven.
\v 6 And when this voice was made, the multitude came together, and was astonished in mind, because every man heard them speak in his own tongue.
\v 7 And they were all amazed, and marveled saying: Are not, lo, all these that speak, Galilæans,
\v 8 and how \f 1 \fr 2.8 \fq have we heard \ft \+it do we hear.\+it*\f* have we heard, each man our own tongue wherein we were born?
\v 9 Parthians, & Medians, & Elamites, & that inhabit Mesopotamia, Jewry, & Cappadocia, Pontus, and Asia,
\v 10 Phrygia, and Pamphilia, Ægypt & the parts of Libya that is about Cyrenee, & strangers of Rome,
\v 11 Jews also, and Proselytes, Cretensians, and Arabians: we \f 1 \fr 2.11 \fq have heard \ft \+it do hear\+it*\f* have heard them speak in our own tongues the great works of God.
\v 12 And they were all astonished, and marveled, saying one to another: What meaneth this?
\v 13 But others deriding said: That these are full of new wine.
\v 14 \f 1 \fr 2.14 \ft a The lesson before the Epistle on Imber Wednesday in Whitsunweek.\f* But \f 2 \fr 2.14 \ft Peter the head of the rest and now newly replenished with all knowledge and fortitude, maketh the first Sermon.\f* Peter standing with the Eleven, lifted up his voice; and spake to them: Ye men, Jews, and all you that dwell in Jerusalem, be this known to you, and with your ears receive my words.
\v 15 For these are not drunk, as you suppose, whereas it is the third hour of the day:
\v 16 But this is it that was said by the Prophet Joel:
\v 17 \x - \xt Ioel. 2, 28.\x* \it And it shall be, in the last days (saith our Lord) of my Spirit I will pour out upon all flesh: and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, and your young men shall see visions, and your Ancients shall dream dreams.\it*
\v 18 \it and upon my servants truly, and upon my handmaids will I pour out in those days, of my Spirit, and they shall prophesy:\it*
\v 19 \it and I will give wonders in the Heaven above, and signs in the earth beneath, blood and fire, and vapour of smoke.\it*
\v 20 \it The sun shall be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood, before the great and manifest day of our Lord doth come.\it*
\v 21 \it And it shall be, every one whosoever calleth upon the name of our Lord, shall be saved.\it*
\v 22 Ye men of Israel hear these words: \sc Jesus\sc* of Nazareth a man approved of God among you, by miracles & wonders and signs which God did by him in the midst of you, as you also know;
\v 23 this same, by the determinate counsel & prescience of God being delivered, you by the hands of wicked men have crucified and slain. \ef - \fr 2.23 \fq By the determinate counsel of God delivered. \ft ¹ God delivered him, and he delivered himself, for love and intention of our salvation, and so the act was holy and God's own determination. But the Jews and others which betrayed and crucified him, did it of malice and wicked purpose, and their fact was damnable, and not of God's counsel or causing: though he tolerated it, for that he could and did turn their abominable fact to the good of our salvation. Therefore abhor those new Manichees of our time, both Lutherans and Calvinists, that make God the author and cause of Judas' betraying of Christ, no less than of Paul's conversion; besides the false translation of ² Beza, saying for God's prescience or foreknowledge (in the Greek, \+it πρόγνωσις\+it* ) \+it God's providence\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft God's determination that Christ should die, excuseth not the Jews. \fq ² \ft Beza.\ef*
\v 24 Whom God hath raised up loosing the sorrows of hell, according as it was impossible that he should be holden of it. \ef - \fr 2.24 \fq Loosing the sorrows. \ft Christ was not in pains himself, but loosed other men of those dolours of Hell, wherewith it was impossible himself should be touched. \+it See\+it* ¹ \+it S. Augustine.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft \+it * Li. 12. c. 13. de Gen. ad lit.\+it*\ef*
\v 25 For David saith concerning him: \x - \xt Ps. 15, 8.\x* \it I foresaw the Lord in my sight always: because he is at my right hand that I be not moved.\it*
\v 26 \it For this, my heart hath been glad and my tongue hath rejoiced: moreover my flesh also shall rest in hope.\it*
\v 27 \it Because thou wilt not leave my soul \it* \f 1 \fr 2.27 \ft Who but an infidel (saith S. Augustine) will deny Christ to have descended to Hell? \+it ep. 99.\+it*\f* \it in hell, nor give thy Holy one to see \it* \f 2 \fr 2.27 \ft As his soul suffered no pains in Hell, so neither did his body take any corruption in the grave.\f* \it corruption.\it* \ef - \fr 2.27 \fq My soul in Hell. \ft ¹ Where all the Faithful, according to the Creed, ever have believed, that Christ according to his soul, went down to Hell, to deliver the Patriarchs and all just men there holden in bondage till his death, and the Apostle here citing the Prophet's words, most evidently expresseth the same, distinguishing his soul in Hell, from his body in the grave: Yet the Calvinists to defend against God's express words, the blasphemy of their Master, that Christ suffered the pains of Hell, and that no where but upon the Cross, and that otherwise he descended not into Hell, most falsely and flatly here corrupt the text, by turning and wresting both the Hebrew and Greek words ² from their most proper and usual significations of, \+it Soul\+it*, and \+it Hell\+it*, into \+it body\+it*, and \+it grave\+it*: saying for, \+it my soul in Hell\+it*, thus, \+it my body,\+it* ³ \+it life, person\+it*, year (as Beza in his new Testament an. 1556) \+it my carcass\+it* in the \+it grave\+it*. and this later they corrupt almost throughout the Bible for that purpose. But for refelling of both corruptions, it shall be sufficient in this place: first, that all Hebrews & Greeks, and all that understand these tongues, know that the foresaid Hebrew & Greek words are as proper, peculiar, and usual to signify, \+it soul\+it* and \+it Hell\+it*, as \+it anima\+it* and \+it infernus\+it* in Latin, yea as \+it soul\+it* and \+it Hell\+it* in English do properly signify the soul of man, and Hell that is opposite to heaven: and that they are as unproperly used to signify body and grave, as to say in English, soul for body, or Hell for grave. Secondly, it doth so mislike the Heretics themselves, that Castaleo one of their fine Translators refelleth it, and to make it the more sure, he for, \+it in inferno\+it*, translateth, \+it in Orco\+it*, that is, \+it in Hell\+it*. Thirdly, Beza himself partly recanteth in his later edition, and confesseth that, \+it Carcass\+it*, was no fit word for the body of Christ, and therefore, \+it I have\+it* (saith he) \+it changed it, but I retain and keep the same sense still.\+it* meaning, that he hath now translated it, \+it soul\+it*, but that he meaneth thereby as before, Christ's dead body. Fourthly, ⁴ he saith plainly, that translating thus, \+it Thou shalt not leave my carcass in the grave\+it*, he did it of purpose against \+it Limbus Patrum\+it*, Purgatory, and Christ's descending into Hell, which he calleth foul errors, and marveleth, \+it that most of the ancient Fathers were in that error\+it*: namely of Christ's descending into Hell, and delivering the old Fathers. What need we more? He opposeth himself both against plain Scriptures and all ancient Fathers, perverting the one, and contemning the other, to overthrow that truth which is an Article of our \+it Crede\+it*. Whereby it is evidently false which some of them say for their defence, that none of them did ever of purpose translate falsely. See the Annotation upon \+it 1. Pet. 3. v. 19.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Corrupt translation against the Article of Christ's descending into Hell. \fq ² \ft נפש \+it ψυχὴ\+it* שאול \+it ᾅδου\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it * Eng. Bible. 1579.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it * No. Test. an. 1556. annot. in 2. Act. v. 27. & 24. & in 1. Pet. 3, 19.\+it*\ef*
\v 28 \it Thou hast made known to me the ways of life: thou shalt make me full of joyfulness with thy face.\it*
\v 29 Ye men, Brethren, let me boldly speak to you of the Patriarch David: that \x - \xt 3. Reg. 2, 10.\x* he died, and was buried; and his sepulchre is with us until this present day.
\v 30 Whereas therefore he was a Prophet, and knew that by an oath \x - \xt Ps. 131, 11.\x* \it God hath sworn to him, that of the fruit of his loins there should sit upon his seat;\it*
\v 31 foreseeing, he spake of the resurrection of Christ, for neither was he left in hell, neither did his flesh see corruption.
\v 32 This \sc Jesus\sc* hath God raised again, whereof all we are witnesses.
\v 33 Being exalted therefore by the right hand of God, and having received of his Father the promise of the Holy Ghost, he hath poured out \f 1 \fr 2.33 \fq this whom \ft \+it this gift which\+it*\f* this whom you see and hear.
\v 34 For David ascended not into Heaven; but he saith: \x - \xt Ps. 109, 1.\x* \it Our Lord hath said to my Lord, sit on my right hand.\it*
\v 35 \it until I make thine enemies the foot-stool of thy feet.\it*
\v 36 Therefore let all the house of Israel know most certainly that God hath made him both Lord, & \sc Christ\sc*, this \sc Jesus\sc*, whom you have crucified.
\v 37 \f 1 \fr 2.37 \ft Contrition.\f* And hearing these things, they were compunct in heart, and said to Peter and to the rest of the Apostles: What shall we do men, brethren.
\v 38 But Peter said to them, \f 1 \fr 2.38 \ft Not only amendment of life, but penance also required before Baptism, in such as be of age, though not in that sort as afterward in the Sacrament of penance. \+it Aug. de fid. et oper. c. 11. et ep. 108.\+it*\f* do penance, & be every one of you baptized in the name of \sc Jesus Christ\sc* for remission of your sins; and you shall receive the gift of the Holy Ghost.
\v 39 For to you is the promise, and to your children, and to all that are far off, whomsoever the Lord our God shall call.
\v 40 With very many other words also did he testify and exhorted them, saying: Save yourselves from this perverse Generation.
\v 41 They therefore that received his word, were baptized: & there were added in that day about \f 1 \fr 2.41 \ft Three thousand were converted at this first sermon, & they were put to the other visible company and Church.\f* three thousand souls.
\v 42 And they were persevering in the doctrine of the Apostles, and in the communication of \f 1 \fr 2.42 \ft c This was the B. Sacrament, which the Apostles daily ministered to the Christians at least in one kind. See chap. 20, 7.\f* the breaking of bread, and prayers.
\v 43 And fear came upon every soul; many wonders also and signs were done by the Apostles in Jerusalem, and there was great fear in all.
\v 44 All they also that believed, were together, \x - \xt Act. 4, 32.\x* & had all things common. \ef - \fr 2.44 \fq All things common. \ft ¹ This living in common is not a rule or a precept to all Christian men, as the Anabaptists falsely pretend: but a life of perfection and counsel, followed of our Religious in the Catholic Church. \+it See S. Aug. in Ps. 132. in principio. & ep. 109.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Living in common.\ef*
\v 45 Their possessions and substance they sold, and divided them to all, according as every one had need.
\v 46 Daily also continuing with one accord in the temple, and breaking bread from house to house, they took their meat with joy and simplicity of heart:
\v 47 praising God, and having grace with all the people. And our Lord increased them that should be saved, daily together. \ef - \fr 2.47 \fq Increased. \ft ¹ More and more were added to the Church (as the Greek ² more plainly expresseth) that we may see the visible propagation & increase of the same. From which time a diligent man may deduce the very same visible Society of men joined in Christ, through the whole book, and afterward by the Ecclesiastical story, down till our days, against the pretensed invisible Church of the Heretics. \fq ¹ \ft The increase & perpetuity of the VISIBLE CHURCH. \fq ² \ft \+it προσετίθει τῇ ἐκκλησίᾳ.\+it*\ef*
\c 3
\cd A miracle, and a Sermon of Peter's to the people, shewing that JESUS is Christ, and exhorting them to faith in him and penance for their sins, and so they shall have by him (in Baptism) the Benediction which was promised to Abraham.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \f 1 \fr 3.1 \ft The Epistle upon SS. Peter and Paul's eve. Iun. 28.\f* Peter and John went up into the temple, at the \f 2 \fr 3.1 \ft This maketh for distinction of Canonical hours and diversity of appointed times to pray in. \+it See Annot. c. 10, 9.\+it*\f* ninth hour of prayer.
\v 2 And a certain man that was lame from his mother's womb, was carried; whom they laid every day at the gate of the temple, that is called Specious, that he might ask alms of them that went into the temple.
\v 3 He, when he had seen Peter and John about to enter into the temple, asked to receive an alms.
\v 4 But Peter with John looking upon him, said: Look upon us.
\v 5 But he looked earnestly upon them, hoping that he should receive something of them.
\v 6 But Peter said: Silver and gold I have not, but that which I have, the same I give to thee: In the name of \sc Jesus Christ\sc* of Nazareth arise, and walk. \ef - \fr 3.6 \fq That which I have. \ft ¹ This power of working miracles was in Peter, and Peter properly did give this man his health, though he received that force and virtue of God, and in & by him executed the same. Therefore he saith, \+it That which I have, I give to thee.\+it* And the Heretics are ridiculous that note here, \+it a miracle done by Christ by the hands of the Apostles\+it*, to make the simple believe that they had no more to do than a dead instrument in the workman's hand. \fq ¹ \ft Saints do miracles and the like, but by the power of God.\ef*
\v 7 And taking his right hand, he lifted him up, and forthwith his feet & soles were made strong.
\v 8 And springing he stood, & walked; & went in with them into the temple walking, and leaping, and praising God.
\v 9 And all the people saw him walking and praising God.
\v 10 And they knew him, that it was he which sat for alms at the Specious gate of the temple: and they were exceedingly astonished and aghast at that that had chanced to him.
\v 11 And as he held Peter and John, all the people ran to them unto the porch which is called Salomon's, wondering.
\v 12 \f 1 \fr 3.12 \ft The Epistle upon Wednesday in Easter week.\f* But Peter seeing them, made answer to the people: Ye men of Israel, why marvel you at this, or why look you upon us, as though by our power or holiness we have made this man to walk? \ef - \fr 3.12 \fq By our power. \ft When the Apostle's remit sins, or do any other miracles, they do it not by any human, proper, or natural power in themselves: but of supernatural force given them from above, to prove that the faith of Christ is true, and that he is God whom the Jews crucified, in whose name and faith they work, and not in their own.\ef*
\v 13 The God of Abraham, and the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob, the God of our fathers hath glorified his Son \sc Jesus\sc*, whom you indeed delivered & denied before the face of Pilate, he judging him to be released.
\v 14 But you denied the Holy and the Just One, \x - \xt Luc. 23, 18.\x* and asked a man-killer to be given unto you.
\v 15 But the Author of life you killed, whom God hath raised from the dead, of which we are witnesses.
\v 16 And in the \f 1 \fr 3.16 \ft This faith was not the faith of the lame man (for he looked only for alms) nor a special faith of the Apostle's own salvation: but the whole belief of Christian Religion.\f* faith of his name, this man whom you see and know, his name hath strengthened; & the faith which is by him, hath given this perfect health in the sight of all you.
\v 17 And now (brethren) I know that you did it through ignorance, as also your princes.
\v 18 But God who foreshewed by the mouth of all the Prophets that his \sc Christ\sc* should suffer, hath so fulfilled it.
\v 19 Be Penitent therefore & convert, that your sins may be put out.
\v 20 That, when the times shall come of refreshing by the sight of our Lord, and he shall send him that hath been preached unto you \sc Jesus Christ\sc*.
\v 21 \f 1 \fr 3.21 \ft \+it c ὃν δεῖ οὐρανὸν μὲν δέξασθαι\+it*\f* Whom \f 2 \fr 3.21 \ft Some Heretics foully corrupt this place, thus: \+it Who must be contained in heaven,\+it* of purpose (as they protest) to hold Christ in heaven, from the B. Sacrament. \+it Beza.\+it* As though his presence there, drew him out of heaven. Neither can they pretend the Greek, which is word for word as in the vulgar Latin, and as we translate.\f* Heaven truly must receive until the times of the restitution of all things, which God spake by the mouth of his holy Prophets from the beginning of the world.
\v 22 Moyses indeed said: \x - \xt Deu. 18, 15.\x* \it That a prophet shall the\it* Lord your God raise up to you of your brethren, as \it myself: him you shall hear according to all things whatsoever he shall speak to you.\it*
\v 23 \it And it shall be, every soul that shall not hear that Prophet, shall be destroyed out of the people.\it*
\v 24 And all the Prophets from Samuel and afterward that have spoken, told of these days.
\v 25 You are the children of the Prophets and of the Testament which God made to our fathers, saying to Abraham: \x - \xt Gen. 22, 18. 26, 4.\x* \it And in thy seed shall all the families of the earth be blessed.\it*
\v 26 To you first God raising up his Son, hath sent him blessing you: that every one should convert himself from his naughtiness.
\c 4
\cd The Rulers of the Jews oppose themselves and imprison Peter and John. 4. But yet thousands of the people are converted: 5. and to the Rulers also, Peter boldly avoucheth by the foresaid miracle, that JESUS is Christ, telling them of their heinous fault out of the Psalms, and that without him they cannot be saved. 13. They though confounded with the miracle, yet proceed in their obstinacy, forbidding them to speak any more of JESUS, adding also threats. 23. Whereupon the Church fleeth to prayer, wherein they comfort themselves with the omnipotency of God, and prediction of David, and ask for the gift of boldness and miracles against these threats. 31. And God sheweth miraculously that he hath heard their prayer. 32. The whole Church's unity and community of life. 36. Of Barnabas by name.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* when they were speaking to the people, the Priests and Magistrates of the temple & the Sadducees came upon them,
\v 2 being grieved that they taught the people, and shewed in \sc Jesus\sc* the resurrection from the dead:
\v 3 and they laid hands upon them, and put them into ward, until the morrow, for it was now evening.
\v 4 And \f 1 \fr 4.4 \ft Here again we see the proceeding and increase of the Church visibly.\f* many of them that had heard the word, believed: and the number of the men was made five thousand.
\v 5 And it came to pass on the morrow, that their princes, and Ancients, and Scribes were gathered into Jerusalem.
\v 6 And Annas the high Priest, and Caiphas and John, and Alexander, and as many as were of the priest's stock.
\v 7 And setting them in the midst, they asked: In what power or in what name have you done this?
\v 8 Then Peter replenished with the Holy Ghost, said to them: Ye princes of the people and Ancients:
\v 9 If we this day be examined for a good deed upon an impotent man, in what he hath been made whole,
\v 10 be it known to all you and to all the people of Israel, that in \f 1 \fr 4.10 \ft The name of JESUS. \+it See Annot. Philip. 2, 10.\+it*\f* the name of \sc Jesus Christ\sc* of Nazareth, whom you did crucify, whom God hath raised from the dead, in this same this man standeth before you whole.
\v 11 This is \x - \xt Psa. 117, 22.\x* \it the stone that was rejected of you the builders: which is made into the head of the corner:\it*
\v 12 and there is not salvation in any other. For neither is there any other name under Heaven given to men, wherein we must be saved.
\v 13 \f 1 \fr 4.13 \ft The Apostles' constancy, learning and wisdom after the coming of the Holy Ghost, being but idiots, that is, simple unlettered men and timorous before.\f* And seeing Peter's constancy and John's, understanding that they were men unlettered, and of the vulgar sort, they marveled; and they knew them that they had been with \sc Jesus\sc*:
\v 14 seeing the man also that had been cured, standing with them, they could say nothing to the contrary,
\v 15 But they commanded them to go aside forth out of the council: and they conferred together,
\v 16 saying: What shall we do to these men? for a notorious sign indeed hath been done by them, to all the inhabitants of Jerusalem: it is manifest, and we cannot deny it.
\v 17 But that it be no further spread abroad among the people, let us threaten them, that they speak no more in this name to any man.
\v 18 And calling them, they charged them that they should not speak at all, nor teach in the name of \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 19 But Peter and John answering, said to them: \f 1 \fr 4.19 \ft Their constancy and courage after their confirmation, being so weak before. And if any Magistrate command against God, that is to say, forbid Catholic Christian men to preach or serve God, this same must be their answer, though they be whipped and killed for their labour. \+it See c. 5. v. 29.\+it*\f* If it be just in the sight of God, to hear you rather than God, judge ye.
\v 20 For we cannot but speak the things which we have seen and heard.
\v 21 But they threatening, dismissed them: not finding how they might punish them, for the people, because all glorified that which had been done, in that which was chanced.
\v 22 For the man was more than forty years old in whom that sign of health had been wrought.
\v 23 And being dismissed they came to theirs, and shewed all that the chief priests and Ancients had said to them.
\v 24 Who having heard it, with one accord lifted up their voice to God, and said: Lord, thou that didst make Heaven & earth, the sea, and all things that are in them,
\v 25 who in the Holy Ghost by the mouth of our Father David thy servant hast said: \x - \xt Ps. 2, 1.\x* \it Why did the Gentiles rage, and the people meditate vain things:\it*
\v 26 \it the kings of the earth stand up, and the Princes assemble together against our Lord, and against his \+sc Christ\+sc*?\it*
\v 27 For there assembled indeed in this city against the holy child \sc Jesus\sc* whom thou hast anointed, Herod, and Pontius Pilate, with the Gentiles and the people of Israel;
\v 28 to do what \f 1 \fr 4.28 \ft Christ's death, as needful for man's redemption, was of God's determination: but as of the malice of the Jews, it was not his act otherwise than by permission.\f* thy hand and thy counsel decreed to be done.
\v 29 And now Lord look upon their threatenings, and give unto thy servants with all confidence to speak thy word,
\v 30 in that, that thou extend thy hand to cures & signs and wonders to be done by the name of thy holy son \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 31 And when they had prayed, the place was moved wherein they were gathered: and they were all replenished with the Holy Ghost, and they spake the word of God with confidence.
\v 32 And the multitude of believers had one heart and one soul: neither did any one say that ought was his own of those things which he possessed, \x - \xt Act. 2, 44.\x* but all things were common unto them.
\v 33 And with great power did the Apostles give testimony of the resurrection of \sc Jesus Christ\sc* our Lord: and great grace was in all them.
\v 34 For neither was there any one needy among them. For as many as were owners of lands or houses, \f 1 \fr 4.34 \ft Note the ardent charity and contempt of worldly things in the first Christians: who did not only give great alms, but\f* sold and brought the prices of those things which they sold,
\v 35 and laid it before the feet of the Apostles. And to every one was divided according as every one had need.
\v 36 And Joseph who was surnamed of the Apostles Barnabas (which is by interpretation, the son of consolation) a Levite, a Cyprian born,
\v 37 whereas he had a piece of land, sold it, and brought the price, and laid it before the feet of the Apostles. \ef - \fr 4.37 \fq Before the feet. \ft ¹ He, as the rest, did not only give his goods as in vulgar alms, but in all humble and reverent manner as things dedicated to God, he laid them down at the Apostles' holy feet, as S. Luke always expresseth, and gave them not into their hands. The Sunamite ² fell down and embraced Elisæus feet. Many that asked benefits of Christ (as the woman sick of the bloody flux) fell down at his feet ³, and Mary kissed his feet ⁴ ⁵. Such are signs of due reverence done both to Christ and to other sacred persons, either Prophets, Apostles, Popes, or others representing his person in earth. See in S. Hierom of Epiphanius Bishop in Cypres, how the people of Hierusalem \+it of all sorts flocked together unto him, offering their children\+it* (to take his blessing) \+it kissing his feet, plucking the hems of his garment, so that he could not move for the throng.\+it* Ep. 61. c. 4. cont. erro. Io. Hierosol. \fq ¹ \ft Reverence to holy persons. \fq ² \ft \+it 4. Reg. 4, 27.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Luc. 8, 47.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Kissing the feet of holy persons. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Lu. 7, 38.\+it*\ef*
\c 5
\cd Ananias and his wife Saphira, for their sacrilege, at Peter's word fall down dead, to the great terror of the rest. 12. By the Apostle's miracles, not only the number, but also their faith so increaseth, that they seek in the streets to the very shadow of Peter, the towns also about bringing their diseased to Hierusalem. 17. The Rulers again oppose themselves, but in vain. 19. For out of prison an Angel delivereth them, bidding them preach openly to all: 27. and in their Councel Peter is nothing afraid of their big words: 34. Yea Gamaliel being one of themselves casteth a doubt among them, lest the matter be of God, and therefore impossible to be dissolved. 40. Finally, the Apostles being scourged by them, count it an honour, and cease no day from preaching.
\p
\v 1 \sc But\sc* a certain man named Ananias, with Saphira his wife sold a piece of land,
\v 2 and defrauded of the price of the land, his wife being privy thereto: and bringing a certain portion, laid it at the feet of the Apostles. \ef - \fr 5.2 \fq Defrauded. \ft In that (saith S. Augustine ¹ ) he withdrew any part of that which he promised, he was guilty at once, both of sacrilege ², and of fraud. Of sacrilege, because he robbed God of that which was his by promise: of fraud, in that he withheld of the whole gift, a piece. Let now the Heretics come, and say it was for lying or hypocrisy only that this fact was condemned: because they be loath to have sacrilege counted any such sin, who have taught men not only to take away from God some piece of that, or all that themselves gave, but plainly to spoil & apply to themselves all that other men gave. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Aug. ser. 9. in append. de diversis tom. 10.\+it* \fq ² \ft Sacrilege.\ef*
\v 3 And Peter said: Ananias, why hath Satan tempted thy heart, that thou shouldest lie to the Holy Ghost, and defraud of the price of the land? \ef - \fr 5.3 \fq Peter said. \ft S. Peter (as you see here) without man's relation knew this fraud and the cogitations of Ananias, and as head of the College and of the whole Church against which this robbery was committed, executed this heavy sentence of Excommunication ¹ both against him, and his wife consenting to the Sacrilege. For it was excommunication by S. Augustine's judgement, \+it (li. 3 cont. ep. Parm. c. 1. to. 7.)\+it* and had this corporal miraculous death joined withal, as the Excommunication that S. Paul ² gave out against the incestuous and others, had the corporal vexation of Satan incident unto it. \fq ¹ \ft Excommunication joined with corporal pains. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Cor. 5.\+it*\ef*
\v 4 Remaining, did it not remain to thee: and being sold, was it not in thy power? Why hast thou put this thing in thy heart? Thou hast not lied to men, but to God. \ef - \fr 5.4 \fq In thy power. \ft ¹ \+it If it displeased God\+it* (saith S. Augustine) \+it to withdraw of the money which they had vowed to God how is he angry when chastity is vowed and is not performed? For to such may be said that which S. Peter said of the money: Thy virginity remaining did it not remain to thee, and before thou didst vow, was it not in thine own power? For, whosoever have vowed such things and have not payed them, let them not think to be condemned to corporal deaths, but to everlasting fire. August. Ser. 10. de diversis.\+it* And S. Gregory ² to the same purpose writeth thus, \+it Ananias had vowed money to God, which afterward overcome with devilish persuasion he withdrew: but with what death he was punished, thou knowest. If then he were worthy of that death, who took away the money that he had given to God, consider what great peril in God's judgement thou shalt be worthy of, which hast withdrawn, not money, but thyself from almighty God, to whom thou hadst vowed thyself under the habit or weed of a Monk.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Vow of Chastity, & the breach thereof. \fq ² \ft \+it Greg. li. 1. ep. 33.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 5.4 \fq Not to men, but. \ft To take from the Church or from the Governors thereof, things dedicated to their use and the service of God, or to lie unto God's Ministers, is so judged of before God, as if the lie were made, and the fraud done to the Holy Ghost himself, who is the Church's President and Protector.\ef*
\v 5 And Ananias hearing these words, fell down, and gave up the ghost. And there came great fear upon all that heard it.
\v 6 And young men rising up, removed him, & bearing him forth buried him.
\v 7 And it was the space as it were of three hours, and his wife, not knowing what was chanced, came in.
\v 8 And Peter answered her: Tell me woman, whether did you sell the land for so much? But she said: Yea, for so much.
\v 9 And Peter unto her: Why have you agreed together to tempt the Spirit of our Lord? Behold, their feet that have buried thy husband, at the door, and they shall bear thee forth.
\v 10 Forthwith she fell before his feet, and gave up the ghost. And the young men going in, found her dead: and carried her forth, & buried her by her husband.
\v 11 And there fell great \f 1 \fr 5.11 \ft Hereupon rose great reverence, awe, and fear of the vulgar Christians toward the holy Apostles; for an example to all Christian people how to behave themselves toward their Bishops and Priests.\f* fear in the whole Church, and upon all that heard these things.
\v 12 \f 1 \fr 5.12 \ft The Epistle upon Imber Wednesday in Whitsunweek. And within the octave, & in a votive of SS. Peter and Paul.\f* And by the hands of the Apostles were many signs and wonders done among the people. And they were all with one accord in Salomon's porch.
\v 13 But of the rest none durst join themselves unto them: but the people magnified them.
\v 14 And the multitude of men and women that believed in our Lord, was more increased:
\v 15 so that they did bring forth the sick into the streets, & laid them in beds and couches, that when Peter came, \f 1 \fr 5.15 \ft Peter's shadow.\f* his shadow at the least might overshadow any of them, and they all might be delivered from their infirmities. \ef - \fr 5.15 \fq His shadow. \ft ¹ Specially they sought to Peter the chief of all, who not only by touching, as the other, but by his very shadow cured all diseases. Whereupon S. Augustine saith, If then the shadow of his body could help, how much more now the fullness of power? And if then a certain little wind of him passing by did profit them that humbly asked, how much more the grace of him now being permanent & remaining? \+it Ser. 29. de Sanctis\+it*, speaking of the miracles done by the Saints now reigning in heaven. \fq ¹ \ft Peter's shadow & intercession.\ef*
\v 16 And there ran together unto Jerusalem the multitude also of the cities adjoining, bringing sick persons and such as were vexed of unclean spirits; who were all cured.
\v 17 And the high Priest rising up, and all that were with him, which is the heresy of the Sadducees, were replenished with zeal:
\v 18 laid hands upon the Apostles, and put them in the common prison.
\v 19 But an Angel of our Lord by night opening the gates of the prison, & leading them forth, \f 1 \fr 5.19 \ft An Angel leadeth them out of prison.\f* said:
\v 20 Go; and standing speak in the temple to the people all the words of this life.
\v 21 Who having heard this, early in the morning entered into the temple, and taught. And the high priest coming, and they that were with him, called together the Council & all the ancients of the children of Israel: and they sent to the prison that they might be brought.
\v 22 But when the ministers were come, and opening the prison, found them not; returning they told, saying:
\v 23 The prison truly we found shut with all diligence, and the keepers standing before the gates: but opening it, we found no man within.
\v 24 And as soon as the Magistrates of the temple and the chief Priests heard these words, they were in doubt of them, what would befall.
\v 25 And there came a certain man and told them: That the men, lo, which you did put in prison, are in the temple standing, and teaching the people.
\v 26 Then went the Magistrate with the ministers, & brought them without force, for they feared the people, lest they should be stoned.
\v 27 And when they had brought them, they set them in the Council. And the high Priest asked them,
\v 28 saying: \x - \xt Act. 4, 18.\x* Commanding we commanded you that you should not teach in this name: and behold you have filled Jerusalem with your doctrine, and you will bring upon us the blood of this man.
\v 29 But Peter answering and the Apostles, said: God must be obeyed, rather than men.
\v 30 The God of our Fathers hath raised up \sc Jesus\sc*, whom you did kill, hanging him upon a tree.
\v 31 This Prince and Saviour God hath exalted with his right hand, to give repentance to Israel, and remission of sins.
\v 32 And we are witnesses of these words, and the Holy Ghost, whom God hath given to all that obey him.
\v 33 When they had heard these things, it cut them to the heart, and they consulted to kill them.
\v 34 But one in the Council rising up, a Pharisee named Gamaliel, a Doctor of law honourable to all the people, commanded the men to be put forth a while.
\v 35 and he said to them: Ye men of Israel, take heed to yourselves touching these men what you mean to do.
\v 36 For before these days there rose \f 1 \fr 5.36 \ft \+it Theudas\+it*\f* Theodas, saying he was somebody, to whom consented a number of men about four hundred, who was slain: and all that believed him, were dispersed, and brought to nothing.
\v 37 After this fellow there rose Judas of Galilee in the days of the Enrolling, and drew away the people after him, and he perished: and as many as ever consented to him, were dispersed.
\v 38 And now therefore I say to you, depart from these men and let them alone: for if this counsel or work be of men, it will be dissolved:
\v 39 but if it be \f 1 \fr 5.39 \ft Time, and the evident success of Christ's Church and religion, prove it to be of God: no violence of the Jews, no persecution of the Heathen Princes, no endeavour of domestical Adversaries, heretics, Schismatics, or ill livers, prevailing against it; as on the other side, many attempts have been made by Arius, Macedonius, Nestorius, Luther, and the like, who thought themselves somebody: but after they had played their parts a while, their memory is buried, or liveth only in malediction & infamy, and their scholars come to naught. Therefore let no Cath. man be scandalized that this heresy holdeth up for a time. For the Arians & some others flourished much longer than these, and were better supported by Princes and learning, & yet had an end.\f* of God, you are not able to dissolve \f 2 \fr 5.39 \fq them \ft \+it it\+it*\f* them, lest perhaps you be found to resist God also. And they consented to him.
\v 40 And calling in the Apostles, after they had scourged them, they charged them that they should not speak in the name of \sc Jesus\sc*, and dismissed them.
\v 41 And they went from the sight of the Council rejoicing, because they were accounted worthy to suffer reproach for the name of \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 42 And every day they ceased not in the temple, and from house to house to teach and evangelize Christ \sc Jesus\sc*.
\c 6
\cd By occasion of a murmur in the Church (whose number now is so grown that it cannot be numbered) Seven of them being ordered by the Apostles in the Holy Order of Deacons: 8. one of them, Steven, worketh great miracles: and is by such as he confounded in disputation, falsely accused in the Councel, of blasphemy against the Temple and rites thereof.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* in those days the number of Disciples increasing, there arose a murmuring of the Greeks against the Hebrews, for that their widows were despised in the daily ministry. \ef - \fr 6.1 \fq Murmuring. \ft ¹ It cometh of human infirmity, that in every society of men (be it never so holy) there is some cause given or taken by the weak, of murmur and difference, which must ever be provided for and stayed in the beginning, lest it grow to further schism or sedition. And to all such defects, the more the Church increaseth in number and diversity of men and Provinces, the more it is subject. In all which things the spiritual Magistrates, by the Apostles' example and authority, must take order, as time and occasion shall require. \fq ¹ \ft Murmuring & emulation.\ef*
\v 2 And the Twelve calling together the multitude of the Disciples, said: it is not reason, that we leave the word of God, and serve tables.
\v 3 Consider therefore brethren, seven men of you of good testimony, full of the Holy Ghost and wisdom, whom we may appoint over this business. \ef - \fr 6.3 \fq Seven men. \ft ¹ We may not think that these Seven (here made Deacons) were only chosen to serve profane tables or dispose of the Church's mere temporals, though by that occasion only they may seem to some now elected, no express mention being made of any other function. For, diverse circumstances of this same place give evidence, and so doth all antiquity, that their Office stood not principally about profane things, but about the holy Altar. The persons to be elected, must be full of the Holy Ghost and wisdom, they must after public prayer be ordered and consecrated by the Apostles' imposition of hands, as Bishops and Priests were afterward ordered. \+it ep. ad Tim.\+it* ² Where S. Paul also requireth in a manner the same conditions in them as in Bishops. All which would not have been prescribed for any secular stewardship. Yea straight upon their Ordering here (no doubt by commission of the Apostles, which they had not before their election) they preached, baptized, disputed, and as it may appear by the words spoken of S. Steven ³, that he was full of grace and fortitude, they received great increase of grace by their Deaconship. \fq ¹ \ft The 7. Deacons \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Tim. 3. & 4.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Act. 6, 8.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 6.3 \ft ¹ But S. Ignatius \+it ep. 2 ad Tral.\+it* can best witness of their Office and the Apostles' manner and meaning in such things, who writeth thus: \+it It behoveth also to please by all means the Deacons, which are for the ministry of \+sc Jesus Christ\+sc*. For they are not servitors of meat and drink, but ministers of the Church of God. For what are Deacons but imitators or followers of Christ, ministering to the Bishops, as Christ to his Father, & working unto him a clean and immaculate work, even as S. Steven to S. James &c.\+it* S. Polycarpe hath the like in his epistle ad Philippenses. And S. Denys ² writeth that their Office was about the Altar, and putting the holy bread and chalice upon the same. S. Clement also \+it (Apost. Const. li. 2. c. 61.)\+it* that their Office among other things, is to assist the Bishops, and read the Gospel in the Service &c. S. Cyprian in diverse places \+it (ep. 65. & ep. 49. ad Cornel.)\+it* calleth Deacons, the Church's and the Apostles' Ministers, and their Office, \+it administrationem sacram\+it*, an holy administration. S. Hierom affirmeth, \+it in caput 7 Micheæ.\+it* and \+it in episto. 85. ad Evagrium tom. 2\+it*, where he checketh some of them for preferring themselves before Priests, and putteth them in remembrance of their first calling, that they be as the Levites were in respect of the Priests of the old Law. Finally by S. Ambrose \+it li. 1. Offic. c. 41.\+it* and Prudentius \+it in Hymno de S. Laurent.\+it* speaking of S. Laurence the Deacon, we may see their Office was most holy. See S. Augustine also of the dignity of Deacons \+it ep. 148. ad Valerium. Conc. Carthag. 4. can. 37. 38. 39. 41.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The office of Deacons. \fq ² \ft \+it Li. Eccl. Hier. c. 3. part. 2.\+it*\ef*
\v 4 But we will be instant in prayer and the ministry of the word.
\v 5 \f 1 \fr 6.5 \ft The election of the 7 first Deacons.\f* And the saying was liked before all the multitude. And they chose Steven a man full of faith and of the Holy Ghost, and Philippe, and Prochorus, and Nicanor, and Timon, and Parmenas, and Nicolas a \f 2 \fr 6.5 \ft \+it c προσήλυτον.\+it*\f* stranger of Antioch:
\v 6 These they did set in the presence of the Apostles: and praying \f 1 \fr 6.6 \ft Order of Deacons given by imposition of hands. See Annot. Act. 13, 3.\f* they imposed hands upon them.
\v 7 And the word of God increased, and the number of the Disciples was multiplied in Jerusalem exceedingly: a great multitude also of the \f 1 \fr 6.7 \ft Now also the Priests and they of greater knowledge and estimation began to believe.\f* Priests obeyed the faith.
\v 8 \f 1 \fr 6.8 \ft b The Epistle upon S. Steven's day in Christmas. And his Invention Aug. 3.\f* And Steven full of grace and fortitude did great wonders & signs among the people.
\v 9 And there arose certain of that which is called the Synagogue of the Libertines, and of the Cyrenians, and of the Alexandrians, and of them that were of Cilicia and Asia, disputing with Steven:
\v 10 and they could not resist the wisdom and the Spirit that spake.
\v 11 Then they suborned men, to say they had heard him speak words of blasphemy against Moyses and God.
\v 12 They therefore stirred up the people, and the Ancients, and the Scribes: and running together they took him, and brought him into the Council,
\v 13 and they set false witnesses that said: This man ceaseth not to speak words against the holy place & the Law.
\v 14 For we have heard him say, that this same \sc Jesus\sc* of Nazareth shall destroy this place, and shall change the traditions, which Moyses delivered unto us.
\v 15 And all that sat in the Council beholding him, saw his face as it were \f 1 \fr 6.15 \ft Such is the face of all constant & cheerful Martyrs, to their persecutors and judges.\f* the face of an Angel.
\c 7
\cd Steven being permitted to answer, beginning at Abraham, sheweth that God was with their fathers both in other places, and also long before the Temple. 48. and that after it was built, it could not be (as they grossly imagined) a house for God to dwell in. 51. Then he inveigheth against their stiffneckedness, and telleth them boldly of their treacherous murdering of Christ, as their fathers had done his Prophets afore him. 54. Whereat they being wood, he seeth heaven open, and JESUS there in his Divine Majesty. 57. Whereat they become more mad, so that they stone him to death (Saul consenting) \it he commending his soul to \+sc Jesus\+sc*, and humbly praying for them.\it*
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* the chief priest said: Are these things so?
\v 2 Who said: Ye men, brethren and fathers, hear. The God of glory appeared to our father Abraham when he was in Mesopotamia, before that he abode in Charan,
\v 3 & said to him: \x - \xt Gen. 12, 1.\x* \it Go forth out of thy country, and out of thy kindred, & come into a land that I shall shew thee.\it*
\v 4 Then went he forth out of the land of the Chaldees, and dwelt in Charan. And from thence, after his father was dead, he translated him into this land wherein you do now dwell.
\v 5 And he gave him no inheritance in it, no not the pace of a foot: and he promised to give it him in possession, and to his seed after him, when as he had no child.
\v 6 And God spake to him: \x - \xt Gen. 15, 13.\x* \it That his seed shall be a sojourner in a strange country, and they shall subdue them to servitude, and shall evil entreat them four hundred years.\it*
\v 7 \it and the nation which they shall serve, will I judge,\it* said God. \it And after these things they shall go forth and \it* \f 1 \fr 7.7 \ft \+it c λατρεύσουσι.\+it*\f* \it shall serve me in this place.\it*
\v 8 And he \x - \xt Gen. 17.\x* gave him the testament of circumcision, and so he \x - \xt Gen. 21.\x* begat Isaac, and circumcised him the eighth day: and \x - \xt Gen. 25.\x* Isaac, Jacob: and \x - \xt Gen. 29, 30. 35.\x* Jacob, the twelve Patriarchs.
\v 9 And the Patriarchs through emulation, \x - \xt Gen. 37.\x* sold Joseph into Ægypt. And God was with him:
\v 10 and delivered him out of all his tribulations. And he \x - \xt Gen. 41.\x* gave him grace and wisdom in the sight of Pharao the king of Ægypt, and he appointed him Governor over Ægypt and over all his house.
\v 11 And there came famine upon all Ægypt and Chanaan, and great tribulation: and our fathers found no victuals.
\v 12 But when \x - \xt Gen. 42.\x* Jacob had heard that there was corn in Ægypt; he sent our fathers first:
\v 13 and at the \x - \xt Gen. 45.\x* second time Joseph was known of his brethren, and his kindred was made known unto Pharao.
\v 14 And Joseph sending, called thither Jacob his father and all his kindred in seventy five souls.
\v 15 And \x - \xt Gen. 46.\x* Jacob descended into Ægypt: and \x - \xt Gen. 49. Gen. 50.\x* he died, and our fathers.
\v 16 And they were \x - \xt Gen. 23.\x* translated into Sichem, and were \x - \xt Ios. 24.\x* laid in the sepulchre that Abraham \f 3 \fr 7.16 \ft Translation of Saints' bodies agreeable to nature & Scripture. And the desire to be buried in one place more than another (which the holy Patriarchs also had \+it Gen. 49, 29. 50, 24. Hebr. 11, 22.\+it* ) hath sometime great causes. \+it Aug. de Cur. pro mort. c. 1. & ult.\+it*\f* bought for a price of silver of the sons of Hemor the son of Sichem.
\v 17 And when the time drew near of the promise which God had promised to Abraham, the people \x - \xt Exo. 1, 7.\x* increased and was multiplied in Ægypt,
\v 18 until another king arose in Ægypt, that knew not Joseph.
\v 19 This same circumventing our stock, afflicted our fathers, that they should expose their children, to the end they might not be kept alive.
\v 20 The same time was \x - \xt Exo. 2, 2.\x* Moyses born, and he was acceptable to God; who was nourished three months in his father's house.
\v 21 And when he was exposed, Pharao's daughter took him up, and nourished him for her own son.
\v 22 And Moyses was instructed in all the wisdom of the Ægyptians: and he was mighty in his words and works.
\v 23 And \x - \xt Exo. 2, 11.\x* when he was fully of the age of forty years, it came to his mind to visit his brethren the children of Israel.
\v 24 And when he had seen one suffer wrong, he defended him: and striking the Ægyptian, he revenged his quarrel that sustained the wrong.
\v 25 And he thought that his brethren did understand that God by his hand would save them: but they understood it not.
\v 26 And the day following \x - \xt Exo. 2, 13.\x* he appeared to them being at strife: and he reconciled them unto peace, saying: Men, ye are brethren, wherefore hurt you one another?
\v 27 But he that did the injury to his neighbour, repelled him, saying: \it Who hath appointed thee prince and judge over us:\it*
\v 28 \it What, wilt thou kill me, as thou didst yesterday kill the Ægyptian?\it*
\v 29 And Moyses fled upon this word: and he became a sojourner in the land of Madian, where he begat two sons.
\v 30 And after forty years were expired, there \x - \xt Exo. 3, 2.\x* appeared to him in the desert of mount Sina an Angel in the fire of the flame of a bush.
\v 31 And Moyses seeing it, marveled at the vision. And as he went near to view it, the voice of our Lord was made to him:
\v 32 \it I am the God of thy fathers; the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob.\it* And Moyses being made to tremble, durst not view it.
\v 33 And our Lord said to him: \it Loose off the shoe of thy feet, for the place wherein thou standest, is holy ground.\it* \ef - \fr 7.33 \fq Holy ground. \ft ¹ If that apparition of God himself or an Angel, could make the place and ground holy, and to be used of Moyses with all signs of reverence and fear: ² how much more the corporal birth, abode, and wonders of the Son of God in Jewry, and his personal presence in the B. Sacrament, may make that country and all Christian Churches & altars holy? And it is the greatest blindness that can be, to think it superstition to reverence any things or places in respect of God's presence or wondrous operation in the same. \+it See S. Hierom. ep. 17. 18. 27. of the holy land.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The holy land. \fq ² \ft Holy places.\ef*
\v 34 \it Seeing I have seen the affliction of my people which is in Ægypt, and I have heard their groaning and am descended to deliver them. And now come, and I will send thee into Ægypt.\it*
\v 35 This Moyses, whom they denied, saying: \it Who hath appointed thee prince and Captain?\it* him God sent prince & \f 1 \fr 7.35 \ft Christ is our Redeemer, and yet Moyses is here called redeemer. So Christ is our Mediator and Advocate, and yet we may have Saints our inferior mediators and advocates also. \+it See Annot. 1. Io. 2, 1.\+it*\f* redeemer with the hand of the Angel that appeared to him in the bush.
\v 36 He \x - \xt Exo. 7. 8. 9. 10. 11. 12, 37.\x* brought them forth doing wonders and signs in the land of Ægypt, and in the red sea, and in the \x - \xt Exo. 16.\x* desert forty years.
\v 37 This is that Moyses which said to the children of Israel: \x - \xt Deu. 18.\x* \it A Prophet will God raise up to you of your own brethren as myself: him you shall hear.\it*
\v 38 This is he that \x - \xt Exo. 19, 3. 19.\x* was in the assembly in the wilderness, with the Angel that spake to him in Mount Sina, and with our fathers: who received the words of life to give unto us.
\v 39 To whom our fathers would not be obedient: but they repelled him, and in their hearts turned away into Ægypt,
\v 40 saying to Aaron: \x - \xt Exo. 32, 1.\x* \it Make us gods that may go before us. For this Moyses that brought us out of the land of Ægypt, we know not what is befallen to him.\it*
\v 41 And they made a calf in those days, and offered sacrifice to the idol, and rejoiced in the works of their own hands.
\v 42 And God turned, and \x - \xt Amos. 5, 25.\x* delivered them up \f 2 \fr 7.42 \ft \+it c λατρεύειν.\+it*\f* to serve the host of Heaven, as it is written in the book of the Prophets: \f 3 \fr 7.42 \ft For a just punishment of their former offences God gave them up to work what wickedness they would themselves, as it is said of the Gentiles \+it Rom. 1.\+it*\f* \it Did you offer victims and hosts unto me forty years in the desert, O house of Israel?\it*
\v 43 \it And you took unto you the tabernacle of Moloch, and the star of your god Rempham, figures which you made, to adore them. And I will translate you beyond Babylon.\it*
\v 44 The tabernacle of testimony was among our fathers in the desert, as God ordained speaking to Moyses, \x - \xt Exo. 25, 40.\x* \it that he should make it according to the form which he had seen.\it*
\v 45 Which our fathers \x - \xt Ios. 3, 14.\x* with \f 2 \fr 7.45 \ft c This is Josue, so called in Greek in type of our Saviour.\f* Jesus receiving, brought it in also into the possession of the Gentiles. Which God expelled from the face of our fathers, till in the days of David,
\v 46 who found grace before God, and \x - \xt Ps. 131, 5.\x* desired that he might find a tabernacle for the God of Jacob.
\v 47 And \x - \xt 1. Par. 17.\x* Salomon built him a house.
\v 48 But the Highest dwelleth not in houses \x - \xt Act. 17. 25.\x* made by hand, as the Prophet saith: \ef - \fr 7.48 \fq Not in houses. \ft The vulgar Heretics allege this place against the corporal being of Christ in the B. Sacrament & in Churches: by which reason they might have driven him out of all houses, Churches, and corporal places, when he was visible in earth. But it is meant of the Divinity only, & spoken to correct the carnal Jews: ¹ who thought God either so to be contained, compassed, and limited to their Temple, that he could be no where else, or at least that he would not hear or receive men's prayers and sacrifices in the Churches of the Gentiles, or elsewhere, out of the said Temple. And so as it maketh nothing for the Sacramentaries, no more doth it serve for such as esteem Churches and places of public prayer no more convenient nor more holy than any other profane houses or chambers. For though his person or virtue be not limited to any place, yet it pleaseth him condescending to our necessity and profit, to work his wonders and to be worshipped of us in holy places rather than profane. \fq ¹ \ft God is not contained in place, yet he will be worshipped in one place more than in another.\ef*
\v 49 \x - \xt Esa. 66, 1.\x* \it Heaven is my seat: and the earth the foot-stool of my feet. What house will you build me, saith our Lord, or what place is there of my resting?\it*
\v 50 \it Hath not my hand made all these things?\it*
\v 51 You stiff-necked and of uncircumcised hearts and ears, you always resist the Holy Ghost: as your fathers, yourselves also.
\v 52 Which of the Prophets did not your fathers persecute? And they slew them that foretold of the coming of the Just one, of whom now
\v 53 you have been betrayers and murderers: who received the Law by the disposition of Angels, and have not kept it.
\v 54 And hearing these things they were cut in their hearts, and they gnashed with their teeth at him.
\v 55 But he being full of the Holy Ghost, looking steadfastly unto Heaven, \f 1 \fr 7.55 \ft c The comfort of all Martyrs.\f* saw the glory of God, and \sc Jesus\sc* standing on the right hand of God. And he said: Behold I see the Heavens opened, and the Son of man standing on the right hand of God.
\v 56 And they crying out with a loud voice, stopped their ears, and with one accord ran violently upon him.
\v 57 And casting him forth without the city they stoned him: and the witnesses laid off their garments \x - \xt Act. 22, 20.\x* beside the feet of a young man that was called Saul. \ef - \fr 7.57 \fq They stoned him. \ft Read a marvelous narration in S. Augustine of one stone, that hitting the Martyr on the elbow, rebounded back to a faithful man that stood near. ¹ Who keeping and carrying it with him, was by revelation warned to leave it at Ancona in Italy: whereupon a Church or Memory of S. Steven was there erected, and many miracles done after the said Martyr's body was found out, and not before. \+it Aug. to. 10. ser. 38. de diversis in edit. Paris.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Relics.\ef*
\v 58 And they stoned Steven invocating, and saying: Lord \sc Jesus\sc*, receive my spirit.
\v 59 And falling on his knees, he cried with a loud voice, saying: \f 1 \fr 7.59 \ft Eusebius Emissenus saith, when he prayeth for his persecutors, he promiseth to his worshippers his manifest intercession & suffrages. in ho. S. Steph. & S. Augustine, Si Stephanus sic non orasset, Ecclesia Paulum non haberet. Serm. 1. de S. Stephano.\f* Lord, lay not this sin unto them. And when he had said this, he fell asleep. And Saul was consenting to his death.
\c 8
\cd So far is persecution from prevailing against the Church, that by it the Church groweth from Hierusalem into all Jewry and Samaria. 5. The second of the Deacons, Philip. converteth with his miracles the city itself of Samaria, and baptizeth them, even Simon Magus also himself among the rest, 14. But the Apostles Peter and John are the Ministers to give them the Holy Ghost. 18. Which ministry Simon Magus would buy of them. 26. The same Philip being sent of an Angel to a great man of Æthiopia, who came a Pilgrimage to Hierusalem, first catechizeth him: 36. and then (he professing his faith and desiring Baptism) doth also baptize him. \f - \ft The 3. part. THE propagation of the Church from Hierusalem into all Jewry and to Samaria.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* the same day there was made a great persecution in the Church, which was at Jerusalem, and all were dispersed through the countries of Jewry and Samaria, saving the Apostles.
\v 2 And devout men \f 1 \fr 8.2 \ft \+it c curaverunt συνεκόμισαν\+it*\f* took order for Steven's funeral, and made great mourning upon him. \ef - \fr 8.2 \fq Devout men. \ft ¹ As here great devotion was used in burying his body, so afterward at the Invention & Translation thereof. And the miracles wrought by the same, and at every little memory of the same, were infinite: as S. Augustine witnesseth. \+it li. 22. de Civit. Dei c. 8. & Sermon. de S. Steph. to. 10.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft S. Steven's relics.\ef*
\v 3 But Saul \x - \xt Act. 22, 4.\x* wasted the Church: entering in from house to house, and drawing men and women, delivered them into prison.
\v 4 They therefore that were dispersed, passed through, \f 1 \fr 8.4 \ft This persecution wrought much good, being an occasion that the dispersed preached Christ in diverse Countries where they came.\f* evangelizing the word.
\v 5 \f 1 \fr 8.5 \ft The Epistle upon Thursday in Whitsunweek.\f* And Philippe descending into the city of Samaria, preached \sc Christ\sc* unto them.
\v 6 and the multitudes were attent to those things which were said of Philippe, with one accord hearing, and seeing the signs that he did.
\v 7 For many of them that had unclean spirits, crying with a loud voice, went out. And many sick of the palsy and lame were cured.
\v 8 There was made therefore great joy in that city.
\v 9 And a certain man named Simon, who before had been in that city a Magician, seducing the nation of Samaria, saying himself to be some great one:
\v 10 unto whom all harkened from the least to the greatest, saying: This man is the power of God, that is called great.
\v 11 And they were attent upon him, because a long time he had bewitched them with his magical practices.
\v 12 But when they had believed Philippe evangelizing of the Kingdom of God, and of the name of \sc Jesus Christ\sc*, they were baptized, men and women.
\v 13 Then Simon also himself believed: & being baptized, he cleaved to Philippe. Seeing also signs and very great miracles to be done, he was astonished with admiration.
\v 14 \f 1 \fr 8.14 \ft The Epistle upon Tuesday in Whitsunweek. And in a votive of the Holy Ghost.\f* And when the Apostles who were in Jerusalem, had heard that Samaria had received the word of God: they sent unto them \f 2 \fr 8.14 \ft \+it Sæpe sibi socium Petrus facit esse Ioannem: Ecclesiæ quia virgo placet.\+it* Peter often maketh John his companion, because the Church loveth a virgin. Arator apud Bedam in Act.\f* Peter and John. \ef - \fr 8.14 \fq Sent Peter. \ft Some Protestants use this place to prove S. Peter not to be head of the Apostles, because he and S. John were sent by the Twelve, by which reason they might as well conclude that he was not equal to the rest. For commonly the Master sendeth the man, and the Superior the inferior, when the word of sending is exactly used. ¹ But it is not always so taken in the Scriptures, for then could not the Son be sent by the Father, nor the Holy Ghost from the Father and the Son: nor otherwise in common use of the world, seeing the inferior or equal may intreat his friend or Superior to do his business for him. And specially a body Politic or a Corporation may by election or otherwise choose their Head and send him. So may the Citizens send their Mayor to the Prince or Parliament, though he be the head of the city, because he may be more fit to do their business. Also the Superior or equal may be sent by his own consent or desire. Lastly, the College of the Apostles comprising Peter with the rest (as every such Body implieth both the head and the members) was greater than Peter their head alone. As the Prince and Parliament is greater than the Prince alone. And so Peter might be sent as by authority of the whole College, notwithstanding he were the head of the same. \fq ¹ \ft That Peter was sent, is no reason against his Primacy.\ef*
\v 15 Who when they were come, prayed for them, that they might receive the Holy Ghost.
\v 16 For he was not yet come upon any of them, but they were only baptized in the name of our Lord \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 17 Then did they impose their hands upon them, & they received the Holy Ghost. \ef - \fr 8.17 \fq Did they impose. \ft \+it If this Philip had been an Apostle\+it* (saith S. Bede) \+it he might have imposed his hands, that they might have received the Holy Ghost, but this none can do saving Bishops.\+it* ¹ \+it For though Priests may baptize, and anoint the Baptized also with Chrism consecrated by a Bishop: yet he cannot sign his forehead with the same holy oil, because that belongeth only to Bishops, when they give the Holy Ghost to the Baptized.\+it* So saith he touching the Sacrament of Confirmation in 8. Act. This imposition therefore of hands together with the prayers here specified (which no doubt were the very same that the Church yet useth to that purpose) was the ministration of the Sacrament of Confirmation. Whereof S. Cyprian ² saith thus, They that in Samaria were baptized of Philip, because they had lawful and Ecclesiastical Baptism, ought not to be baptized any more: but only that which wanted, was done by Peter and John, to wit, that by prayer made for them and imposition of hands, the Holy Ghost might be poured upon them. Which now also is done with us, that they which in the Church are baptized, be by the Ruler's of the Church offered, and by our prayer and imposition of hand receive the Holy Ghost, and be signed with our Lord's seal. So S. Cyprian. But the Heretics object that yet here is no mention of oil. To whom we say, that many things were done and said in the administration of this and other Sacrament, and all instituted by Christ himself and delivered to the Church by the Apostles, which are not particularly written by the Evangelists or any other in the Scripture, among which this is evident by all antiquity and most general practice of the Church, to be one. \fq ¹ \ft The Sacrament of Confirmation, ministered by Bishops only. And that with solemn prayer and imposition of hands. \fq ² \ft \+it ep. 73. nu. 3. ad Iubaianum.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 8.17 \ft ¹ S. Denys ² saith, The Priests did present the baptized to the Bishop, that he might sign them \+it divino & deifico unguento\+it*, with the divine and deifical ointment. And again, \+it Adventum S. Spiritus consummans inunctio largitur\+it*, the inunction consummating giveth the coming of the Holy Ghost. Tertullian \+it de resur. carn. nu. 7. & li. 1. adv. Marcio.\+it* speaketh of this Confirmation by Chrism thus: \+it The flesh is anointed, that the soul may be consecrated: the flesh is signed, that the soul may be fenced: the flesh by imposition of hand is shadowed, that the soul by the Spirit may be illuminated.\+it* S. Cyprian likewise, \+it ep. 70. nu. 2. He must also be anointed, that is baptized, with the oil sanctified on the Altar.\+it* And ep. 72. (see also ep. 73. nu. 3.) he expressly calleth it a Sacrament, joining it with Baptism, as Melchiades doth \+it (ep. ad omnes Hispaniæ Episcopos nu. 2. to. 1. Conc.)\+it* Shewing the difference betwixt it an Baptism. S. Augustine also, \+it cont. lit. Petil li. 2. c. 104. The Sacrament of Chrism in the kind of visible seals is sacred and holy, even as Baptism itself.\+it* We omit S. Cyril \+it mystag. 3.\+it* S. Ambrose \+it li. 3. de Sacram. c. 2. & de ijs qui mysterijs initiantur c. 7.\+it* S. Leo ep. 88, the ancient Councels also of Laodicea, can. 48. Carthage 3. can. 39. and Aravsicanum 4. can. 1. and others. And S. Clement \+it (Apost. Const. li. 7, c. 44.) reporteth certain constitutions of the Apostles touching the same.\+it* ³ S. Denys referreth the manner of consecration of the same Chrism to the Apostles' instruction. S. Basil \+it li. de Sp. Sancto c. 27.\+it* calleth it a tradition of the Apostles. And the most ancient Martyr S. Fabian \+it ep. 2. ad omnes Orientales Episcopos in initio, to. 1. Conc.\+it* saith plainly that Christ himself did so instruct the Apostles at the time of the institution of the B. Sacrament of the Altar. And so doth the Author of the book \+it de unctione Chrismatis apud D. Cyprianum nu. 1.\+it* telling the excellent effects and graces of this Sacrament, and why this kind of oil and balsam was taken of the old Law, & used in the Sacraments of the new Testament. Which thing the Heretics can with less cause object against the Church, seeing they confess ⁴ that Christ and his Apostles took the ceremony of imposition of hands in this and other Sacraments, from the Jews' manner of consecrating their hosts deputed to sacrifice. ⁵ To conclude, never none denied or contemned this Sacrament of Confirmation and holy Chrism, but known Heretics. S. Cornelius that B. Martyr so much praised of S. Cyprian, \+it ep. ad Fabium apud Euseb. li. 6. c. 35.\+it* affirmeth, that Novatus fell to Heresy, for that he had not received the Holy Ghost by the consignation of a Bishop. Whom all the Novatians did follow, never using that holy Chrism, as Theodorete writeth, \+it li. 3. Fabul. Hær.\+it* And Optatus \+it li. 2. cont. Parm.\+it* writeth that it was the special barbarous sacrilege of the Donatists, to conculcate the holy oil. But all this is nothing to the savage disorder of Calvinists in this point. \fq ¹ \ft Chrism in Confirmation. \fq ² \ft \+it Ec. Hier. c. 2. & 4.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it * Ec. Hier. c. 4.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it * Beza in Act. c. 6. v. 6.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft Old heresies against confirmation and Chrism.\ef* \ef - \fr 8.17 \fq And they received the Holy Ghost. \ft The Protestants charge the Catholics, ¹ that by approving and commending so much the Sacrament of Confirmation, and by attributing to it specially the gift of the Holy Ghost, they diminish the force of Baptism, challenging also boldly the ancient Fathers for the same. As though any Catholic or Doctor ever said more than the express words of Scripture here and elsewhere plainly give them warrant for. If they diminish the virtue of Baptism, then did Christ so, appointing his Apostles and all the Faithful even after their Baptism to expect the Holy Ghost & virtue from above; then did the Apostles injury to Baptism, in that they imposed hands on the baptized, and gave them the Holy Ghost. ² And this is the Heretics' blindness in this case, that they cannot, or will not see that the Holy Ghost is given in Baptism to remission of sins, life, and sanctification: & in Confirmation, for force, strength, and corroboration to fight against all our spiritual enemies, and to stand constantly in confession of our faith, even to death, in times of persecution either of the Heathen or of Heretics, with great increase of grace. And let the good Reader note here our Adversaries' great perversity and corruption of the plain sense of the Scriptures in this point: ³ some of them affirming the Holy Ghost here to be no other but the gift of wisdom in the Apostles and a few more to the government of the Church, when it is plain that not only the Governers but all that were baptized, received this grace, both men and women. Some, that it was no internal grace, but only the gift of diverse languages: Which is very false, the gift of Tongues being but a sequel and an accident to the grace, and an external token of the inward gift of the Holy Ghost, and our Saviour calleth it virtue from above. Some say, that whatsoever it was, it was but a miraculous thing, and dured no longer than the gift of the Tongues joined thereunto: by which evasion they deny also the Sacrament of Extreme Unction, and the force of Excommunication, because the corporal punishments which were annexed often times in the Primitive Church unto it, ceaseth, and so may they take away (as they mean to do) all Christ's faith or religion, because it hath not the like operation of miracles as in the beginning. But S. Augustine ⁴ toucheth this point fully. \+it Is there any man\+it* (saith he) \+it of so perverse an heart, to deny these Children on whom we now imposed hands, to have received the Holy Ghost, because they speak not with Tongues? &c.\+it* Lastly, some of them make no more of Confirmation or the Apostles' fact, but as of a doctrine, instruction, or exhortation to continue in the faith received. Whereupon they have turned this holy Sacrament ⁵ into a Catechism. ⁶ There are also that put the baptized coming to years of discretion, to their own choice whether they will continue Christians or no. To such devilish and diverse inventions they fall, that will not obey God's Church nor the express Scriptures, which tell us of prayer, of imposition of hands, of the Holy Ghost, of grace and virtue from above, and not of instruction, which might and may be done as well before Baptism, & by others, as by Apostles and Bishops, to whom only this Holy function pertaineth, in so much that in our Country it is called \+it Bishoping\+it* ⁷. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Kemnit. in exam. conc. Trid. de Confir.\+it* \fq ² \ft The effects of Baptism and Confirmation differ. \fq ³ \ft Heretical shifts and evasions against manifest Scriptures, and against this Sacrament of Confirmation. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Tractat. 6. in ep. Io.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it * See Conc. Trid. Sess. 7. can. 1. de Confirmat.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft \+it * Conc. Trid. sess 7. can. 14. de Bapt.\+it* \fq ⁷ \ft Bishoping.\ef*
\v 18 And when Simon had seen that by the imposition of the hand of the Apostles, the Holy Ghost was given, he offered them money, \ef - \fr 8.18 \fq Offered money. \ft This wicked sorcerer Simon is noted by S. Irenæus li. 1. c. 20. and others, to have been the first Heretic, & father of all Heretics to come, in the Church of God. He taught, only faith in him, without good life and works, to be enough to salvation. He gave the onset to purchase with his money a spiritual function, that is to be made a Bishop. For, to have power to give the Holy Ghost by imposition of hands, is to be a Bishop: ¹ as to buy the power to remit sins or to consecrate Christ's body, is to buy to be a Priest, or to buy Priesthood: and to buy the authority to minister Sacraments, to preach or to have cure of souls, is to buy a benefice. And likewise in all other spiritual things, whereof either to make sale or purchase for money or money worth, is a great horrible sin called Simony: and in such as think it lawful (as here Simon judged it) it is named \+it Simoniacal Heresy\+it*, of this detestable man who first attempted to buy a spiritual function or office. \+it D. Greg. apud Ioan. Diac. in vit. li. 3. c. 2. 3. 4. 5.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Simony.\ef*
\v 19 saying: Give me also this power, that on whomsoever I impose my hands, he may receive the Holy Ghost.
\v 20 But Peter said to him: Thy money be with thee unto perdition: because thou hast thought that the gift of God is purchased with money.
\v 21 Thou hast no part, nor lot in this word. For thy heart is not right before God.
\v 22 \f 1 \fr 8.22 \ft \+it c μετανόησον ἀπὸ See Apocal. 9, 21.\+it*\f* Do penance therefore from this thy wickedness: and pray to God, if perhaps this cogitation of thy heart may be remitted thee. \ef - \fr 8.22 \fq Do penance. \ft ¹ S. Augustine (ep. 108.) understanding this of the penance done in the Primitive Church for heinous offences, doth teach us to translate this and the like places (2. Cor. 12, 21. Apoc. 9, 21) as we do, and as it is in the vulgar Latin, and consequently that the Greek \+it μετανοεῖν\+it* doth signify so much. Yea when he addeth, that very good men do daily penance for venial sins by fasting, prayer, and alms, he warranteth this phrase and translation throughout the new Testament, specially himself also reading so as it is in the vulgar Latin, and as we translate. \fq ¹ \ft Penance.\ef* \ef - \fr 8.22 \fq If perhaps. \ft You may see, great penance is here required for remission of sin, & that men must stand in fear and dread lest they be not worthy to be heard or to obtain mercy. Whereby all men that buy or sell any spiritual functions, dignities, offices, or livings, may specially be warned that the sin is exceeding great.\ef*
\v 23 For I see thou art in the gall of bitterness and the obligation of iniquity.
\v 24 And Simon answering said: Pray you for me to our Lord, that nothing come upon me of these things which you have said. \ef - \fr 8.24 \fq Pray you for me. \ft ¹ As this Sorcerer had more knowledge of the true religion than the Protestants have, who see not that the Apostles and Bishops can give the Holy Ghost in this Sacrament or other, which he plainly perceived and confessed, so surely he was more religious than they, that being so sharply checked by the Apostle, yet blasphemed not as they do when they be blamed by the Governers of the Church, but desired the Apostles to pray for him. \fq ¹ \ft Simon Magus more religious than the Protestants.\ef*
\v 25 And they indeed having testified and spoken the word of our Lord, returned to Jerusalem, and evangelized to many countries of the Samaritans.
\v 26 \f 1 \fr 8.26 \ft b The Epistle upon Thursday in Easter week.\f* And an Angel of our Lord spake to Philippe, saying: Arise, and go toward the South, to the way that goeth down from Jerusalem into Gaza: this is desert. \ef - \fr 8.26 \fq This is desert. \ft Intolerable boldness of some Protestants, here also (as in other places) against all copies both Greek and Latin, to surmise corruption or falsehood of the text, saying it cannot be so: Which is to accuse the holy Evangelist, and to blaspheme the Holy Ghost himself. See ¹ ² Beza, who is often very saucy with S. Luke. \fq ¹ \ft Beza. \fq ² \ft \+it Annot. no. Test. 1556.\+it*\ef*
\v 27 And rising he went. And behold, a man of Æthiopia, an Eunuch, of great authority under Candace the queen of the Æthiopians, who was over all her treasures, was come to Jerusalem \f 1 \fr 8.27 \ft Note that this Æthiopian came to Hierusalem to adore, that is, on Pilgrimage. Whereby we may learn that it is an acceptable act of religion to go from home to places of greater devotion & sanctification.\f* to adore:
\v 28 and he was returning and sitting upon his chariot, and reading Esay the Prophet.
\v 29 And the Spirit said to Philippe: Go near, and join thyself to this same chariot.
\v 30 And Philippe running thereunto, heard him reading Esay the Prophet, and he said: Trowest thou that thou understandest the things which thou readest?
\v 31 Who said: And \f 1 \fr 8.31 \ft The Scriptures are so written that they cannot be understood without an interpreter, as easy as our Protestants make them. See S. Hierom \+it Ep. ad Paulinum de omnibus divinæ historiæ libris\+it*, set in the beginning of Latin Bibles.\f* how can I, unless some man shew me? & he desired Philippe that he would come up and sit with him.
\v 32 And the place of the scripture which he did read, was this: \x - \xt Es. 53, 7.\x* \it As a sheep to slaughter was he led: and as a lamb before his shearer, without voice, so did he not open his mouth.\it*
\v 33 \it In humility his judgement was taken away. His generation who shall declare, for from the earth shall his life be taken?\it*
\v 34 And the Eunuch answering Philip, said: I beseech thee, of whom doth the Prophet speak this? of himself, or of some other?
\v 35 And, Philip opening his mouth, and beginning from this scripture, evangelized unto him \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 36 And as they went by the way, they came to a certain water: and the Eunuch said: Lo water, \f 1 \fr 8.36 \fq who \ft what\f* who doth let me to be baptized?
\v 37 And Philip said: If thou believe with all thy heart, thou mayest. And he answering said: I believe that \sc Jesus Christ\sc* is the Son of God.
\v 38 And he commanded the chariot to stay: and both went down into the water, Philip and the Eunuch, and he baptized him. \ef - \fr 8.38 \fq He baptized him. \ft ¹ When the Heretics of this time find mention made in Scripture of any Sacrament ministered by the Apostles or other in the Primitive Church, they imagine no more was done than there is expressly told, nor scarcely believe so much. As if imposition of hands in the Sacrament of Confirmation be only expressed, they think there was no chrism nor other work or word used. So they think no more ceremony was used in the baptizing of this noble man, than here is mentioned. Whereupon S. Augustine ² hath these memorable words, \+it In that that he saith, Philip baptized him, he would have it understood that all things were done, which though in the\+it* The Epistle upon the Conversion of S. Paul Ian. 25. \+it Act. 22, 4. Gal. 1, 13.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The ceremonies of Sacraments done, though not mentioned. \fq ² \ft \+it De fid. & op. c. 9.\+it*\ef*
\v 39 And when they were come up out of the water, the Spirit of our Lord took away Philip, & the Eunuch saw him no more. And he went on his way rejoicing.
\v 40 But Philip was found in Azotus, and passing through, he evangelized to all the cities, till he came to Cæsarea.
\c 9
\cd Saul not content to persecute so cruelly in Hierusalem, 3. is in the way to Damascus told by our Lord JESUS of his vain attempt, and miraculously converted to be an Apostle: and after great penance, restored to his sight by Ananias, and baptized. 20. And presently he dealeth mightily against the Jews, proving JESUS to be Christ, to their great admiration. 23. But such is their obstinacy, that they lay all Damascus to kill him, 26. From thence he goeth to Hierusalem, and there joineth with the Apostles, and again by the obstinate Jews his death is sought. 31. The Church being now grown over all Jewry, Galilee, and Samaria, Peter visiteth all: and in his visitation, 33. healing a lame man, 36. and raising a dead woman, converteth very many.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \x - \xt Act. 22, 4. Gal. 1, 13.\x*\f 2 \fr 9.1 \ft The Epistle upon the Conversion of S. Paul Ian. 25.\f* Saul as yet breathing forth threatenings and slaughter against the Disciples of our Lord, came to the high priest,
\v 2 and asked letters of him unto Damascus to the Synagogues that if he had found any men and women of this way, he might bring them bound unto Jerusalem.
\v 3 And as he went on his journey, it chanced that he drew nigh to Damascus: and \x - \xt 1. Cor. 15, 8.\x* suddenly a light from Heaven shined round about him.
\v 4 And falling on the ground, he heard a voice saying to him, \f 1 \fr 9.4 \ft The heretics that conclude CHRIST so in heaven that he can be no where else till the day of Judgement, shall hardly resolve a man that would know where CHRIST was when he appeared here in the way, and spake these words to Saul.\f* Saul, Saul why persecutest thou me?
\v 5 Who said: Who art thou Lord? And he: I am \sc Jesus\sc* whom thou doest persecute. It is hard for thee to kick against the prick.
\v 6 And trembling and being astonished he said: Lord, what wilt thou have me to do?
\v 7 And our Lord to him: Arise and go into the city, & it shall be told thee what thou must do. But the men that went in company with him, stood amazed, hearing the voice, but seeing no man.
\v 8 And Saul rose up from the ground, and his eyes being opened, he saw nothing. And they drawing him by the hands, brought him into Damascus.
\v 9 And he was there three days not seeing, and he did neither eat nor drink.
\v 10 And there was a certain Disciple at Damascus, named Ananias: and our Lord said to him in a vision, Ananias. But he said, Lo, here I am Lord.
\v 11 And our Lord to him: Arise, and go into the street that is called Straight: and seek in the house of Judas, one named Saul of Tarsus. For behold he prayeth.
\v 12 (And he saw a man named Ananias, coming in and imposing hands upon him for to receive his sight.)
\v 13 But Ananias answered: Lord, I have heard by many of this man, how much evil he hath done to thy saints in Jerusalem:
\v 14 and here he hath authority from the chief priests to bind all that invocate thy name.
\v 15 And our Lord said to him: Go, for a vessel of election is this man unto me, to carry my name before the Gentiles, and kings, and the children of Israel.
\v 16 For I will shew him how great things he must suffer for my name.
\v 17 And Ananias went, and entered into the house: and imposing hands upon him, he said: Brother Saul, our Lord \sc Jesus\sc* hath sent me, he that appeared to thee in the way that thou camest; that thou mayest see and be filled with the Holy Ghost.
\v 18 And forthwith there fell from his eyes as it were scales, and he received sight: and rising he was \f 1 \fr 9.18 \ft Paul also himself, though with the divine and heavenly voice prostrated and instructed, yet was sent to a man to receive the Sacraments, & to be joined to the Church. \+it August. de doct. Chr. lib. 1. in prooem.\+it*\f* baptized.
\v 19 And when he had taken meat, he was strengthened. And he was with the Disciples that were at Damascus, for certain days.
\v 20 And incontinent entering into the Synagogues, \f 1 \fr 9.20 \fq he \ft \+it in the Synagogues he.\+it*\f* he preached \sc Jesus\sc*, that this is the Son of God.
\v 21 And all that heard, were astonished, and said: Is not this he that expugned in Jerusalem those that invocated this name; and came hither to this purpose that he might bring them bound to the chief priests?
\v 22 But Saul waxed mighty much more and confounded the Jews that dwelt at Damascus, affirming that this is \sc Christ\sc*.
\v 23 And when many days were passed, the Jews consulted that they might kill him.
\v 24 But their conspiracy came to Saul's knowledge. And \x - \xt 2. Cor. 11, 32.\x* they kept the gates also day and night, that they might kill him.
\v 25 But the Disciples taking him in the night, conveyed him away by the wall, letting him down in a basket.
\v 26 And when he was come into Jerusalem, he assayed to join himself to the Disciples, & all feared him, not believing that he was a Disciple.
\v 27 But Barnabas took him & brought him to the Apostles, and told them how in the way he had seen our Lord, and that he spake unto him, & how in Damascus he dealt confidently in the name of \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 28 And he was with them going in & going out in Jerusalem, & dealing confidently in the name of our Lord.
\v 29 He spake also to the Gentiles, & disputed with the Greeks: but they sought to kill him.
\v 30 Which when the brethren had known, they brought him down to Cæsarea & sent him away to Tarsus.
\v 31 The \f 1 \fr 9.31 \ft The Church visibly proceedeth still with much comfort & manifold increase even by persecution.\f* \sc Church\sc* truly through all Jewry & Galilee and Samaria had peace, & was edified, walking in the fear of our Lord, and was replenished with the consolation of the Holy Ghost.
\v 32 And it came to pass, that Peter as he passed through all, came to the saints that dwelt at Lydda.
\v 33 and he found there a certain man named Æneas, lying in his bed from eight years before, who had the palsy.
\v 34 And Peter said to him: Æneas, our Lord \sc Jesus Christ\sc* \f 1 \fr 9.34 \fq heal \ft healeth\f* heal thee: arise, and make thy bed. And incontinent he arose.
\v 35 And all that dwelt at Lydda and Sarona, saw him: who converted to our Lord.
\v 36 And in Joppe there was a certain Disciple named Tabitha, which by interpretation is called Dorcas. This woman was full of \f 1 \fr 9.36 \ft Behold good works and alms-deeds, & the force thereof reaching even to the next life.\f* good works & alms-deeds which she did.
\v 37 And it came to pass in those days, that she was sick and died. Whom when they had washed, they laid her in an upper chamber.
\v 38 And whereas Lydda was nigh to Joppe, the Disciples hearing that Peter was in it, they sent two men unto him, desiring him: Be not loath to come so far as to us.
\v 39 And Peter rising up came with them. And when he was come, they brought him into the upper chamber: and all the widows stood about him weeping, \f 1 \fr 9.39 \ft The prayers of our Almsfolk & beadsmen may do us great good even after our departure. For if they procured her temporal life, much more may they help us to God's mercy and to release of punishment in Purgatory.\f* and shewing him the coats and garments which Dorcas made them.
\v 40 And all being put forth, Peter falling on his knees prayed, and turning to the body he said: Tabitha, arise. And she opened her eyes: and seeing Peter, she sat up.
\v 41 And giving her his hand, he lifted her up. And when he had called the saints and the widows, he presented her alive.
\v 42 And it was made known throughout all Joppe: and many believed in our Lord.
\v 43 And it came to pass that he abode many days in Joppe, with one Simon a tanner.
\c 10
\cd Because the Jews so much abhorred the Gentiles, for the better warrant of their Christening, an Angel appeareth to Cornelius the devout Italian. 9. and a vision is shewed to Peter himself (the chief and Paster of all) 19. and the Spirit speaketh to him, 34. yea and as he is Catechizing them about JESUS, 44. the Holy Ghost cometh visibly upon them: and therefore not fearing any longer the offence of the Jews, he commandeth to baptize them. \f - \ft The 4. part. THE propagation of the Church to the Gentiles also.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* there was a certain man in Cæsarea, named Cornelius, Centurion of that which is called the Italian band,
\v 2 religious, & fearing God with all his house, doing many alms-deeds to the people. And always praying to God, \ef - \fr 10.2 \fq Doing many alms deeds. \ft \+it He knew God creator of all, but that his omnipotent Son was incarnate, he knew not:\+it* ¹ \+it and in that faith he made prayers and gave alms which pleased God. And by well doing he deserved to know God perfectly, to believe the mystery of the Incarnation, and to come to the Sacrament of Baptism.\+it* So saith Venerable Bede ² out of S. Gregory. And S. Augustine thus, \+it li. 1. de Bapt. c. 8. Because whatsoever goodness he had in prayers and alms, the same could not profit him unless he were by the band of Christian Society and peace, incorporated to the Church, he is bidden to send unto Peter, that by him he may learn Christ, by him he may be baptized, &c.\+it* Whereby it appeareth that such works as are done before justification, though they suffice not to salvation, yet be acceptable preparatives to the grace of justification, and such as move God to mercy, as it might appear also by God's like provident mercifulness ³ to the Eunuch, though all such works preparative come of grace also: otherwise they could never deserve at God's hand of congruity or any otherwise toward justification. \fq ¹ \ft Good works before faith, are preparatives to the same, not properly meritorious. \fq ² \ft \+it Bed. in hunc locum.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Act. 8.\+it*\ef*
\v 3 he saw in a vision manifestly, about the ninth hour of the day, an Angel of God coming in unto him and saying to him: Cornelius.
\v 4 But he beholding him, taken with fear, said: Who art thou Lord? And he said to him: Thy prayers and thy alms-deeds are ascended into remembrance in the sight of God.
\v 5 And now send men unto Joppe, and call hither one Simon that is surnamed Peter.
\v 6 He lodgeth with one Simon a tanner, whose house is by the sea side. He will tell thee what thou must do.
\v 7 And when the Angel was departed that spake to him, he called two of his household, and a soldier that feared our Lord, of them that were under him.
\v 8 To whom when he had told all, he sent them unto Joppe.
\v 9 And the next day whiles they were going on their journey, and drawing nigh to the city, Peter went up into the higher parts, to pray about the sixth hour. \ef - \fr 10.9 \fq To pray about the sixth hour. \ft ¹ The hour is specified, for that there were certain appointed times of prayer used in the Law, which devout persons, according to the public service in the Temple, observed also privately: and which the Apostles and holy Church afterward both kept and increased. Whereof thus writeth S. Cyprian ² very notably. \+it In celebrating their prayers, we find that the three children with Daniel observed the third, sixth, and ninth hour, as in Sacrament\+it* (or mystery) \+it of the holy Trinity. &c.\+it* And a little after, \+it Which spaces of hours the worshippers of God spiritually\+it* (or mystically) \+it determining long since, observed set times to pray: and afterward the thing became manifest, that it was for Sacrament\+it* (or mystery) \+it that the just so prayed. For at the third hour the Holy Ghost descended upon the Apostles\+it* ³ \+it , fulfilling the grace of our Lord's promise, and at the sixth hour Peter going up to the higher room of the house\+it* ⁴ \+it , was both by voice and sign from God instructed, that all Nations should be admitted to the grace of salvation, whereas of cleansing the Gentiles he doubted before. And our Lord being crucified at the sixth hour\+it* ⁵ \+it , at the ninth washed away our sins with his blood. But to us (dearly beloved) beside the seasons observed of old, both the times and sacraments of praying be increased. For we must pray in the morning early\+it* ⁶ \+it , that the Resurrection of our Lord may be celebrated by morning prayer: as of old the Holy Ghost designed in the Psalm\+it* ⁷ \+it , saying, In the morning early will I stand to thee, early in the morning wilt thou hear my voice.\+it* Toward the evening ⁸ \+it also when the sun departeth, and the day endeth, we must of necessity pray again.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The Canonical hours. \fq ² \ft \+it De Orat. Dom. nu. 15.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Act. 2.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Act. 10.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Luc. 23.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft Matins. \fq ⁷ \ft \+it Psal. 5.\+it* \fq ⁸ \ft Evensong.\ef* \ef - \fr 10.9 \ft S. Hierom also writing of ¹ Daniel's praying \+it three times in a day\+it*, saith: \+it There are three times, wherein we must bow our knees to God. The third, the sixth, and the ninth hour the Ecclesiastical tradition doth well understand. Moreover at the third hour the Holy Ghost descended upon the Apostles. At the sixth, Peter went up into a higher chamber to pray. At the ninth,\+it* ² \+it Peter and John went to the Temple.\+it* Again writing to Eustochium a virgin and Nun ep. 22. c. 16. \+it Though the Apostle bid us pray always, and to holy persons their very sleep is prayer: yet we must have distinct hours of prayer, that if perhaps we be otherwise occupied, the very time may admonish us of our office or duty. The third, sixth, ninth hour, morning early, and the evening, no man can be ignorant of.\+it* And to Demetrias ep. 8. c. 8. that in the Psalms and prayer she must keep \+it always the third, sixth, ninth hour, evening, midnight, and morning.\+it* He hath the like ep. 7. c. 5. And (ep. 27. c. 10.) he telleth how Paula the holy Abbess with her religious Nuns \+it sang the Psalter in order, in the morning, at the third, sixth, ninth hour, evening, midnight:\+it* by midnight meaning the time of Matins (therefore called \+it Nocturnes\+it* agreeably to S. Cyprian de Orat. Do. num. 15.) and by \+it the morning\+it*, the first hour called \+it Prime\+it*: all correspondent to the times and hours of Christ's Passion, as in S. Matthew is noted c. 26. 27. By all which we see, how agreeable the use of the Church's service is even at this time to the Scriptures and primitive Church: and how wicked the Puritan-Calvinists be, that count all such order and set seasons of prayer, superstition: and lastly, how insufficient and unlike the new pretended Church-service of England is to the primitive use, which hath no such hours of night or day, saving a little imitation of the old Matins and Evensong, and that in Schism and Heresy, and therefore not only unprofitable, but also damnable. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Dan. 6.\+it* \fq ² \ft \+it Act. 3.\+it*\ef*
\v 10 And being hungry, he was desirous to take somewhat. And as they were preparing, there fell upon him an excess of mind:
\v 11 and he saw the Heaven opened, and a certain vessel descending, as it were a great linen sheet with four corners let down from Heaven to the earth,
\v 12 wherein were all four-footed beasts, and that creep on the earth, and fowls of the air.
\v 13 And there came a voice to him: Arise Peter, kill, and eat.
\v 14 But Peter said: God forbid, Lord; for I did never eat any common and unclean thing.
\v 15 And \f 1 \fr 10.15 \ft Here God first uttered to Peter that the time was come to preach also to the Gentiles, and to converse with them for their salvation, no less than with the Jews, with full freedom to eat all meats without respect of the prohibition of certain, made in the old Law.\f* a voice came to him again the second time: That which God hath purified, do not thou call common.
\v 16 And this was done thrice, & forthwith the vessel was taken up again into Heaven.
\v 17 And whiles Peter doubted within himself, what the vision should be that he had seen, behold the men that were sent from Cornelius, inquiring for Simon's house, stood at the gate.
\v 18 And when they had called, they asked, if Simon that is surnamed Peter were lodged there.
\v 19 And as Peter was thinking of the vision, the Spirit said to him: Behold, three men do seek thee.
\v 20 Arise therefore, and get thee down, and go with them, doubting nothing; for I have sent them.
\v 21 And Peter going down to the men, said: Behold I am he whom you seek; what is the cause, for the which you are come?
\v 22 Who said: Cornelius the Centurion, a just man and that feareth God, and having testimony of all the nation of the Jews, received an answer of an holy Angel to send for thee into his house, and to hear words of thee.
\v 23 Therefore bringing them in, he lodged them. And the day following he arose and went with them: and certain of the brethren of Joppe accompanied him.
\v 24 And on the morrow he entered into Cæsarea. And Cornelius expected them, having called together his kin, and special friends.
\v 25 And it came to pass, when Peter was come in, Cornelius came to meet him, & falling at his feet adored. \ef - \fr 10.25 \fq Adored. \ft ¹ S. Chrysostom \+it ho. 21. in Act.\+it* thinketh Peter refused this adoration of humility only, because every falling down to the ground for worship sake, is not Divine worship or due only to God, ² the word of adoration and prostration being commonly used in the Scriptures toward men. But S. Hierom. \+it adv. Vigil. c. 2. to. 2.\+it* rather thinketh that Cornelius by error of Gentility, and of Peter's person, did go about to adore him with Divine honour, and therefore was lifted up by the Apostle, adding that he was but a man. \fq ¹ \ft Adoration of creatures. \fq ² \ft \+it * προσκυνεῖν.\+it*\ef*
\v 26 But Peter lifted him up saying: Arise, myself also am a man.
\v 27 And talking with him, he went in, and findeth many that were assembled.
\v 28 And he said to them: You know how abominable it is for a man that is a Jew, to join, or to approach unto a stranger: but God hath shewed to me, to call no man common or unclean.
\v 29 For the which cause, making no doubt, I came when I was sent for. I demand therefore, for what cause you have sent for me?
\v 30 And Cornelius said: Four days since, until this hour, I was \f 1 \fr 10.30 \ft c At the time of prayer specially God sendeth men comfortable visitations.\f*\f 2 \fr 10.30 \ft \+it c In the Greek, fasting & praying.\+it*\f* praying the ninth hour in my house, and behold \f 3 \fr 10.30 \ft Note these apparitions and visions to S. Peter, Cornelius, and others, in the Scriptures very often, against the incredulity of our Heretics, that will believe neither vision nor miracle, not expressed in Scripture: these being believed of Christian men even before they were written.\f* a man stood before me in white apparel,
\v 31 and said: Cornelius, thy prayer is heard, and thy alms-deeds are in memory in the sight of God.
\v 32 Send therefore to Joppe, and call hither Simon that is surnamed Peter: he lodgeth in the house of Simon a tanner by the sea side.
\v 33 Immediately therefore I sent to thee: and thou hast done well in coming. Now therefore all we are present in thy sight, to hear all things whatsoever are commanded thee of the Lord.
\v 34 And Peter opening his mouth, said: In very deed I perceive that God is not an accepter of persons.
\v 35 But in every nation, he that feareth him, and \f 1 \fr 10.35 \ft c Not such as believe only, but such as fear God and work justice, are acceptable to him.\f* worketh justice, is acceptable to him.
\v 36 The word did God send to the children of Israel, preaching peace by \sc Jesus Christ\sc* (this is Lord of all.)
\v 37 \x - \xt Mat. 4, 12.\x* You know the word that hath been made through all Jewry, for \f 2 \fr 10.37 \ft b The Epistle upon Monday in Easter week.\f* beginning from Galilee, after the baptism which John preached:
\v 38 \sc Jesus\sc* of Nazareth how God anointed him with the Holy Ghost and with power, who went throughout doing good and healing all that were oppressed of the devil, because God was with him.
\v 39 And we are witnesses of all things that he did in the country of the Jews and in Jerusalem, whom they killed hanging him upon a tree.
\v 40 Him God raised up the third day and gave him to be made manifest, \ef - \fr 10.40 \fq Gave him. \ft Christ did not utter his Resurrection and other mysteries to all at once, and immediately to the vulgar: but to a few chosen men that should be the governors of the rest, instructing us thereby to take our faith and all necessary things of salvation, at the hands of our Superiors.\ef*
\v 41 not to all the people, but to us, who did eat and drink with him after he rose again from the dead.
\v 42 \f 1 \fr 10.42 \ft The Epistle upon Monday in Whitsunweek.\f* And he commanded us to preach to the people, & to testify that it is he that of God was appointed judge of the living and of the dead.
\v 43 To him all the prophets give testimony, that all receive remission of sins by his name, which believe in him.
\v 44 As Peter was yet speaking these words, the Holy Ghost fell upon all that heard the word.
\v 45 And the faithful of the Circumcision that came with Peter, were astonished, for that the grace of the Holy Ghost was poured out upon the Gentiles also.
\v 46 For they heard them speaking with tongues, and magnifying God.
\v 47 Then Peter answered: Can any man forbid water, that these should not be baptized which have received the Holy Ghost as well as we? \ef - \fr 10.47 \fq Baptized, which have received. \ft ¹ Such may be the grace of God sometimes toward men, and their charity and contrition so great, that they may have remission, justification, and sanctification before the external Sacrament of Baptism, Confirmation, or Penance be received, as we see in this example, where at Peter's preaching they all received the Holy Ghost before any Sacrament. But in the same we learn one necessary lesson, that such notwithstanding must needs receive the Sacraments appointed by Christ, which whosoever contemneth, can never be justified. \+it Aug. super Levit. q. 84. to. 4.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft They that are justified before, must not omit the Sacraments.\ef*
\v 48 And he commanded them to be baptized in the name of our Lord \sc Jesus Christ\sc*. Then they desired him that he would tarry with them certain days.
\c 11
\cd The Christian Jews reprehend the foresaid fact of Peter in baptizing the Gentiles 4. But he alleging his foresaid warrants, and shewing plainly that it was of God, 18. they like good Catholics do yield. 19. By the foresaid persecution, the Church is yet further dilated, not only into all Jewry, Galilee, and Samaria, but also into other Countries: specially in Antiochia Syriæ the increase among the Greeks, is notable, first by the foresaid dispersed, 22. then by Barnabas, thirdly by him and Saul together: so that there beginneth the name of Christians: 27. with perfit unity between them and the Church that was before them at Hierusalem.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* the Apostles and brethren that were in Jewry, heard that the Gentiles also received the word of God.
\v 2 And when Peter was come up to Jerusalem, they that were of the Circumcision reasoned against him, saying:
\v 3 Why didst thou enter in to men \f 1 \fr 11.3 \ft \+it c præputium habentes.\+it*\f* uncircumcised, and didst eat with them?
\v 4 But Peter began and declared to them the order, saying:
\v 5 \x - \xt Act. 10, 9.\x* I was in the city of Joppe praying, & I saw in an excess of mind a vision, a certain vessel descending as it were a great sheet with four corners let down from heaven, & it came even unto me.
\v 6 Into which I looking considered, & saw four footed beasts of the earth, & cattle, & such as creep, & fowls of the air.
\v 7 And I heard also a voice saying to me: Arise Peter, kill and eat.
\v 8 And I said: Not so Lord; for common or unclean thing never entered into my mouth.
\v 9 And a voice answered the second time from heaven: That which God hath made clean, do not thou call common.
\v 10 And this was done thrice: and all were taken up again into heaven.
\v 11 And behold, three men immediately were come to the house wherein I was, sent to me from Cæsarea.
\v 12 And the spirit said to me, that I should go with them, doubting nothing. And there came with me these six brethren also: and we went into the man's house.
\v 13 And he told us, how he had seen an Angel in his house, standing and saying to him: Send to Joppe, and call hither Simon, that is surnamed Peter,
\v 14 who shall speak to thee words wherein thou shalt be saved and all thy house.
\v 15 And when I had begun to speak, the Holy Ghost fell upon them, as upon us also in the beginning.
\v 16 And I remembered the word of our Lord, according as he said: \x - \xt Act. 1, 5.\x* \it John indeed baptized with water, but you shall be baptized with the Holy Ghost.\it*
\v 17 If therefore God hath given them the same grace, as to us also that believed in our Lord \sc Jesus Christ\sc*: who was I that might prohibit God?
\v 18 Having heard these things, they \f 1 \fr 11.18 \ft Good Christians hear and obey gladly such truths as be opened unto them from God by their chief Pastors, by vision, revelation, or otherwise.\f* held their peace: & glorified God, saying: God then to the Gentiles also hath given repentance unto life.
\v 19 \x - \xt Act. 8, 1.\x* And they truly that had been dispersed by the tribulation that was made under Steven, walked throughout unto Phoenice & Cypres & Antioch, speaking the word to none, but to the Jews only.
\v 20 But certain of them were men of Cypres and Cyrene, who when they were entered into Antioch, spake to the Greeks, preaching our Lord \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 21 \f 1 \fr 11.21 \ft The Epistle upon S. Barnabee's day. Iun. 11.\f* And the hand of our Lord was with them: and a great number of believers was converted to our Lord.
\v 22 And the report came to the ears of the Church that was at Jerusalem, touching these things: and they sent \x - \xt Act. 4, 36.\x* Barnabas as far as Antioch.
\v 23 Who when he was come, and saw the grace of God, rejoiced: and he exhorted all with purpose of heart to continue in our Lord:
\v 24 because he was a good man, and full of the Holy Ghost and faith. And a great multitude was added to our Lord. \ef - \fr 11.24 \fq Multitude added. \ft As before (c. 10.) a few, so now great numbers of Gentiles are adjoined also to the visible Church ¹, consisting before only of the Jews. Which Church hath been ever since Christ's Ascension, notoriously seen and known: their preaching open, their Sacraments visible, their discipline visible, their Heads and Governors visible, the provision for their maintenance visible, the persecution visible, their dispersion visible: the Heretics that went out from them, visible: the joining either of men or Nations unto them, visible: their peace and rest after persecutions, visible: their Governors in prison, visible: the Church prayeth for them visibly, their Councels visible, their gifts and graces visible, their name (Christians) known to all the world. Of the Protestants' invisible Church we hear not one word. \fq ¹ \ft The Church visible.\ef*
\v 25 And he went forth to \x - \xt Act. 9, 30.\x* Tarsus, to seek Saul:
\v 26 whom when he had found, he brought him to Antioch. And they conversed there in the church a whole year: and they taught a great multitude, so that the Disciples were at Antioch first named \f 1 \fr 11.26 \ft The name of CHRISTIANS.\f* \sc Christians\sc*. \ef - \fr 11.26 \fq Christians. \ft This name, \+it Christian\+it*, ought to be common to all the Faithful, and other new names of Schismatics and Sectaries must be abhorred. \+it If thou hear\+it* (saith S. Hierom ¹ ) \+it any where, such as be said to be of Christ, not to have their names of our Lord \+sc Jesus Christ\+sc*, but to be called after some other certain name, as Marcionites, Valentinians\+it*, (as now also the Lutherans, Calvinists, Protestants) \+it know thou that they belong not to the Church of Christ, but to the Synagogue of Antichrist\+it*. Lactantius also (li. 7. Divin. instit. c. 30.) saith thus, \+it when Phrygians, or Novatians, or Valentinians, or Marcionites, or Anthropomorphites, or Arians, or any other be named, they cease to be Christians, who having lost the name of Christ, have done on the names of men\+it* ². Neither can our new Sectaries discharge themselves, for that they take not to themselves these names, but are forced to bear them as given by their Adversaries. For, so were the names of Arians and the rest of old, imposed by others, and not chosen commonly of themselves: Which notwithstanding were callings that proved them to be Heretics. And as for the name of ³ Protestants, our men hold them well content therewith. But concerning the Heretics turning of the argument against the peculiar callings of our Religious, ⁴ as Dominicans, Franciscans, Jesuits, Thomists, or such like, it is nothing, except they could prove that the orders & persons so named, were of diverse faiths & Sects, or differed in any necessary point of religion, or were not all of one Christian name and communion: and it is as ridiculous as if it were objected, that some be Ciceronians, some Plinians, some good Augustine men, some Hieronymians, some Oxford men, some Cambridge men, & (which is most like) some Rechabites, some ⁵ Nazareites. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Hierony. cont. Lucif c. 7. in fine.\+it* \fq ² \ft Names of Sectaries and Heretics. \fq ³ \ft Protestants. \fq ⁴ \ft Diverse religious orders are not diverse sects. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Ierem. 35. Num. 6.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 11.26 \ft ¹ Neither doth their objection, that we be called Papists, help or excuse them in their new names. For, besides that it is by them scornfully invented (as the name Homousians was of the Arians) this name is not of any one man, B. of Rome or elsewhere, known to be the author of any schism or sect, as their callings be: but it is of a whole state and order of governors, and that of the chief Governors, to whom we are bound to cleave in religion and to obey in all things. So to be a Papist, is to be a Christian man, a child of the Church, and subject to Christ's Vicar. And therefore against such impudent Sectaries as compare the faithful for following the Pope, to the diversity of Heretic's bearing the names of new Masters, let us ever have in readiness this saying of S. Hierom ² to Pope Damasus, ³ \+it Vitalis I know not, Meletius I refuse, I know not Paulinus, whosoever gathereth not with thee, scattereth: that is to say, whosoever is not Christ's, is Antichrist's\+it*. And again, \+it If any man join with Peter's Chair, he is mine\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Papists, Catholics, and true Christians, all one. \fq ² \ft \+it to. 2. ep. 57. & ep. 58. ad Damas.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Not to be with the Pope, is to be with Antichrist.\ef* \ef - \fr 11.26 \ft ¹ \fq ¹ \ft The name of CHRISTIANS.\ef* \ef - \fr 11.26 \ft We must here further observe that this name, Christian, given to all believers and to the whole Church, was specially taken to distinguish them from the Jews and Heathens which believed not at all in Christ, and the same now severeth and maketh known all Christian men from Turks and others that hold not of Christ at all. But when Heretics began to rise from among the Christians, who professed Christ's name and sundry Articles of faith as true believers do, the name \+it Christian\+it* was too common to sever the Heretics from true faithful men: ¹ and thereupon the Apostles by the Holy Ghost imposed this name \+it Catholic\+it* upon the Believers which in all points were obedient to the Church's doctrine. \+it When heresies were risen\+it* (saith S. Pacianus ep. ad Symphorianum) \+it & endeavoured by diverse names to tear the dove of God and Queen, and to rent her in pieces, the Apostolical people required their surname, whereby the incorrupt people might be distinguished. &c.\+it* and so those that before were called Christians, are now surnamed also Catholics. \+it Christian is my name\+it*, saith he, \+it Catholic my surname\+it*. And this word, Catholic, is the proper note whereby the holy Apostles in their Creed ² taught us to discern the true Church from the false heretical congregation of what sort soever. And not only the meaning of the word, which signifieth universality of times, places, and persons, but the very name and word itself, by God's providence, always and only appropriated to the true believers, and (though sometimes at the beginning of Sects challenged) yet never obtained by Heretics, giveth so plain a mark and evidence, that S. Augustine said, \+it In the lap of the Church the very name of Catholic keepeth me.\+it* cont. ep. fund. c. 4. And again tract. 32. in Io. \+it we receive the Holy Ghost if we love the Church, if we be joined together by charity, if we rejoice in the Catholic name and faith\+it*. And again de ver. rel. c. 7. to. 1. \+it We must hold the communion of that Church which is named Catholic, not only of her own, but also of all her enemies. For, will they nill they, the Heretics also and Schismatics themselves, when they speak not with their own fellows but with strangers, call the Catholic Church nothing else but the Catholic Church: for they could not be understood unless they discern it by this name wherewith she is called of all the world\+it*. ³ The Heretics when they see themselves prevented of this name \+it Catholic\+it*, then they plainly reject it and deride the name, as the Donatists did, calling it an \+it human forgery or fiction\+it*, which S. Augustine calleth words of blasphemy. \+it li. 1. c. 33. cont. Gaudent.\+it* and some Heretics of this time call them scornfully cartholics, and cacolics. Another calleth it, \+it the most vain term Catholic. Beza in præf. no. Test. an. 1565\+it*. Another calleth the Catholic religion, \+it a Catholic Apostasy or defection, Humfrey in vit. Iuel. pag. 213.\+it* Yea and some have taken the word out of the Creed, ⁴ putting \+it Christian\+it* for it. But against these good fellows let us follow that which S. Augustine \+it (de util. cred. c. 8. to. 6.)\+it* giveth as a rule to direct a man the right and sure way from the diversity & doubtfulness of all error, saying, \+it If after these troubles of mind thou seem to thyself sufficiently tossed and vexed, & wilt have an end of these molestations, follow the way of Catholic discipline, which from Christ himself by the Apostles hath proceeded even unto us, and shall proceed from hence to the posterity\+it*. See the Annotation \+it 1. Tim. 3. c. 15.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The name of CATHOLICS. \fq ² \ft CREDO ECCLESIAM CATHOLICAM. \fq ³ \ft The Protestants deride the name CATHOLIC. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it * In the Catechisms of the Lutherans.\+it*\ef*
\v 27 And in these days there came Prophets from Jerusalem to Antioch,
\v 28 and one of them rising, named Agabus, did by the Spirit signify a great famine that should be in the whole world, which fell under Claudius.
\v 29 And the Disciples according as each man had, purposed every one to send, for to serve the brethren that dwelt in Jewry:
\v 30 which also they did, sending to the ancients by the hands of Barnabas and Saul.
\c 12
\cd Herod the first king that persecuted the Church, having at Hierusalem (when Barnabas and Saul were there with the collation of the Antiochians) killed James the Apostle, 3. and to please the Jews imprisoned Peter with the mind to kill him also, but frustrate by an Angel sent of God at the continual prayers of the Church made for her chief Pastor, 19. being puffed up with such pride that at Cæsarea he refuseth not to be honoured as God: 23. is miraculously striken of God's Angel. 24. And so after the persecutor's death, the Church's preaching prospereth exceedingly.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \f 1 \fr 12.1 \ft The Epistle upon SS. Peter and Paul's day Iun. 29.\f* at the same time Herod the king set his hands, to afflict certain of the Church.
\v 2 And he killed James the brother of John with the sword.
\v 3 And seeing that it pleased the Jews, he added to apprehend Peter also. And it was the days of the Azymes.
\v 4 Whom when he had apprehended, he cast into prison, delivering him to \f 1 \fr 12.4 \ft As Peter's person was more notorious than others, & therefore better guarded than other, for fear he should escape; so God's providence in preserving & delivering him for the longer government of his Church, is very marvelous.\f* four quaternions of soldiers to be kept, meaning after the Pasch to bring him forth to the people.
\v 5 And Peter indeed was kept in prison. But prayer was made of the Church without intermission unto God for him. \ef - \fr 12.5 \fq Prayer was made. \ft The Church prayed incessantly for her chief Pastor, and was heard of God: and all Christian people are warned thereby to pray for their Bishops and Pastors in prison.\ef*
\v 6 And when Herod would have brought him forth, the same night Peter was sleeping between two soldiers, bound with two chains: and the keepers before the door kept the prison. \ef - \fr 12.6 \fq Two chains. \ft ¹ These chains are famous for miracles, and were brought from Hierusalem to Rome by Eudoxia the Empress, wife to Theodosius the younger, where they were matched & place with another chain that the same Apostle was tied with by Nero, & a Church founded thereupon, named \+it Petri ad vincula\+it*, where they are religiously kept and reverenced until this day, and there is a Feast in the whole Church for the same, the first of August, which we call, \+it Lammas day\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft S. Peter's chains.\ef*
\v 7 And behold an Angel of our Lord stood in presence: and light shined in the house: and striking Peter's side, he raised him, saying: Arise quickly. And the chains fell from his hands.
\v 8 And the Angel said to him: Gird thee, and put on thy shoes. And he did so. And he said to him: Put thy garment about thee, & follow me.
\v 9 And going forth he followed him, & he knew not that it was true which was done by the Angel: but he thought that he saw a vision.
\v 10 And passing through the first & the second watch, they came to the iron gate that leadeth to the city, which of itself opened to them. And going out, they went forward one street: and incontinent the Angel departed from him.
\v 11 And Peter returning to himself, said: Now I know in very deed that our Lord hath sent his Angel, and delivered me out of Herod's hand, & from all the expectation of the people of the Jews.
\v 12 And considering, he came to the \f 1 \fr 12.12 \ft It is much for the praise of these good Christians that the assembly to God's service & prayer was kept in their houses in the time of persecution, & that the Apostle came thither straight out of prison, as his first refuge; as now Christian people do much to their commendation, in places where Heresy doth reign.\f* house of Mary the mother of John, who was surnamed Mark, where many were gathered and praying.
\v 13 And when he knocked at the door of the gate, there came forth a wench to see, named Rhode.
\v 14 And as she knew Peter's voice, for joy she opened not the gate, but running in she told that Peter stood before the gate.
\v 15 But they said to her: Thou art mad. But she affirmed that it was so. But they said: It is his Angel. \ef - \fr 12.15 \fq His Angel \ft ¹ \+it If proper Angels\+it* (saith S. Chrysostom) \+it be deputed by our Lord to such as have only charge of their own life, (as one of the just said\+it* ² \+it , The Angel which hath delivered me from my youth upward) much more are supernal Spirits at hand to help them unto whom the charge and burden of the world is committed\+it*. Chrys. in laud. Pauli. ho. 7, to. 3. \fq ¹ \ft Patronage of Angels. \fq ² \ft \+it Gen. 48, 16.\+it*\ef*
\v 16 And Peter continued knocking. And when they had opened, they saw him, & were astonished.
\v 17 And beckoning with his hand to them, that they should hold their peace, he told how our Lord had brought him out of prison, & he said: Tell these things to James & to the brethren. And going forth he went \f 1 \fr 12.17 \ft Though God had so miraculously delivered him, yet he would not tempt God by tarrying among his persecutors, but according to Christ's commandment fled for a time.\f* into another place. \ef - \fr 12.17 \fq Tell James. \ft ¹ He willeth them to shew this to S. James Bishop of Hierusalem and to the Christians, that they might see the effect of their prayers for him, & give God thanks. For S. James no doubt published common prayer for S. Peter. \fq ¹ \ft Public prayer for S. Peter the head.\ef*
\v 18 And when day was come, there was no little ado between the soldiers, what was become of Peter.
\v 19 And Herod, when he had sought him, and had not found, making inquisition of the keepers, commanded them to be led away: & going down from Jewry into Cæsarea, there he abode.
\v 20 And he was angry with the Tyrians and the Sidonians. But they with one accord came to him, and persuading Blastus that was chief of the king's chamber, they desired peace, for that their countries were nourished by him.
\v 21 And upon a day appointed, Herod being arrayed with kingly attire, sat in the judgement seat, and made an oration to them.
\v 22 And the people made acclamation, The voices of a God, & not of a man.
\v 23 And forthwith an Angel of our Lord \f 1 \fr 12.23 \ft Princes that take delight in the flattery and praises of the people, so much that they forget themselves to be men, & to give the honour to God, may be warned by this example.\f* strook him, because he had not given the honour to God: and being consumed of worms, he gave up the ghost.
\v 24 But the word of our Lord increased and multiplied.
\v 25 And Barnabas and Saul returned from Jerusalem, having accomplished their \x - \xt Act. 11, 29.\x* ministry, taking with them John that was surnamed Mark.
\c 13
\cd The preachers of the Church of Antioch preparing themselves, the Holy Ghost out of them all, chooseth Saul and Barnabas. 3. They being first consecrated Bishops, 4. go their appointed circuit over all the land of Cypres, the Proconsul whereof is also converted, seeing the miraculous excecation of a Jew by Paul. 13. Thence, into Pamphilia: 14. and Pisidia, where in Antioch Paul preacheth to the Jews, shewing that JESUS is Christ, 38. and that in him is salvation, and not in their Law of Moyses: 40. warning them to beware of the reprobation foretold by the Prophets. 44. But the next Sabboth, they \it blaspheming, he in plain terms forsaketh them, and turneth to the Gentiles. Whereat the Gentiles be as glad on the contrary side. 50. Finally the Jews raising persecution, they forsake them, pronouncing them to be obstinate contemners.\it* \f - \ft The 5. part. THE taking of the Gospel away from the obstinate Jews, and giving of it to the Gentiles, by the ministry of Paul and Barnabas.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* there were in the Church which was at Antioch, Prophets and Doctors, among whom was Barnabas, and Simon that was called Niger, and Lucius of Cyrene, and Manahen who was the foster-brother of Herod the Tetrarch, and Saul.
\v 2 And \f 1 \fr 13.2 \ft \+it c λειτουργούντων ἀυτῶν\+it*\f* as they were ministering to our Lord, & fasting, the Holy Ghost said: Separate me Saul and Barnabas unto the work, whereto I have taken them. \ef - \fr 13.2 \fq As they were ministering. \ft ¹ If we should, as our Adversaries do, boldly turn what text we list, and flee from one language to another for the advantage of our cause, we might have translated for \+it ministering, sacrificing\+it*. For so ² the Greek doth signify, and so Erasmus translated. Yea we might have translated, \+it Saying Mass\+it*, for so they did: and the Greek Fathers here had their name, Liturgy, which Erasmus translateth \+it Mass\+it*, saying, \+it Missa Chrysostomi\+it*. But we keep our text, as the translators of the Scriptures should do most religiously. \fq ¹ \ft The Apostle's liturgy or Mass. \fq ² \ft \+it λειτουργούντων ἀυτῶν.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 13.2 \fq Separate me. \ft ¹ Though Paul were taught by God himself ² and specially designed by Christ to be an Apostle, and here chosen by the Holy Ghost together with Barnabas, yet they were to be ordered, consecrated, and admitted by men. Which wholly condemneth all these new rebellious disordered spirits, that challenge and usurp the office of preaching and other sacred actions from heaven, without the Church's admission. \fq ¹ \ft Paul & Barnabas are consecrated by men. \fq ² \ft \+it Gal. 1.\+it*\ef*
\v 3 Then they fasting and praying, and imposing hands upon them, dismissed them. \ef - \fr 13.3 \fq Fasting. \ft ¹ Hereof the Church of God useth and prescribeth public fasts at the four solemn times of giving Holy Orders (which are our \+it Imber days\+it* ) as a necessary preparative to so great a work, as S. Leo declareth by this place, naming it also an Apostolical tradition. See. S. ² Leo \+it Ser. 9. de ieiunio 7. mensis, & Calixtus ep. 1. to. 1. Conc. Magunt. c. 34. 35. to. 3\+it*. ³ And this fasting was not fasting from sin, nor moral or Christian temperance, as the Protestants ridiculously affirm, for such fasting they were bound ever to keep: but it was abstinence for a time from all meats or from some certain kinds of meats, which was joined with prayer and sacrifice, and done specially at such seasons as the Church prescribed, of all together (as in Lent, the Imber days, Friday, Saturday) and not when every man list, as Ærius and such Heretics did hold. \+it S. August. hær. 53.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Imber days. \fq ² \ft \+it * Leo ep. 81. c. 1. Epipha. in compend. Leo ser. 3. et 4. de ieiu. 7. mensis.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Præscript times of fasts.\ef* \ef - \fr 13.3 \fq Imposing hands. \ft ¹ Because all blessings and consecrations were done in the Apostles' time by the external ceremony of imposition of hands, diverse Sacraments were named of the same, specially Confirmation, as is noted before, and ² holy Ordering or consecrating Bishops, Priests, and Deacons, and Subdeacons, as we see here and elsewhere. In which though there were many holy words and ceremonies and a very solemn action: yet whatsoever is done in those Sacraments, is altogether called \+it imposition of hands\+it* ³: as whatsoever was done in the whole divine mystery of the B. ⁴ Sacrament, is named \+it fraction of bread\+it*. For the Apostles (as S. Denys \+it Eccl. hier. c. 1. in fine\+it* writeth) purposely kept close in their open speeches and writings which might come to the hands or ears of Infidels, the sacred words and actions of the Sacraments. And S. Ambrose saith, \+it in 1. Tim. c. 4. The imposition of the hands is mystical words, wherewith the elected is conformed and made apt to his function, receiving authority (his conscience bearing witness) that he may be bold in our Lord's stead to offer sacrifice to God.\+it* And S. Hierom ⁵, \+it The imposition of hands is the Ordering of Clerks, Which is done by prayer of the voice, and imposition of the hand\+it*. And this is in some inferior orders also, but Paul and Barnabas were ordered to a higher function than inferior Priests, even to be Bishops throughout all Nations. \fq ¹ \ft Imposition of hands. \fq ² \ft Holy Orders. \fq ³ \ft \+it Act. 6, 6.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it * Act. 2, 42.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Hiero. in c. 58. Esa.\+it*\ef*
\v 4 And they being sent of the Holy Ghost, went to Seleucia, and thence sailed to Cypres. \ef - \fr 13.4 \fq Sent of the Holy Ghost. \ft ¹ Whosoever be sent by the Church, are sent of the Holy Ghost, though in such an extraordinary sort it be not done. Whereby we see how far the Officers of our souls in the Church do pass the temporal Magistrates, who though they be of God's ordinance, yet not of the Holy Ghost's special calling. \fq ¹ \ft Spiritual officers of our souls.\ef*
\v 5 And when they were come to Salamina, they preached the word of God in the Synagogues of the Jews. And they had John also in their ministry.
\v 6 And when they had walked throughout the whole island as far as Paphos, they found a certain man that was a magician, a false-prophet, a Jew, whose name was \f 1 \fr 13.6 \fq Bar-iesu \ft \+it Barieu\+it*\f* Bar-iesu,
\v 7 who was with the Proconsul Sergius Paulus a wise man. He sending for Barnabas & Saul, desired to hear the word of God.
\v 8 But Elymas the magician (for so is his name interpreted) resisted them, seeking to avert the Proconsul from the faith.
\v 9 But Saul, otherwise Paul, replenished with the Holy Ghost, looking upon him,
\v 10 said: O full of all guile, and all deceit, son of the devil, enemy of all justice, thou ceasest not to subvert the right ways of our Lord.
\v 11 And now behold the hand of our Lord upon thee, & thou shalt be blind, not seeing the sun until a time. And forthwith there fell dimness and darkness upon him, and going about he sought somebody that would give him his hand.
\v 12 Then the Proconsul, when he had seen that which was done, believed, marveling at the doctrine of our Lord.
\v 13 And when Paul and they that were with him had sailed from Paphos, they came to Perge in Pamphylia. And John departing from them, returned to Jerusalem.
\v 14 But they passing through Perge, came to Antioch in Pisidia: and entering into the Synagogue on the day of the Sabboth, they sat down.
\v 15 And after the lesson of the Law and the Prophets, the princes of the Synagogue sent to them, saying: Men brethren, if there be among you any sermon of exhortation to the people, speak.
\v 16 And Paul rising up, and with his hand beckoning for silence, said: Ye men of Israel, and you that fear God, harken.
\v 17 The God of the people of Israel chose our fathers, and exalted the people when they were sojourners in the land of Ægypt, and in a mighty arm brought them out thereof, \f 1 \fr 13.17 \ft \+it Exod.\+it*\f*
\v 18 and for the space of forty years tolerated their manners in the desert. \f 1 \fr 13.18 \ft \+it Iosue.\+it*\f*
\v 19 And destroying seven nations in the land of Chanaan, by lot he divided their land among them,
\v 20 as it were after four hundred and fifty years, and after these things he gave Judges, until Samuel the Prophet.
\v 21 And thenceforth they desired a king: & he gave them \x - \xt Iud. 1. Reg. 8.\x* Saul the son of Cis, a man of the tribe of Benjamin, forty years.
\v 22 and removing him, he raised them up \x - \xt 1. Reg. 16.\x* David to be king: to whom giving testimony, he said: \x - \xt Ps. 88, 21.\x* \it I have found David the son of Jesse, a man according to my heart, who shall do all my wills.\it*
\v 23 Of his seed God according to his \x - \xt Psa. 131, 11.\x* promise hath brought forth to Israel a Saviour \sc Jesus\sc*,
\v 24 John \x - \xt Lu. 3, 3.\x* preaching before the face of his coming, baptism of penance to all the people of Israel.
\v 25 And when John fulfilled his course, he said: Whom do \x - \xt Lu. 3, 15.\x* you think me to be? I am not he: but behold there cometh after me, whose shoes of his feet I am not worthy to loose.
\v 26 \f 1 \fr 13.26 \ft The Epistle upon Tuesday in Easter week.\f* Men brethren, children of the stock of Abraham, & they among you that fear God; to you the word of this salvation was sent.
\v 27 For they that inhabited Jerusalem, and the princes thereof, not knowing him, nor the voices of the prophets that are read every Sabboth, judging have fulfilled them,
\v 28 and finding no cause of death in him, \x - \xt Lu. 23, 1.\x* desired of Pilate, that they might kill him.
\v 29 And when they had consummated all things that were written of him, taking him down from the tree, they put him in a monument.
\v 30 But God raised him up from the dead the third day:
\v 31 who was \x - \xt Act. 1, 3.\x* seen for many days of them that came up together with him from Galilee into Jerusalem, who until this present are his witnesses to the people.
\v 32 And we preach unto you that promise which was made to our fathers:
\v 33 that God hath fulfilled this same \x - \xq to our children \xt Ps. 2, 7.\x* to our children, raising up \sc Jesus\sc*, as in the second Psalm also it is written: \f 2 \fr 13.33 \ft \+it to us their children,\+it*\f* \it My Son art thou, this day have I begotten thee.\it*
\v 34 And that he raised him up from the dead, not to return now any more into corruption, thus he said: \x - \xt Esa. 55, 3.\x* \it That I will give you the holy things of David faithful.\it*
\v 35 And therefore in another place also he saith: \x - \xt Ps. 15, 10.\x* \it Thou shalt not give thy holy one to see corruption.\it*
\v 36 For David in his generation when he had served, according to the will of God slept: and he was laid to his fathers & saw corruption.
\v 37 But he whom God hath raised up, saw no corruption.
\v 38 Be it known therefore to you, men brethren, that through him, forgiveness of sins is preached to you, from all the things from the which you could not be justified by the law of Moyses.
\v 39 In him every one that believeth, is justified.
\v 40 Take heed therefore lest that come upon you which is spoken in the prophets,
\v 41 \x - \xt Abac. 1, 5.\x* \it See ye contemners, and wonder, and perish: because I work a work in your days, a work which you will not believe, if any man shall tell it you.\it*
\v 42 And they going forth, \f 1 \fr 13.42 \ft \+it c the Gentiles desired\+it*\f* they desired them that the Sabboth following they would speak unto them these words.
\v 43 And when the Synagogue was dismissed, many of the Jews, and of the \f 1 \fr 13.43 \ft \+it c devout proselytes,\+it*\f* strangers serving God, followed Paul & Barnabas: who speaking exhorted them to continue in the grace of God.
\v 44 But the next Sabboth the whole city almost assembled to hear the word of God.
\v 45 And the Jews seeing the multitudes, were replenished with envy, & contradicted those things which were said of Paul, blaspheming.
\v 46 Then Paul and Barnabas constantly said: To you it behoved us first to speak the word of God: but because \f 1 \fr 13.46 \ft The Jews of their own free will repelling the truth, are unworthy of Christ and worthily forsaken: and the Gentiles though they believed specially by God's grace and preordination, yet they believe also by their own free will, which standeth well with God's providence.\f* you repel it, and judge yourselves unworthy of eternal life; behold we turn to the Gentiles.
\v 47 For so our Lord commanded us: \x - \xt Esa. 49, 6.\x* \it I have put thee to be the light of the Gentiles; that thou mayest be salvation unto the utmost of the earth.\it*
\v 48 And the Gentiles hearing it, were glad, and glorified the word of our Lord: and there believed as many as were preordinate to life everlasting.
\v 49 And the word of our Lord was spread throughout the whole country.
\v 50 But the Jews stirred up religious and honest women, and the chief of the city, and raised persecution against Paul and Barnabas: and they did cast them forth out of their coasts.
\v 51 But they \x - \xt Lu. 9, 5.\x* shaking off the dust of their feet against them, came to I conium.
\v 52 The Disciples also were replenished with joy and with the Holy Ghost.
\c 14
\cd Next in Iconium they preach, where many being converted of both sorts, the obstinate Jews raise persecution. 6. Then in the towns of Lycaonia, where the Heathen first seeing that Paul had healed one born lame, are hardly persuaded but they are Gods. 18. but afterward, by the instigation of the malicious Jews, they stone Paul, leaving him for dead. 20. And so having done their circuit, they return the same way confirming the Christians, and making Priests for every Church. 25. And being come home to Antioch in Syria, they report all to the Church there.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* it came to pass at I conium that they entered together into the Synagogue of the Jews, and so spake, that a very great multitude of Jews and of the Greeks did believe.
\v 2 But the Jews that were incredulous, stirred up and incensed the hearts of the Gentiles to anger against the brethren.
\v 3 Along time therefore they abode, dealing confidently in our Lord, who gave testimony to the word of his grace, granting signs and wonders to be done by their hands.
\v 4 And the multitude of the city was divided: and certain of them indeed were with the Jews, but certain with the Apostles.
\v 5 And when the Gentiles and the Jews with their princes had made an assault, to use them contumeliously, and to stone them,
\v 6 understanding it, they fled to the cities of Lycaonia, Lystra and Derbe, and the whole country about, and there they were evangelizing.
\v 7 And a certain man at Lystra impotent of his feet sat there, lame from his mother's womb, that never had walked.
\v 8 This same heard Paul speaking. Who looking upon him, and seeing that he had faith for to be saved,
\v 9 he said with a loud voice: Stand up right on thy feet. And he leaped & walked.
\v 10 And the multitudes when they had seen what Paul had done, lifted up their voice in the Lycaonian tongue, saying: Gods made like to men, are descended to us.
\v 11 And they called Barnabas, Jupiter: but Paul Mercury, because he was the chief speaker.
\v 12 The Priest also of \f 1 \fr 14.12 \ft \+it c διὸς τοῦ ὄντος\+it*\f* Jupiter that was before the city, bringing oxen and garlands before the gates, would with the people sacrifice. \ef - \fr 14.12 \fq They would sacrifice. \ft ¹ This lo is the divine worship, consisting in external sacrifice, and in acknowledging the parties worshipped to be gods: which ² may be done to no man nor creature, and therefore the Apostles refuse it with all possible diligence, and all the Angels and Saints in heaven refuse that adoration by sacrifice. The Catholic Church suffereth no Priest nor other so to worship any Saint in heaven or earth. She hath but one external Sacrifice, which is in the holy Mass, of Christ's body and blood: that she offereth to God alone, and \+it neither to Peter nor to Paul\+it* (saith S. Augustine ³ ) \+it though the Priest that sacrificeth, standeth over their bodies, and offereth in their memories\+it*. ⁴ But other kinds of honours and duties, inferior without all comparison (how great so ever they be) to this, we do, as the Scriptures and Nature teach us, to all Superiors in heaven and earth, according to the degrees of grace, honour, and blessedness that God hath called them unto, from our B. Lady Christ's own mother, to the least servant he hath in the world. For which the Heretics would never accuse Christian people of Idolatry, if they had either grace, learning, faith, or natural affection. \fq ¹ \ft Latria. \fq ² \ft \+it Aug. li. 10. de Civ. Dei c. 1.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Aug. li. 8. de Civ. c. 27.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Dulia.\ef*
\v 13 Which thing when the Apostles Barnabas and Paul heard, renting their coats, they leaped forth into the multitudes, crying
\v 14 and saying: Ye men, why do you these things? We also are mortal men like unto you, preaching to you for to convert from these vain things, to the living God that made the Heaven, and the earth, and the sea, and all things that are in them:
\v 15 who in the Generations past suffered all the Gentiles to go their own ways.
\v 16 Howbeit he left not himself \f 1 \fr 14.16 \ft The Heathen might by the daily benefits of god have known him at the least to have been their Creator and only Lord, though the mystery of our Redemption were not opened to them.\f* without testimony, being beneficial from Heaven, giving rains, and fruitful seasons, filling our hearts with food and gladness.
\v 17 And speaking these things, they scarce appeased the multitudes from sacrificing to them.
\v 18 But there came in certain Jews from Antioch and I conium: and persuading the multitudes, and \x - \xt 2. Cor. 11, 25.\x* stoning Paul, they drew him out of the city, thinking him to be dead.
\v 19 But the Disciples compassing him round about, he rising up, entered into the city, and the next day he went forth with Barnabas unto Derbe.
\v 20 And when they had evangelized to that city, & had taught many, they returned to Lystra and I conium, and to Antioch:
\v 21 confirming the hearts of the Disciples, and exhorting them to continue in the faith; and that by many tribulations we must enter into the Kingdom of God.
\v 22 And when \f 1 \fr 14.22 \ft We see by this, first that SS. Paul & Barnabas were Bishops, having here authority to give Holy Orders: secondly, that there was even then a difference betwixt Bishops and Priests, though the name in the primitive Church was often used indifferently: lastly, that always fasting & praying were preparatives to the giving of Holy Orders.\f* they had ordained to them Priests in every Church, and had prayed with fastings, they commended them to our Lord in whom they believed. \ef - \fr 14.22 \fq Had ordained. \ft The Heretics, to make the world believe that all Priests ought to be chosen by the voices of the people, and that they need no other Ordering or Consecration by Bishops, pressing the profane use of the ¹ Greek word more than the very natural signification requireth and Ecclesiastical use beareth, translate thus, \+it Ordained by election\+it* ². Whereas indeed this word in Scripture signifieth, Ordering by imposition of hands, as is plain by other words equivalent, \+it Act. 6, 13. 1. Tim. 4. 5. 2. Tim. 1\+it*. Where the Ordering of Deacons, Priests, and others is called ³ Imposition of hands: not of the people, but of the Apostles. And this to be the Ecclesiastical use of the word, appeareth by S. Hierom ⁴ saying (as is before alleged) that \+it χειροτονία is the Ordering of Clerks or Clergy men by prayer of voice and imposition of hand.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft \+it χειροτονήσαντες\+it* \fq ² \ft Heretical translation against Holy Orders. \fq ³ \ft \+it ἐπίθεσις τῶν χειρῶν.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Hiero. in 38. Esa.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 14.22 \fq Priests. \ft Even so here also, as before, fleeing from the proper, apt, & known word, which is most precisely correspondent to the very Greek in our tongue and all nations, ¹ they translate for \+it Priest\+it*, Elder, that is, for a calling of Office, a word of age: for a term of art and by consent of all the Church and Apostolic authority and Fathers, appropriated to Holy Order, a vulgar, common, and profane term: With all little grace as if they should translate \+it Pontificem\+it*, a bridgemaker, the \+it Maior\+it* of London, the Bigger of London. And thus you see within three words' compass they flee guilefully from the Latin to the Greek, and again guilefully from the Greek to the vulgar English. Such corruption of Scriptures their hatred of Priesthood driveth them unto. If they had translated it so when the Scriptures were first written, (at which time the word was but newly received into the special and Ecclesiastical signification, and when it was yet taken sometimes in common profane sort, as \+it 1. Tim. 5.\+it* or there only where our ancient Latin version turneth \+it Presbyter\+it* into \+it Senior\+it*, because the word was not yet wholly and only appropriated to Holy Orders, as afterward by use of many hundred years it was and is) their dealing might have had some colour of honesty and plainness, which now cannot be but of plain falsehood and corruption, and that of further purpose than the simple can see. Which is to take away the office of Sacrificing and other functions of Priests, proper in the new Testament to such as the Apostles often, and the posterity in manner altogether call Priests, \+it Presbyteros\+it*. Which word doth so certainly imply the authority of sacrificing, that it is by use made also the only English of \+it Sacerdos\+it*, the Adversaries themselves as well as we, so translating it in all the old and new Testament: ² though they cannot be ignorant that \+it Priest\+it* cometh of \+it Presbyter\+it*, and not of \+it Sacerdos\+it*: and that antiquity for no other cause applied the signification of \+it Presbyter\+it* to \+it Sacerdos\+it*, but to shew that \+it Presbyter\+it* is in the new Law, that which \+it Sacerdos\+it* was in the old: the Apostles abstaining from this and other like old names at the first, and rather using the words, Bishops, Pastors, and Priests, because they might be distinguished from the Governors and sacrificers of Aaron's order, who as yet in the Apostles' time did their old functions still in the Temple. And this to be true, and that to be a Priest, is to be a man appointed to sacrifice, the Heretics themselves calling \+it Sacerdos\+it* always a Priest, must needs be driven to confess. Although their folly is therein notorious, to apply willingly the word \+it Priest\+it* to \+it Sacerdos\+it*, and to take it from \+it Presbyter\+it* whereof it is properly derived ³, not only in English, but in other languages both French and Italian. Which is to take away the name that the Apostles and fathers gave to the Priests of the Church, & to give it wholly & only to the order of Aaron, which never had it before our Priesthood began. Never did there Heretics stand so much upon doubtful derivations and descant of words as these Protestants do, and yet never men behaved themselves more fondly in the same: as whosoever marketh the distinction of their Elders, Ministers, Deacons, and such like, shall perceive. \fq ¹ \ft Heretical translation against Priesthood. \fq ² \ft If \+it Sacerdos\+it* be Priest, much more \+it Presbyter\+it*. \fq ³ \ft Presbyter. Priest. Prebstre. Preti.\ef*
\v 23 And passing through Pisidia, they came into Pamphylia,
\v 24 and speaking the word of our Lord in Perge, they went down into Attalia:
\v 25 and from thence they sailed to Antioch, \x - \xt Act, 13, 2.\x* whence they had been delivered to the grace of God unto the work which they accomplished.
\v 26 And when they were come, and had assembled the Church, they reported what great things God had done with them, & that he had opened a door of faith to the Gentiles.
\v 27 And they abode no little time with the Disciples.
\c 15
\cd Some of those Jews also that were Christians, do fall, and are authors of the Heresy of Judaizing. 2. They refer the matter to Councel: 7. Wherein after great disputation, Peter striking the stroke, 12. and other confirming his sentence with miracles, 13. and with Scriptures: 22. the Apostles and Priests do write and command in the name of the Holy Ghost what is to be done. 30. And the faithful thereby are straightways quieted in mind. 36. After which, Paul and Barnabas thinking to go again their above said circuit together, are by occasion of Mark parted, to the greater increase of the Church.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* certain coming down from Jewry, taught the brethren: That \x - \xt Gal. 5, 2.\x* unless you be circumcised according to the manner of Moyses, you cannot be saved.
\v 2 No little sedition therefore being risen to Paul and Barnabas against them, they appointed that Paul and Barnabas should go up, & certain others of \f 1 \fr 15.2 \fq the rest \ft \+it them,\+it*\f* the rest, to the Apostles and \f 2 \fr 15.2 \ft \+it c ρεσβυτέρους.\+it*\f* Priests unto Jerusalem, upon this question. \ef - \fr 15.2 \fq Appointed. \ft We learn by this example, what is to be done when any controversy ariseth in religion between the teachers or other Christian people. We see it is not enough to contend by allegations of Scriptures or other proofs seeming to make for either part: for so of contentious part-taking there should be no end, but the more writing, wrestling, striving there were, every one for his own fancy, cloaking it with the title of God's word and Scripture, the more Schisms, Sects, and divisions would fall: as we see specially in the restless Heresies of our time. Whose fautors admitting no judges, stand to no trial of mortal men, to no tribunal of Pope, Councels, Bishops, Synods, but each man to his own phantastical spirit, his own sense of Scriptures, and his own willful obdurate rebellion against God's Church and his Superiors in the same. ¹ But here we see S. Paul and Barnabas, men that were Apostles and full of the Spirit of God, and the other parties, though never so much partial to the ceremonies of their Law by their former long use and education therein, yet not to stand stiffly to their own opinion on either side, but to condescend to refer the whole controversy and the determination thereof to the Apostles, Priests or Ancients of Hierusalem, that is to say, to commit the matter to be tried by the Heads and Bishops and their determination in Councel. This is God's holy and wise providence among other judgements in his Church, to keep the Christian people in truth and unity, and to condemn sects and false teachers and troublers of the Church. By which judgements and order, whosoever will not or dare not be tried in all their doctrine and doings, they shew themselves to mistrust their own cause, and to flee from the light, and ordinance of God. Without which order of appeasing all differences in faith and constructions of the Scriptures, the Church had been more defectual and insufficient, than any Commonwealth or Society of men in the world: none of which ever wanteth good means to decide all discords and dissension arising among the subjects & citizens of the same. \fq ¹ \ft The way to end dissension in religion, is to commit it to a Councel.\ef*
\v 3 They therefore being brought on their way by the Church, passed through Phoenice & Samaria, reporting the conversion of the Gentiles: and they made great joy to all the brethren.
\v 4 And when they were come to Jerusalem, they were received of the Church and of the Apostles and \f 1 \fr 15.4 \ft c Ancients here, & often in this chapter, are the same that Priests vers. 2. as S. Hierom taketh it also \+it 1. Pet. 5.\+it* &\f*\f 2 \fr 15.4 \ft \+it c πρεσβυτέρων\+it*\f* Ancients, declaring whatsoever God had done with them.
\v 5 And there arose certain of the heresy of the Pharisees that believed, saying, That they must be circumcised; commanded also to keep the law of Moyses.
\v 6 And the Apostles and Ancients assembled to consider of this word. \ef - \fr 15.6 \fq Apostles and Ancients. \ft The Heresies of our Protestants which would have all men to give voice, or to be present in Councels, and of others that would have none but the holy or elect to be admitted, are refuted by this example, where we see none but Apostles & Priests or Ancients assembled to dispute of the matter, though many devout people were in the city the same time. ¹ Neither did ever any other in the Ancient Councels of the Church assemble to debate and define the matter, but such, though many other for other causes be ever present. Secular men or women, be their gifts never so great, cannot be judges in causes of faith and religion. ² \+it If any thing\+it*, saith God \+it be hard and doubtful, thou shalt come to the Priests of the Levitical stock, and thou shalt follow their sentence\+it*. Again, ³ \+it The lips of the Priest shall keep knowledge, and the Law thou shalt require of his mouth\+it*. Again, ⁴ \+it Ask the Law of the Priest\+it*. Much more must we refer all to our Bishops and Pastors, whom God hath placed in the regiment of the Church with much larger privilege, than ever he did the old Priests over the Synagogue. To whom it is said, ⁵ \+it He that despiseth you, despiseth me\+it*. ⁶ And it is to be noted that the Bishops so gathered in Councel, represent the whole Church, have the authority of the whole Church, and the Spirit of God to protect them from error, as the whole Church: \+sc Ss\+sc*. Paul and Barnabas come hither for the definition of the whole Church. \+it The sentence of a plenary or general Councel\+it* (saith S. Augustine ⁷ ) \+it is the consent of the whole Church\+it*. And so it must needs be in the Church, because the Magistrates, Senate, Councel or deputies of all commonwealths, represent the whole body: and to have it otherwise (as the Church's Rebels wish) were to bring all to hell and horror, and themselves to be perpetually, by the seditious and popular persons, upholden against Law, reason, and religion, in their wickedness. \fq ¹ \ft Of what persons a Councel consisteth. \fq ² \ft \+it Deut. 17.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Mal. 2, 7.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Agga. 2, 12.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Luc. 10, 11.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft A general Councel representeth the whole Church. \fq ⁷ \ft \+it li. 1. c. 18. de bapt.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 15.6 \fq Assembled. \ft ¹ A Councel was called to discuss the matter. Which Councel was the more easily gathered, because the Christian Bishops and countries were not yet so many, but that the principal Governors of the Church being not far dispersed, and as many learned men as were necessary, might be in Hierusalem, or easily called thither. And it was not a Provincial Councel or Synod only, but a general Councel, consisting of the chief Apostles and Bishops that then were, though the number was nothing so great as afterward used to assemble, when the Church was spread into all nations. \fq ¹ \ft The first Councel at Hierusalem.\ef*
\v 7 And when there was made a \x - \xt Act. 10, 20.\x* great disputation, Peter rising up said to them: Men brethren, you know that \f 2 \fr 15.7 \ft b See the Annot. vers. 28. toward the end.\f* of old days God among us chose, that by my mouth the Gentiles should hear the word of the Gospel, and believe. \ef - \fr 15.7 \fq Peter rising up. \ft ¹ S. Peter as the head of the Church speaketh first, as his successors have ever had, not only in their personal presence, but in their absence by their legates and substitutes, the chief voice in all Councel's general, none ever received into authority and credit in the Church without their Confirmation. And therefore the Councels of the Arians and of other Heretics, were they never so great, wanting the Pope's assent, assistance, or Confirmation, did shamefully err, as Ariminense for the Arians, and Ephesinum secundum for the Nestorians, and such like condemned Assemblies. \fq ¹ \ft Peter head of the Councel, & his successors after him.\ef* \ef - \fr 15.7 \fq Chose that by my mouth. \ft ¹ Though Paul were called and appointed specially to be the Apostle of the Gentiles, yet that was S. Peter's special privilege by God's own choice, that the first Gentiles should be called by his mouth, and that he first should utter to the Church that truth of the admission of the Gentiles himself, for that he was Christ's Vicar, being notwithstanding (as his Master was) \+it Minister Circumcisionis\+it* ², that is, Apostle of the Jews, Christ deferring all preeminence unto him in that point also. \fq ¹ \ft Peter's preeminence both toward Jews & Gentiles. \fq ² \ft \+it Ro. 15, 8. Gal. 2, 7.\+it*\ef*
\v 8 And God which knoweth the hearts, gave testimony, \x - \xt Act. 10, 45.\x* giving unto them the Holy Ghost as well as to us,
\v 9 and hath put no difference between us and them, \f 1 \fr 15.9 \ft By that faith which worketh by charity, for a dead faith cannot purify the heart of man. See chap. \+it 16, 31.\+it*\f* by faith purifying their hearts.
\v 10 Now therefore why tempt you God, to put a yoke upon the necks of the Disciples, which neither our fathers nor we have been able to bear?
\v 11 But by the grace of our Lord \sc Jesus Christ\sc* we believe to be saved, in like manner as they also.
\v 12 And all the multitude held their peace: and they heard Barnabas and Paul telling what great signs and wonders God had done among the Gentiles by them.
\v 13 And after they held their peace, James answered, saying: Men brethren, hear me. \ef - \fr 15.13 \fq James. \ft S. James because he was an Apostle and also Bishop of Hierusalem, gave his sentence next. For the speech interposed of \+sc Ss\+sc*. Paul and Barnabas, was but for their better information in the decision of the matter, and for confirmation of S. Peter's sentence, though they being Apostles, and Bishops, had voices in the Councel also: as many more had, though their sentences be not here reported. ¹ And where S. James in his speech saith, \+it I judge\+it*, it is not meant that he gave the principal definitive sentence: for he (as all the rest) followed and allowed the sentence of S. Peter, as it is plain in the text, the whole assembly for reverence of his person and approbation of his sentence, holding their peace. \+it All the multitude\+it* (saith S. Hierom ² ) \+it held their peace, and into his sentence James the Apostle & all the Priests did pass together\+it*. For though S. James did particularize certain points incident to the question debated, as of eating strangled meats &c. yet the proper controversy for which the Councel assembled, was, ³ Whether the Gentiles converted were bound to observe the Law of Moyses: and it was concluded, that they were not bound, nor ought not to be charged with Moyses Law or the Sacraments and ceremonies of the same. This is the substance and principal purpose of this Councel's decree, which doth bind for ever: and \+it Peter\+it* (saith S. Hierom in the same place) was \+it Prince or author of this decree\+it*. The matter of fornication and Idolothytes being but ⁴ incident to the question or resolution, and the forbidding of eating strangled and blood, but a temporal prohibition, which by the consent of the Church or otherwise afterward was abrogated, the Church of God having the true sense of difference of times, places, & persons, when and how far such things are to be observed, and when not. ⁵ And in such things as these, and in other like which according to circumstances require alteration, it is, that S. Augustine saith, li. 2. de bapt. c. 3. to. 7. \+it The former general or plenary Councels may be amended by the later\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft S. James and the rest follow S. Peter's sentence. \fq ² \ft \+it Hiero. to. 2. ep. 89. ad August. c. 2.\+it* \fq ³ \ft The principal question. \fq ⁴ \ft Incident questions. \fq ⁵ \ft How later Councels alter the former.\ef*
\v 14 Simon hath told how God first visited to take of the Gentiles a people to his name.
\v 15 And to this accord the words of the Prophets, as it is written:
\v 16 \x - \xt Tim. 3, 5.\x* \it After these things I will return, and will re-edify the tabernacle of David which was fallen, and the ruins thereof I will re-edify, and set it up:\it*
\v 17 \it that the residue of men may seek after the Lord, and all nations upon whom my name is invocated, saith the Lord that doeth these things.\it*
\v 18 To our Lord was his own work known from the beginning of the world.
\v 19 For the which cause I judge, that they which of the Gentiles are converted to God, are not to be disquieted,
\v 20 but to write unto them that they refrain themselves from the contaminations of idols, and fornication, and strangled things, and blood.
\v 21 For Moyses of old times hath in every city them that preach him in the Synagogues, where he is read every Sabboth.
\v 22 Then it pleased the Apostles and Ancients with the whole Church, to choose men out of them, & to send to Antioch with Paul and Barnabas, Judas, who was surnamed Barsabas, & Silas, chief men among the Brethren,
\v 23 \f 1 \fr 15.23 \ft c Other Latin copies and the Greek read thus \+it Writing by their hands an epistle containing these things.\+it*\f* writing by their hands. The Apostles and Ancients, the brethren, to the brethren of the Gentiles that are at Antioch and in Syria and Cilicia, greeting.
\v 24 Because we have heard that certain going forth from us have troubled you with words subverting your souls, to whom we gave no commandment: \ef - \fr 15.24 \fq Going forth from us. \ft ¹ A proper description or note of Heretics, Schismatics, and seditious teachers, to go out from their spiritual Pastors and Governors, and to teach without their commission and approbation, to disquiet the Catholic people with multitude of words and sweet speeches, and finally to overthrow their souls. \fq ¹ \ft Going out, a mark of heretics.\ef*
\v 25 It hath pleased us being gathered in one, to choose out men & to send them unto you with our dearest Barnabas and Paul,
\v 26 men that have given their lives for the name of our Lord \sc Jesus Christ\sc*.
\v 27 We have sent therefore Judas and Silas who themselves also will in words report unto you the same things.
\v 28 For it hath seemed good to the Holy Ghost and to us, to lay no further burden upon you than these necessary things: \ef - \fr 15.28 \fq To the Holy Ghost and to us. \ft ¹ By this first we note, that it is not such a fault as the Heretics would make it in the sight of the simple, or any incongruity at all, to join God and his creatures as the principal cause and the secondary, in one speech, and to attribute that to both, which though diversly, yet proceedeth of both. \+it God and you\+it*, say good people commonly: \+it God and our Lady, Christ and S. John: We confess to God an to Peter and Paul.\+it* as, ² \+it God and his Angel, To our Lord and Gedeon, The sword of our Lord and of Gedeon, Our Lord and Moyses, Christ and his Angels, Our Lord and all Saints.\+it* ep. ad Philem. \+it S. Paul and our Lord.\+it* 1. Thess. 1, 6. All these speeches being partly Scriptures, partly like unto the Scripture's speeches, are warranted also by this Councel, which saith boldly, & hath given the form thereof to all other Councels lawfully called and confirmed, to say the like, ³ \+it It hath pleased the Holy Ghost and us.\+it* S. Cyprian \+it ep. 54. nu. 2.\+it* reporting the like of a Synod holden in Afrike, saith, \+it It hath pleased us by the suggestion of the Holy Ghost.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft God & our Lady, and the like speeches. \fq ² \ft \+it * Gen. 48, 15, 16. Iud. 7, 18. 20. Exod. 14, 31. 1. Tim. 5, 21.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it VISUM EST SPIRITUI SANCTO ET NOBIS.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 15.28 \ft Secondly we note, that the holy Councels lawfully kept for determination or clearing of doubts, or condemning of errors and Heresies, or appeasing of Schisms and troubles, or reformation of life, and such like important matters, have ever the assistance of God's Spirit, and therefore cannot err in their sentences and determinations concerning the same, because the Holy Ghost cannot err, from whom (as you see here) jointly with the Councel the resolution proceedeth. ¹ Thirdly we learn, that in the holy Councels specially (though otherwise and in other Tribunals of the Church it be also verified) Christ's promise is fulfilled, ² that the Holy Ghost should suggest them and teach them all truth, and that not in the Apostles' time only, but to the world's end. For so long shall Councels, the Church, and her Pastors have this privilege of God's assistance, as there be either doubts to resolve, or Heretics to condemn, or truths to be opened, or evil men to be reformed, or Schisms to be appeased. For which cause S. Gregory \+it li. 1. ep. 24. sub fin.\+it* reverenceth the four general Councels (Nicen. Constantinop. Ephes. Chalced.) as the four books of the holy Gospel, alluding to the number: and of the fifth also he saith that he doth ³ reverence it alike: and so would he have done more, if they had been before his time, who saith of them thus, \+it whiles they are concluded and made by universal consent, himself doth he destroy, and not them, whosoever presumeth either to loose whom they bind, or to bind whom they loose.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The Holy Ghost's assistance in all lawful Councels, to the world's end, and that by Christ's promise. \fq ² \ft \+it Io. 16, 13.\+it* \fq ³ \ft S. Gregory's reverence of General Councels.\ef* \ef - \fr 15.28 \ft S. Gregory therefore reverencing all five alike, ¹ it may be marveled whence the heretics have their fond difference betwixt those four first and other later: attributing much to them, and nothing to the rest. Whereas indeed the later can err no more than the first four, being holden and approved as they were, and having the Holy Ghost as they had. But in those first also when a man findeth any thing against their Heresies (as there be diverse things) then they say plainly that they also may err, and that the Holy Ghost is not tied to men's voices, nor to the number of sentences: Which is directly to reprove this first Councel also of the Apostles, and Christ's promise of the Holy Ghost's assistance to teach all truth. ² Yea that you may know and abhor these Heretics throughly, hear ye what a principal Sect-Master with his blasphemous mouth or pen uttereth, saying, that ³ \+it In the very best times such was partly the ambition of Bishops, partly their foolishness and ignorance, that the very blind may easily perceive, Satan verily to have been president of their assemblies\+it*. Good Lord deliver the people and the world from such blasphemous tongues and books, and give men grace to attend to the holy Scriptures and Doctors, that they may see how much, ⁴ not only S. Augustine and other Fathers attribute to all general Councels specially, (to which they refer themselves in all doubts among themselves and in all their controversies with Heretics:) but to which even S. Paul himself (so specially taught by God) and others also yielded themselves. Notorious is the saying of S. Augustine concerning S. Cyprian, who being a blessed Catholic Bishop and Martyr, yet erred about the rebaptizing of such as were Christened by Heretics. \+it If he had lived\+it* (saith S. Augustine li. 2. de bapt, c. 4.) \+it to have seen the determination of a plenary Councel, which he saw not in his life time, he would for his great humility and charity straight way have yielded, and preferred the general Councel before his own judgement and his fellow Bishops in a Provincial Councel only\+it*. ⁵ Whereby also we learn, that Provincial Councels may err, though many times they do not, and being conformable to the general Councels, or confirmed and allowed by them or the See Apostolic, their resolutions be infallible as the others are. \fq ¹ \ft The Protestants' fond distinction between the 4 first & the later Councels. \fq ² \ft Beza's blasphemy against the first general Councels. \fq ³ \ft \+it Beza in præf. Test. No. an. 1565.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft What the Fathers attribute to Councels, & namely S. Augustine. \fq ⁵ \ft Provincial Councels.\ef* \ef - \fr 15.28 \ft ¹ If any here ask, what need so much disputing, study, and travail in Councels to find out and determine the truth, if the Holy Ghost infallibly guide them? We answer that such is the ordinary providence of God in this case, to assist them when they do their endeavour, and use all human means of industry, and not else. And so (though somewhat otherwise) God assisted the Evangelists and other writers of the holy Scriptures, that they could not err in penning the same, but yet they did and ought to use all possible human diligence to know and learn out the histories and truth of matters, as is plain in the beginning of S. Luke's Gospel ²: else the Holy Ghost would not have assisted them. Even so in this Councel of the Apostles, though they had the Holy Ghost assistant, yet the text saith, \+it cum magna conquisitio fieret\+it*, when there was great disputation, search and examination of the case, then Peter spake &c. ³ If again it be demanded, what need is there to expect the Councel's determination, if the Pope's or See Apostolic's judgement be infallible and have the assistance of God also, as the Catholics affirm? We answer, that for the Catholic and peaceable obedient children of the Church it is a comfort to have such various means of determination, trial, and declaration of the truth, and that it is necessary for the recovery of Heretics, and for the contentation of the weak, who not always giving over to one man's determination, yet will either yield to the judgement of all the learned men and Bishops of all Nations, or else remain desperate and condemned before God and man for ever. And as I said before, this assistance of the Holy Ghost promised to Peter's See, presupposeth human means of searching out the truth, which the Pope always hath used, & will, & must use in matters of great importance, by calling Councels, even as here you see \+sc Ss\+sc*. Peter and Paul themselves and all the Apostles, though endued with the Holy Ghost, yet thought it notwithstanding necessary for further trial & clearing of truth and maintenance of unity, to keep a Councel. \fq ¹ \ft Notwithstanding the Holy Ghost's assistance, yet human means must be used to search the truth. \fq ² \ft \+it Lu. 1, 3.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Though the See Apostolic itself have the same assistance, yet Councels be also necessary for many causes.\ef* \ef - \fr 15.28 \ft Lastly it is to be noted, that as Christ and the Holy Ghost be present by his promise, to all such assemblies as gather in the obedience & unity of the Church, with full mind to obey whatsoever shall be determined, whereby the assembled though of diverse judgments before, do most peaceably yield to truth, and agree in one uniform determination of the same: so all such as gather out of the Church, without humility or intention to yield one to another, or to any Superior, man or Councel, or what else so ever, but challenge to themselves learning, spirit, and we cannot tell what: ¹ such, how many meetings so ever they make, being destitute of the Holy Ghost the author of truth and concord, are further off and further out, than ever before: as God hath shewed by the success of all Heretical Colloquies, Synods, and Assemblies in Germany, France, Pole, and other places in our days. Read a notable place in S. Cyprian ², that the promise of Christ, that he would be in the middes of two or three gathered in his name, pertaineth not to them that assemble out of the Church. \fq ¹ \ft Heretical or Schismatical assemblies. \fq ² \ft \+it de unit. Ec. nu. 7.\+it*\ef*
\v 29 That you abstain from the things immolated to idols, and blood, and that which is strangled, and fornication, from the which things keeping yourselves, you shall do well. Fare ye well. \ef - \fr 15.29 \fq Fornication. \ft Fornication and contamination with Idols, are of themselves mortal sins, and therefore can never be lawful: yet because the Gentiles by custom were prone to both, and of fornication made very small account, it pleased the Holy Ghost to forbid both specially. ¹ Concerning the other points of abstaining from blood and stifled meats, they were things of their own nature indifferent, in which for a time the Jews were to be borne withal, and the Gentiles to be a little exercised to obedience. By which we may see the great authority of God's Church and Councels, which may command for ever, or for a time, such things as be fit for the state of times and nations, without any express Scriptures at all, and so by commandment make things necessary that were before indifferent. \fq ¹ \ft The Church's authority in making Decrees.\ef*
\v 30 They therefore being dismissed went down to Antioch: and gathering the multitude, delivered the epistle.
\v 31 Which when they had read, they rejoiced upon the consolation: \ef - \fr 15.31 \fq Rejoiced upon the consolation. \ft Straight upon the intelligence of the Councel's determination, not only the Gentiles, but even the Masters of the former troubles and dissension, were at rest, & all took great comfort that the controversy was so ended. ¹ And so should all Christian men do, when they see the sects of our time condemned by the like authority and most grave judgement of the holy Councel of Trent. Against which the Heretics of our time make the like frivolous exceptions and false cavillations, as did the old Heretics heretofore against those Councels that specially condemned their errors. The Pope and Bishops (say the) are a party, and they ought not to be our judges: they are partial and come with prejudicate minds to condemn us, and we accuse them all of Idolatry and other crimes, and we will be tried by God's word only, and we will expound it according to another rule, that is to say, as we list. ² So say they against this Councel, and the like said the Arians against the first Nicene Councel, and all such like against those Councels namely that condemned their heresies. And so say all thieves against their correctors and punishers, and would both say and do more against temporal tribunals, Judges, Justices, and Juries, if they had as much licence and liberty in those matters, as men have now in religion. \fq ¹ \ft All good Christians rest upon the determination of a general Councel. \fq ² \ft All Heretics make exceptions against the Councels that condemn them.\ef*
\v 32 but Judas and Silas, themselves also being prophets, with many words comforted the brethren, and confirmed them.
\v 33 And having spent some time there, they were with peace dismissed of the brethren unto them that had sent them.
\v 34 But it seemed good unto Silas to remain there: and Judas departed alone:
\v 35 and Paul and Barnabas tarried at Antioch, teaching and evangelizing with many others the word of our Lord.
\v 36 And after certain days, Paul said to Barnabas, let us return and \x - \xt Act. 13, 14.\x* visit our brethren in all cities wherein we \f 2 \fr 15.36 \ft Hereof our Catholic Bishops took up the necessary use of often visiting their flocks & cures committed to their charge, for confirmation in faith & virtue, & reformation of manners both of clergy & laity.\f* have preached the word of our Lord, how they do.
\v 37 And Barnabas would have taken with them John also that was surnamed Mark.
\v 38 But Paul desired that he (as who \x - \xt Act. 13, 13.\x* had departed from them out of Pamphylia, & had not gone with them to the work) might not be received.
\v 39 And there rose a dissension, so that they departed one from another, & that, Barnabas indeed taking Mark sailed to Cypres. \ef - \fr 15.39 \fq Dissension \ft Such occasions of differences fall out even among the perfect men often, without any great offence. And this their departing fell out to the great increase of Christians. And therefore it is very ridiculously applied to excuse the disagreeing of the Heretics among themselves in the principal points of religion, namely the Sacrament.\ef*
\v 40 But Paul choosing Silas departed, being delivered of the brethren to the grace of God.
\v 41 And he walked through Syria and Cilicia, confirming the Churches: \x - \xt Luc. 10, 11.\x* commanding them to keep the precepts of the Apostles & the Ancients.
\c 16
\cd Paul having for his part visited the Churches of Syria, Cilicia, and Lycaonia, delivering unto them withal to keep the Decrees of the Councel: 6. beginneth a new journey, over Phrygia, Galatia, Mysia: 8. Yea into Europe also he passeth, admonished by a vision, and cometh into Macedonia, 12. and there he beginneth the Church of the Philippians, working miracles, and suffering persecution.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* he came to Derbe and Lystra. And behold, there was a certain Disciple there named Timothee, the son of \f 1 \fr 16.1 \fq a widow \ft \+it Jew\+it*\f* a widow woman that believed, of a father a Gentile.
\v 2 To this man the brethren that were in Lystra and I conium, gave a good testimony.
\v 3 Him Paul would have to go forth with him: and taking him he circumcised him because of the Jews that were in those places. For they all knew that his father was a Gentile.
\v 4 And when they passed through the cities, they delivered unto them to keep the \f 1 \fr 16.4 \ft Here again they take order that the decrees and articles of faith agreed upon in the Councel of Jerusalem, should be executed & observed, whereby we see both the great authority of Councels, & the diligence that all Prelates ought to have to see the decrees & Canons of the Councels put in execution.\f* decrees that were decreed of the Apostles and Ancients which were at Jerusalem.
\v 5 And the Churches were confirmed in faith, and did abound in number daily.
\v 6 And passing through Phrygia and the country of Galatia, they were \f 1 \fr 16.6 \ft This people had not the Gospel denied unto them altogether, but for a time: because (as Venerable Bede thinketh) God foresaw they would not believe, & so should have been more grievously damned.\f* forbidden by the Holy Ghost to preach the word in Asia.
\v 7 And when they were come into Mysia, they attempted to go into Bithynia, and the Spirit of \sc Jesus\sc* permitted them not.
\v 8 And when they had passed through Mysia, they went down to Troas:
\v 9 and a vision by night was shewed to Paul. There was a certain man of Macedonia standing and beseeching him, and saying: Pass into Macedonia, and help us.
\v 10 And as soon as he had seen the vision, forthwith we sought to go into Macedonia, being assured that God had called us to evangelize to them.
\v 11 And sailing from Troas, we came with a straight course to Samothracia, and the day following to Neapolis:
\v 12 and from thence to Philippi, which is the first city of the part of Macedonia, a \f 1 \fr 16.12 \ft \+it Colonia,\+it* is such as city where the most inhabitants are strangers, sent thither from other great cities & States, namely from the Romans.\f* \it colonia.\it* And we were in this city certain days, abiding.
\v 13 And upon the day of the Sabboths, we went forth without the gate beside a river, where it seemed that there was prayer: & sitting we spake to the women that were assembled.
\v 14 And a certain woman named Lydia, a seller of purple of the city of the Thyatirians, one that worshipped God, did hear: whose heart our Lord opened to attend to those things which were said of Paul.
\v 15 And when she was baptized, & her house, she besought us, saying: If you have judged me to be faithful to our Lord, enter into my house & tarry. And she constrained us.
\v 16 And it came to pass as we went to prayer, a certain wench having a Pythonical spirit, met us, that brought great gain to her masters by divining.
\v 17 This same following Paul & us, cried saying: \f 1 \fr 16.17 \ft Either the Devil was compelled by the virtue of Paul's presence to say truth, or else (as such do often times) he spake truth now, that they might the more trust him, and he better beguile them at other times.\f* These men are the servants of the high God, which preach unto you the way of salvation.
\v 18 And this she did many days. And Paul being sorry, and turning, said to the spirit: I command thee in the name of \sc Jesus Christ\sc* to go out from her. And he went out the same hour.
\v 19 But her masters seeing that the hope of their gain was gone, apprehending Paul and Silas, brought them into the market place to the Princes:
\v 20 and presenting them to the magistrates, they said: These men trouble our city, being Jews:
\v 21 and they preach a fashion which it is not lawful for us to receive, nor do, being Romans.
\v 22 And the people ran against them: and the magistrates tearing their coats, commanded them to be beaten with rods.
\v 23 And when they had laid \x - \xt 2. Cor. 11.\x* many stripes upon them, they did cast them into prison, commanding the keeper that he should keep them diligently.
\v 24 Who when he had received such commandment, cast them into the inner prison, and made their feet fast in the stocks.
\v 25 And at mid-night Paul and Silas praying, did praise God. And they that were in prison, heard them.
\v 26 But suddenly there was made a great earthquake, so that the foundations of the prison were shaken. And forthwith all the doors were opened: and the bands of all were loosed.
\v 27 And the keeper of the prison waked out of his sleep, and seeing the doors of the prison opened, drawing out his sword, would have killed himself, supposing that the prisoners had been fled.
\v 28 But Paul cried with a loud voice, saying: Do thyself no harm, for we are all here.
\v 29 And calling for light, he went in, and trembling fell down to Paul and Silas at their feet:
\v 30 and bringing them forth, he said: Masters, what must I do that I may be saved?
\v 31 But they said: \f 1 \fr 16.31 \ft It is no other faith that saveth but that which worketh by Charity. Aug. Enchirid. c. 67.\f* Believe in our Lord \sc Jesus\sc*; and thou shalt be saved and thy house.
\v 32 And they preached the word of our Lord to him with all that were in his house.
\v 33 And he taking them in the same hour of the night, \f 1 \fr 16.33 \ft c Happy Jailers that do mercy toward their godly prisoners, and receive again by them such spiritual benefits.\f* washed their wounds: and himself was baptized and all his house incontinent.
\v 34 And when he had brought them into his own house, he laid the table for them, and rejoiced with all his house, believing God.
\v 35 And when day was come, the magistrates sent the sergeants, saying: Let those men go.
\v 36 And the keeper of the prison told these words to Paul, That the magistrates have sent that you should be let go. Now therefore departing, go ye in peace.
\v 37 But Paul said to them: Being whipped openly, uncondemned, men that are Romans, they have cast us into prison: & now do they send us out secretly? Not so, but let them come, & let us out themselves.
\v 38 And the sergeants reported these words to the magistrates. And they were afraid hearing that they were Romans:
\v 39 and coming they besought them, & bringing them forth they desired them to depart out of the city.
\v 40 And going out of the prison, they entered in unto Lydia: and having seen the brethren, they comforted them, and departed.
\c 17
\cd How in other parts of Macedonia he planted the Church, and namely at Thessalonica, 5. where the obstinate Jews are so malicious, that they pursue him also into Beroea. 14. From whence being conducted into Greece, he preacheth at Athens both to the Jews and Gentiles, disputing with the Philosophers, 19. and in Areopagus, persuading them from their Idols unto one God and JESUS CHRIST raised from the dead.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* when they had walked through Amphipolis and Apollonia, they came to Thessalonica, where there was a Synagogue of the Jews.
\v 2 And Paul according to his custom entered in unto them, & three Sabboths he discoursed to them out of the Scriptures,
\v 3 declaring and insinuating that it behoved \sc Christ\sc* to suffer and to rise again from the dead: & that this is \sc Jesus Christ\sc*, whom I preach to you.
\v 4 And certain of them believed, and were joined to Paul and Silas, and of the Gentiles that served God a great multitude, and noble women not a few.
\v 5 But the Jews \f 1 \fr 17.5 \ft \+it Zelantes.\+it* This is the zeal of Heretics, and a lively pattern of their dealing at this day against Catholic Priests and preachers and the good \+it Jasons\+it* that receive them.\f* envying, & taking unto them of the rascal sort certain naughty men, and making a tumult, stirred the city: and besetting Jason's house, sought to bring them forth unto the people.
\v 6 And not finding them, they drew Jason and certain brethren to the princes of the city, crying, That these are they that stir up the world, and are come hither,
\v 7 whom Jason hath received, and all these do against the decrees of Cæsar, saying that there is another King, \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 8 And they moved the people, and the princes of the city hearing these things.
\v 9 And taking a satisfaction of Jason and of the rest, they dismissed them.
\v 10 But the brethren forthwith by night sent away Paul and Silas unto Beroea. Who when they were come, entered into the Synagogue of the Jews.
\v 11 (And these were more noble than they that are at Thessalonica, who received the word with all greediness, daily searching the scriptures, if these things were so. \ef - \fr 17.11 \fq Searching the Scriptures. \ft ¹ The Heretics use this place to prove that the hearers, must try and judge by the Scriptures, whether their teacher's and preacher's doctrine be true, and so reject that that they find not in the Scriptures, as though here the sheep were made judges of their Pastors, the people of the Priests, and men and women of all sorts, even of S. Paul's doctrine itself: which were the most foolish disorder in the world. And they did not therefore read the Scriptures of the old Testament (for none of the new were yet extant commonly) to dispute with the Apostle, or to try and judge of his doctrine, or whether they should believe him or no: for they were bound to believe him and obey his word, whether he alleged Scripture or no, and whether they could read or understand the Scriptures or no. But it was a great comfort and confirmation for the Jews that had the Scriptures, to find even as S. Paul said, that Christ was God, crucified, risen, and ascended to heaven: which by his preaching and expounding they understood, and never before, though they read them, and heard them read every Sabboth. ² As it is a great comfort to a Catholic man, to hear the Scriptures declared & alleged most evidently for the Church's truth against Heretics, in Sermons or otherwise. And it doth the Catholics good & much confirmeth them, to view diligently the places alleged by the Catholic preachers. Yet they must not be judges for all that, over their own Pastors, whom Christ commandeth them to hear and obey, and by whom they hear the true sense of Scriptures. \fq ¹ \ft The people may not judge of the sense of Scriptures. \fq ² \ft The comfort of Christian men by hearing or reading the Scriptures.\ef*
\v 12 And many surely of them believed, and of honest women Gentiles, and men not a few.)
\v 13 And when the Jews in Thessalonica understood, that at Beroea also the word of God was preached by Paul, they came thither also, moving and troubling the multitude.
\v 14 And then immediately the brethren sent away Paul, to go unto the sea: but Silas and Timothee remained there.
\v 15 And they that conducted Paul, brought him as far as Athens, and receiving commandment of him to Silas and Timothee, that they should come to him very speedily, they departed.
\v 16 And when Paul expected them at Athens, his spirit was incensed within him, seeing the city given to idolatry.
\v 17 He disputed therefore in the Synagogue with the Jews, & them that served God, and in the market-place, every day with them that were there.
\v 18 And certain Philosophers of the Epicures and the Stoics disputed with him, and certain said, what is it that this \f 1 \fr 17.18 \ft \+it b σπερμολόγος.\+it*\f* word-sower would say? But others: He seemeth to be a preacher of new \f 2 \fr 17.18 \ft \+it dæmoniorum.\+it*\f* gods: because he preached to them \sc Jesus\sc* and the resurrection.
\v 19 And apprehending him, they led him to Areopagus, saying: May we know what this new doctrine is that thou speakest of?
\v 20 for thou bringest in certain new things to our ears. We will know therefore what these things may mean.
\v 21 (And all the Athenians, and the strangers sojourning there, employed themselves to nothing else but either to speak, or to hear some news.)
\v 22 \f 1 \fr 17.22 \ft The Epistle for S. Dionysius Areopagita. Octob. 9.\f* But Paul standing in the midst of Areopagus, said: Ye men of Athens, in all things I perceive you as it were superstitious. \ef - \fr 17.22 \fq Superstitious. \ft ¹ S. Paul calleth not them superstitious for adoring the true and only God with much devotion or many ceremonies or in comely prescribed order, or for doing due reverence to holy Sacraments, to Saints and their memories, Images, or Monuments: or for keeping the prescribed laws, days, and fasts of the Church, or for fulfilling vows made to God, or for blessing with the sign of the Cross, or for capping and kneeling at the name of \+sc Jesus\+sc*, or for religiously using creatures sanctified in the same name, or any other Christian observation, for which our new Masters condemn the Catholic people of Superstition: themselves wholly void of that vice by all wise men's judgement, because they have in a manner taken away all religion, and are become Epicureans and Atheists: who are never troubled with superstition, because it is a vice consisting in excess of worship or religion, whereof they are void. ² But the Apostle calleth them superstitious for worshipping the Idols and gods of the Heathen, and ³ for the fear that they had, lest they should leave out any God that was unknown to them: for thus their Altar was inscribed: \+it Dijs Asiæ, Europæ, & Libyæ, Deo ignoto & peregrino.\+it* that is, To the gods of Asia, Europe, and Libya: to the unknown and strange God. This superstition (saith S. Augustine ⁴ ) is wholly taken away from the Church by Christ's incarnation, and by the Apostles' preaching, and by Martyrs' holy life and death. ⁵ Neither doth the Catholic Church allow this or any other kind of superstitious observation. Only we must take heed that we believe not her Adversaries' definition of superstition, for they would imply therein all true religion. \fq ¹ \ft The protestants call devotion, superstition. \fq ² \ft The Apostle speaketh of the Heathens' superstition. \fq ³ \ft \+it * δεισιδαίμονες\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Aug. de cons. Evang. li. 1. c. 26.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft The Catholic Church alloweth no superstition.\ef*
\v 23 For passing by and seeing your \f 1 \fr 17.23 \ft c The Adversaries (in the new Test. 1580.) translate, \+it your devotions,\+it* most corruptly against the nature of the Greek word \+it (2. Thes. 2, 4.)\+it* and most wickedly, against the laudable devotion of good Christians, calling the Pagans idolatry and superstition, their devotions.\f*\f 2 \fr 17.23 \ft \+it c τὰ σεβάσματα\+it*\f* idols, I found an altar also where upon was written: To the unknown God. That therefore which you worship, not knowing it, the same do I preach to you.
\v 24 The God that made the world and all things that are in it, he being Lord of Heaven & earth dwelleth \x - \xt Act. 7, 48.\x* not in \f 2 \fr 17.24 \ft God is not concluded in Temples, nor needeth them for his necessity of dwelling, or other uses of indigence. See Annot. c. 7. Act. v. 48.\f* temples made with hand,
\v 25 neither is he served with men's hands, needing any thing, whereas himself giveth life unto all, and breathing, and all things:
\v 26 and he made of one, all mankind, to inhabit upon the whole face of the earth, assigning set times, and the limits of their habitation,
\v 27 for to seek God, if happily they may feel or find him; although he be not far from every one of us:
\v 28 For in him we live and move and be, as certain also of your own poets said, \f 1 \fr 17.28 \ft \+it Aratus.\+it*\f* \it For of his kind also we are.\it*
\v 29 Being therefore of God's kind, we may not suppose, the Divinity to be like unto gold or silver, or stone, the graving of art and device of man. \ef - \fr 17.29 \fq The Divinity to be like. \ft Nothing can be made by man's hand of what form or sort so ever, that is like to God's essence, or to the form or shape of his Godhead or Divinity. Therefore howsoever the Heathens did paint or grave their Idols, they were nothing like to God. And this also is impertinently alleged by Heretics against the Church's images: which are not made, either to be adored with godly honour, or to be any resemblance of the Divinity or any of the three persons in Godhead, but only of Christ as he was in form of man, who in that respect may be truly expressed, as other men by their portraits: and of the Holy Ghost, not as he is in himself, but as he appeared in fiery tongues or in the similitude of a dove, or such like. ¹ And so to paint or grave any of the three persons as they appeared visibly and corporally, is no more inconvenient or unlawful, than it was undecent for them to appear in such forms. And therefore to paint or portrait the Father also being the first person, as he hath shewed himself in vision to any of the Prophets of the old or new Testament (namely to Daniel as an old man ² ) or the three Angels representing the three Persons to Abraham ³, or the one Angel that wrestling with Jacob bare our Lord's Person ⁴, no such thing is any where forbidden, but is very agreeable to the people's instruction. In which sort the Angels were commonly portraited ⁵ (and namely the Cherubins over the Propitiatory) as they be now in the Church, not in their natural form, but with corporal wings (as the Seraphins appeared to Esay the Prophet ⁶ ) to express their quality and office of being God's Angels, that is, \+it Messengers\+it*: and God the Father with the world in his hand, to signify his creation and government of the same, and such like: Whereof the people being well instructed may take much good, and no harm in the world, being now through their faith in Christ far from all fond imagination of the false gods of the Pagans. ⁷ And therefore S. Gregory ⁸ saith of the Church's Images, \+it That which scripture or writing doth to the readers, the same doth the picture to the simple that look thereupon. For in it even the ignorant see what they ought to follow, in it they do read, that know no letters\+it*. Where he calleth it a matter of antiquity and very convenient, that in holy places Images were painted to the people's instruction, so they be taught that they may not be adored with divine honour. And he in the same place sharply rebuketh Serenus the Bishop of Massilia, that of indiscreet zeal he would take away Images, rather than teach the people how to use them. \fq ¹ \ft How there may be Images or resemblances of the three persons in Trinity, and of Angels. \fq ² \ft \+it Dan. 7, 22.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Gen. 18, 2.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Gen. 32, 24.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Exo. 37, 7,\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Esa. 6, 2.\+it* \fq ⁷ \ft Images are for the people's instruction. \fq ⁸ \ft \+it Grego. li. 9. ep. 9.\+it*\ef*
\v 30 And the times truly of this ignorance whereas God despised, now he denounceth unto men that all every-where do penance,
\v 31 for that he hath appointed a day wherein he will judge the world in equity, by a man whom he hath appointed, giving all men faith, raising him up from the dead.
\v 32 And when they had heard the resurrection of the dead, certain indeed mocked, but certain said: We will hear thee again concerning this point.
\v 33 So Paul went forth out of the midst of them.
\v 34 But certain men joining unto him, did believe: among whom was also \f 1 \fr 17.34 \ft Dionysius Areopagita.\f* Dionysius Areopagita, and a woman named Damaris, and others with them. \ef - \fr 17.34 \fq Dionysius Areopagita \ft ¹ This is that famous Denys that first converted France, and wrote those notable and divine works \+it de Ecclesiastica & Cælesti hierarchia, de divinis nominibus\+it*, and others, in which he confirmeth and proveth plainly almost all things that the Church now useth in the ministration of the holy Sacraments, and affirmeth that he learned them of the Apostles, giving also testimony for the Catholic faith in most things now controversed, so plainly, that our Adversaries have no shift but to deny this Denys to have been the author of them, feigning that they be another's of later age. Which is an old flight of Heretics, but most proper to these of all others. Who seeing all antiquity against them, are forced to be more bold or rather impudent than others in that point. \fq ¹ \ft S. Dionysius Areopagita is all for the Catholics.\ef*
\c 18
\cd At Corinth in Achaia, he worketh with his own hands, preaching JESUS to be CHRIST, unto the Jews upon their Sabboths. 6. But they being obstinate and blaspheming, he in plain terms forsaketh them, and turneth to the Gentiles, among whom according to a vision that he had to embolden him, he planteth the Church in great numbers, 12. the obstinate Jews in vain soliciting the Proconsul against him. 18. From thence at length departing he turneth 19. by Ephesus (where he promiseth the Jews to return to them) 22. and so to Antioch in Syria (from whence he began his journey Act. 15.) 23. but not resting, by and by he goeth again to visit the new Churches that he planted Act. 16. in Galatia and Phrygia: 24. Apollo in his absence mightily confounding the Jews at Ephesus, 27. and afterward at Corinth.
\p
\v 1 \sc After\sc* these things, departing from Athens, he came to Corinth.
\v 2 and finding a certain Jew, named Aquila, born in Pontus, who of late was come out of Italy, & Priscilla his wife (because Claudius had commanded all Jews to depart from Rome,) he came to them.
\v 3 And because he was of the same craft, he remained with them, and wrought, (and they were tent-makers by their craft.)
\v 4 And he disputed in the synagogue every Sabboth, interposing the name of our Lord \sc Jesus\sc*, and he exhorted the Jews and the Greeks.
\v 5 And when Silas and Timothee were come from Macedonia, Paul was instant in preaching, testifying to the Jews that \sc Jesus\sc* is \sc Christ\sc*.
\v 6 But they contradicting and blaspheming, he shaking his garments, said to them: Your blood upon your own head: I being clean, from henceforth will go to the Gentiles.
\v 7 And departing thence, he entered into the house of a certain man, named Titus Justus, one that served God, whose house was adjoining to the synagogue.
\v 8 And Crispus the prince of the Synagogue believed our Lord, with all his house: & many of the Corinthians hearing believed, and were baptized.
\v 9 And our Lord said in the night by a vision to Paul: Do not fear, but speak, and hold not thy peace,
\v 10 for-because I am with thee: and no man shall set upon thee to hurt thee: for I have much people in this city.
\v 11 And he sat there a year & six months, teaching among them the word of God.
\v 12 But Gallio being Proconsul of Achaia, the Jews with one accord rose up against Paul, and brought him to the judgement seat,
\v 13 saying: That this man contrary to the Law persuadeth men to worship God.
\v 14 And Paul beginning to open his mouth, Gallio said to the Jews: If it were some unjust thing, or an heinous fact, O you men Jews, I should by reason bear you.
\v 15 But if they be questions of word and names, and of your law, yourselves look unto it: I will not be judge of these things.
\v 16 And he drove them from the judgement seat.
\v 17 And all apprehending Sosthenes the prince of the Synagogue, strook him before the judgement seat: and Gallio cared for none of those things.
\v 18 But Paul when he had stayed yet many days, taking his leave of the brethren, sailed to Syria, (and with him Priscilla and Aquila,) who had shorn his head in Cenchris. For he had \x - \xt Nu. 6, 18. Act. 21, 24.\x* a vow.
\v 19 And he came unto Ephesus, and them he left there. But himself entering into the Synagogue, disputed with the Jews.
\v 20 And when they desired him, that he would tarry a longer time, he consented not,
\v 21 but taking his leave, and saying: I will return to you again God willing, he departed from Ephesus.
\v 22 And going down to Cæsarea, he went up, and saluted the Church, and came down to Antioch.
\v 23 And having tarried there a certain time, he departed, walking in order through the country of Galatia and Phrygia, confirming all the Disciples.
\v 24 And a certain Jew, named \f 1 \fr 18.24 \ft Apollo.\f* Apollo, born at Alexandria, an eloquent man, came to Ephesus, mighty in the Scriptures.
\v 25 This man was taught the way of our Lord: and being fervent in spirit he spake, & taught diligently those things that pertain to \sc Jesus\sc*, knowing only the Baptism of John.
\v 26 This man therefore began to deal confidently in the Synagogue. Whom when Priscilla and Aquila had heard, they took him unto them, and expounded to him the way of our Lord more diligently.
\v 27 And whereas he was desirous to go to Achaia, the brethren exhorting wrote to the Disciples to receive him. Who, when he was come, profited them much that had believed.
\v 28 For he with vehemency convinced the Jews openly, shewing by the scriptures, that \sc Jesus\sc* is \sc Christ\sc*.
\c 19
\cd How Paul began the Church of Ephesus, first in twelve that were baptized with John's baptism, 8. then preaching three months in the Synagogue of the Jews, until for their obstinacy and blaspheming, he forsook them, disputing afterward in a certain school for two years space to the marvelous increase of the Church, specially through his great miracles also, in healing diseases with the touch of his clothes, and expelling devils, 13. who yet contemned the Exorcists of the Jews. 18. How the Christians there confess their acts, and burn their unlawful books: 21. and how he foretold that after he had been at Hierusalem, he must see Rome. 23. and what a great sedition was raised against him at Ephesus, by them that got their living by working to the idolatrous Temple of Diana.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \f 1 \fr 19.1 \ft The Epistle upon Whitsun-eve.\f* it came to pass when Apollo was at Corinth, that Paul having gone through the higher parts came to Ephesus, and found certain Disciples:
\v 2 and he said to them: Have you received the Holy Ghost, believing? But they said to him: Nay, neither have we heard whether there be a Holy Ghost.
\v 3 But he said: In what then were you baptized? Who said, \f 1 \fr 19.3 \ft c John's Baptism not sufficient.\f* In John's Baptism.
\v 4 And Paul said: \x - \xt Mt. 3, 11. Mr. 1, 8. Lu. 3, 16.\x* John baptized the people with the Baptism of penance, saying, That they should believe in him that was to come after him, that is to say, \f 2 \fr 19.4 \ft c Christ's Baptism necessary.\f* in \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 5 Hearing these things, they were baptized in the name of our Lord \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 6 And when Paul had \f 1 \fr 19.6 \ft S. Paul ministered the Sacrament of Confirmation. \+it See annot. c. 8, 17.\+it*\f* imposed hands on them, the Holy Ghost came upon them, & they spake with tongues, and prophesied.
\v 7 And all the men were about twelve.
\v 8 And entering into the Synagogue, he spake confidently for three months, disputing & exhorting of the Kingdom of God.
\v 9 But when certain were indurate, and believed not, ill-speaking the way of our Lord before the multitude, departing from them, he separated the Disciples, daily disputing in the school of one Tyrannus.
\v 10 And this was done for the space of two years, so that all which dwelt in Asia, heard the word of our Lord, Jews and Gentiles.
\v 11 And God wrought by the hand of Paul miracles not common:
\v 12 so that there were also brought from his body napkins or handkerchiefs upon the sick, and the diseases departed from them, and the wicked spirits went out. \ef - \fr 19.12 \fq Napkins. \ft ¹ The napkins that had touched S. Paul's body, wrought miracles, and it was no superstition to attribute that virtue to them which God gave to them indeed: nor to seek to touch them for health, was any dishonour to God, but it much proved Christ's religion to be true, and him to be the only God, whose servants, yea whose servants' ² shades and napkins could do such wonders, as S. Chrysostome \+it (to. 5. cont. Gentiles. quod Christus sit Deus, in vit. Babylæ.)\+it* sheweth in a whole book to that purpose, against the Pagans, proving hereby and by the like virtue of other Saints and their Relics, that Christ their Lord and Master is God. For it is all one concerning the bodies of Saints, relics, garments, statues, books, or any thing that belonged to them, all which may and have done and yet do (when it is necessary to our edification) the like wonders to God's great honour: not only in their life time, but after their death much more. For S. Paul's napkins had as great force when he was dead, as when he lived, and so much more, as his grace and dignity with God is greater than before. Which S. Chrysostom in the place alleged proveth at large by the shrine of S. Babylas the Martyr: and to think contrary, is the Heresy of Vigilantius, condemned so long since as S. Hierom's time, and by him refuted aboundantly. \fq ¹ \ft Touching of Relics, & miracles done by the same. \fq ² \ft \+it Act. 5, 15.\+it*\ef*
\v 13 And certain also of the Judaical exorcists that went about, assayed to invocate upon them that had evil spirits, the name of our Lord \sc Jesus\sc*, saying: I adjure you by \sc Jesus\sc* whom Paul preacheth.
\v 14 And there were certain sons of Sceva a Jew, chief Priest, seven, that did this.
\v 15 But the wicked spirit answering, said to them: \sc Jesus\sc* I know, and Paul I know: but you, what are ye? \ef - \fr 19.15 \fq Paul I know. \ft ¹ Both the said napkins taken from S. Paul's body, and his name also, were dreadful and able to expel devils. Whereby we learn that not only Christ's name, which is the principal, but his servant's names also invocated upon the possessed, have power over devils: which is a marvelous honour to Saints, and nothing diminisheth the glory of Christ, but exceedingly increaseth the same, not only himself, but his servants also being able to do such things, and to be stronger than any Devil in Hell. So we read in ² S. Hierom that many did invocate the name of S. Hilarion upon the possessed, and the Devils straight departed. So did the Devil know S. Babylas and other Saints, even after they were dead, when they could not speak for the presence of their Relics, and when they were tormented and expelled by them: whereof all antiquity is full of testimonies. But our Heretics Luther and Calvin and their Scholars attempting to cast out Devils, sped much like as these good fellows did. \fq ¹ \ft The name, or presence, or Relics of Saints & holy men, confound the Devil. \fq ² \ft \+it In vit. Hilarionis. Chrys. loco citato.\+it*\ef*
\v 16 And the man in whom the wicked spirit was, leaping upon them, and mastering \f 1 \fr 19.16 \fq both \ft \+it them\+it*\f* both, prevailed against them, so that they fled out of that house naked and wounded.
\v 17 And this was made notorious to all the Jews and the Gentiles that dwelt at Ephesus: and fear fell upon all them, and the name of our Lord \sc Jesus\sc* was magnified.
\v 18 And many of them that believed, came confessing & declaring \f 1 \fr 19.18 \ft They made not only a general confession wherein all men shew themselves alike to be sinners, as our Protestants do: but every one confessed his own proper deeds and faults.\f* their deeds.
\v 19 And many of them that had followed curious things, brought together their books, and burnt them before all: and counting the prices of them, they found the money to be fifty thousand pence. \ef - \fr 19.19 \fq Curious things. \ft ¹ Curious and unlawful sciences, as Witchcraft, Necromancy, and other means of divination by soothsaying, figure-casting, interpretation of dreams, or any way not allowed by God and his Church, must much more be abhorred of old Christians, when these so lately converted were so zealous and diligent to leave them. And by this example all that are newly reconciled to the Church, are taught, the first thing they do, to burn their heretical and naughty books. \fq ¹ \ft Superstitious, heretical, and all hurtful books must be made away.\ef* \ef - \fr 19.19 \fq Books. \ft ¹ A Christian man is bound to burn or deface all wicked books of what sort so ever, specially Heretical books. Which though they infect not him always that keepeth them, yet being forth-coming, they may be noisome and pernicious to others that shall have them and read them after his death, or otherwise. Therefore hath the Church taken order for condemning all such books, and against the reading of them, where danger may ensue: and the Christian Emperors, Constantinus Magnus, Valentinian, Theodosius, Martian, Justinian, made penal laws for the burning or defacing of them. \+it Sozom. li. 1. c. 20. li. 2. c. 31. Conc. Chalc. act. 3. in fine, cap. Amplæ, & in fine totius Conc. c. Imperator. Conc. Constantinop. 2. confes. 5. cap. Debitum. & Act. 1. cap. 1. &. cap. Rom.\+it* See Eusebius \+it li. 3. de vita Constant. c. 61. 62. 63. 64.\+it* The danger of reading them, as it is manifest, so it is signified by Euseb. \+it li. 7. c. 6.\+it* S. Augustine \+it li. 3. de bapt. c. 14.\+it* S. Greg. \+it li. 5. ep. 64.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Decrees and penal laws against heretical books.\ef*
\v 20 \f 1 \fr 19.20 \ft The 6. part.\f* So mightily increased the word of God and was confirmed.
\v 21 And when these things were ended, Paul purposed in the Spirit, when he had passed through Macedonia and Achaia, to go to Jerusalem, saying: After I shall have been there, I must see \f 1 \fr 19.21 \ft Of taking away the Gospel from Hierusalem the head city of the Jews, and giving it to ROME the head city of the Gentiles.\f* Rome also.
\v 22 And sending into Macedonia two of them that ministered unto him, Timothee and Erastus, himself remained for a time in Asia.
\v 23 And at that time there was made no little trouble about the way of our Lord.
\v 24 For one named Demetrius, a silver-smith, that made silver \f 1 \fr 19.24 \ft c The Protestants translate, \+it shrines,\+it* in the Bible an. 1577: to make the people think that it toucheth the holy \+it shrines\+it* of Saints: most corruptly, the Greek signifying plainly, \+it temples,\+it* and that of heathen gods.\f*\f 2 \fr 19.24 \ft \+it c ναοὺς.\+it*\f* temples of Diana, procured to the artificers no small gain:
\v 25 whom calling together and them that were the same kind of workmen, he said: Sirs, you know that our gain is of this occupation;
\v 26 and you see, and hear that this same Paul by persuasion hath averted a great multitude not only of Ephesus, but almost of all Asia, saying: That they are not gods which be made by hands.
\v 27 And not only unto us is this part in danger to be reproved, but also the temple of great Diana shall be reputed for nothing, yea & her majesty shall begin to be destroyed, whom all Asia & the world worshippeth.
\v 28 Hearing these things they were replenished with anger, and cried out saying: Great is Diana of the Ephesians.
\v 29 And the whole city was filled with confusion, and they ran violently with one accord into the theater, catching Gaius & Aristarchus Macedonians, Paul's companions.
\v 30 And when Paul would have entered in to the people, the Disciples did not permit him.
\v 31 And certain also of the Princes of Asia that were his friends, sent unto him, desiring that he would not adventure himself into the theater:
\v 32 and others cried another thing. For the assembly was confuse, & the more part knew not for what cause they were assembled.
\v 33 And of the multitude they drew forth Alexander, the Jews thrusting him forward. But Alexander with his hand desiring silence, would have given the people satisfaction.
\v 34 Whom as soon as they perceived to be a Jew, there was made one voice of all, almost for the space of two hours crying out: Great is Diana of the Ephesians.
\v 35 And when the Scribe had appeased the multitudes, he saith: Ye men of Ephesus, for what man is there that knoweth not the city of the Ephesians to be a worshipper of great Diana, and \f 1 \fr 19.35 \ft c Here the Heretics add to the text this word, \+it image,\+it* more than is in the Greek, to put a scruple into the people's mind concerning holy \+it image\+it*s.\f*\f 2 \fr 19.35 \ft \+it c τοῦ διοπετοῦς.\+it*\f* Jupiter's child?
\v 36 Forasmuch therefore as these things cannot be gainsaid, you must be quieted, and do nothing rashly.
\v 37 For you have brought these men, being neither sacrilegious, nor blaspheming your goddess.
\v 38 But if Demetrius & the artificers that are with him, have matter to say against any man, there are Courts kept in the common place, & there are Proconsuls; let them accuse one another.
\v 39 And if you ask any other matter, it may be resolved in a lawful assembly.
\v 40 For we are in danger also to be accused for this day's sedition: whereas there is no man guilty by whom we may give an account of this concourse. And when he had said these things, he dismissed the assembly.
\c 20
\cd Having visited the Churches of Macedonia and Achaia (as he purposed Act. 19.) and now about to sail from Corinth toward Hierusalem, because of the Jews lying in wait for him, he is constrained to return into Macedonia. 6. And so at Philippis taking boat, cometh to Troas, where upon the Sunday, with a sermon, and a miracle, he greatly confirmeth that Church. 13. Thence coming to Miletum, 17. he sendeth to Ephesus for the Clergy of those parts: to whom he maketh a Pastoral sermon, committing unto their charge the flock begun by him there, and now like to be seen of him no more, considering the troubles that by revelation he looketh for at Hierusalem.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* after that the tumult was ceased, Paul calling the Disciples, and exhorting them, took his leave, and set forward to go into Macedonia.
\v 2 And when he had walked through those parts, & had exhorted them with much speech, he came to Greece:
\v 3 where when he had spent three months, the Jews laid wait for him as he was about to sail into Syria: and he had counsel to return through Macedonia.
\v 4 And there accompanied him Sosipater of Pyrrhus, of Beroea: and of Thessalonians, Aristarchus, and Secundus: and Caius of Derbe, and Timothee: and of Asia, Tychicus and Trophimus.
\v 5 These going before, stayed for us at Troas:
\v 6 But we sailed after the days of Azymes from Philippi, and came to them unto Troas in five days, where we abode seven days.
\v 7 And in the first of the Sabboth when we were assembled to \f 1 \fr 20.7 \ft S. Paul did here break bread on the Sunday as it is broken in the Sacrament of the body of Christ, and had both before & after the celebrating of the Sacrament a sermon to the people. \+it Aug. ep. 86. ad Casulanum. Vener. Beda in 20. Act.\+it*\f* break bread, Paul disputed with them, being to depart on the morrow: and he continued the sermon until mid-night.
\v 8 And there were a great number of lamps in the upper chamber where we were assembled.
\v 9 And a certain young man named Eutychus, sitting upon the window, whereas he was oppressed with heavy sleep: (Paul disputing long) driven by sleep, fell from the third loft down, and was taken up dead.
\v 10 To whom when Paul was gone down, he lay upon him: and embracing him he said: Be not troubled, for his soul is in him.
\v 11 And going up and breaking bread and tasting, and having talked sufficiently to them until day light, so he departed.
\v 12 And they brought the lad alive, & were not a little comforted.
\v 13 But we going up into the ship, sailed to Asson, from thence meaning to receive Paul; for so he had ordained, himself purposing to journey by land.
\v 14 And when he had found us in Asson, taking him with us we came to Mitylene.
\v 15 And sailing thence, the day following we came over against Chios: and the other day we arrived at Samos: and the day following we came to Miletum.
\v 16 For Paul had purposed to sail leaving Ephesus, lest any stay should be made him in Asia. For he hastened, if it were possible for him, to keep the day of Pentecost at Jerusalem. \ef - \fr 20.16 \fq Pentecost. \ft ¹ Though the Apostles might desire to come to the Jews' Festivities, by reason of the general concourse of people to the same, the better to deal for their salvation and to spread the Gospel of Christ, yet it is like that they now kept solemnly the Christian Pentecost or Whitsuntide, for memory of the Holy Ghost, and that S. Paul went to that Feast of the Christians rather than the other of the Jews. And Ven. Bede saith here: \+it The Apostle maketh haste to keep the fiftieth day, that is, of remission and of the Holy Ghost.\+it* For, that the Christians already kept the eighth day, that is, the ² Sunday or our Lord's day, and had altered already the ordinary Sabboth into the same, it is plain by the Scriptures \+it (1. Cor. 16, 2. Apoc. 1, 10.)\+it* and by antiquity. \+it Justin. Mart. Apolog. 2. ad Anton. Pium in fine.\+it* And it is as like that they changed the Jews' Pasch and Pentecost as that, specially when it is evident that ³ these Festivities be kept by Apostolic tradition, and approved by the use of all ancient Churches and Councels. \fq ¹ \ft The Christian Pentecost. \fq ² \ft Sunday. \fq ³ \ft \+it Aug. ep. 118. c. 1.\+it*\ef*
\v 17 \f 1 \fr 20.17 \ft The Epistle for S. Steven Pope. Aug. 2.\f* And sending from Miletum to Ephesus, he called the \f 2 \fr 20.17 \ft c That is, \+it Priests\+it* as \+it Act. 15, 4.\+it* See the marginal annot. there.\f*\f 3 \fr 20.17 \ft \+it c πρεσβυτέρους\+it*\f* Ancients of the Church.
\v 18 Who being come to him, and assembled together, he said to them: You know \x - \xt Act. 19, 1.\x* from the first day that I entered into Asia, in what manner I have been with you all the time,
\v 19 serving our Lord with all humility and tears, and tentations that did chance to me by the conspiracies of the Jews:
\v 20 How I have withdrawn nothing that was profitable, but that I preached it to you, and taught you openly and from house to house,
\v 21 testifying to Jews and Gentiles \f 1 \fr 20.21 \ft Apostolic preaching commendeth not faith only but penance also to the people.\f* penance toward God, and faith in our Lord \sc Jesus Christ\sc*.
\v 22 And now behold, being bound by the spirit, I go to Jerusalem; not knowing what things shall befall me in it,
\v 23 but that the Holy Ghost throughout all cities doth protest to me, saying: that bands and tribulations abide me at Jerusalem.
\v 24 But I fear none of these things, neither do I make my life more precious than myself, so that I may consummate my course & ministry which I received of our Lord \sc Jesus\sc*, to testify the Gospel of the grace of God.
\v 25 And now behold I do know, that you shall no more see my face all you, through whom I have passed preaching the Kingdom of God.
\v 26 Wherefore I take you to witness this present day that I am clear from the blood of all.
\v 27 For I have not spared to declare unto you all the counsel of God.
\v 28 Take heed to yourselves and to the whole flock wherein the \f 1 \fr 20.28 \ft Bishops or Priests (for then these names were sometime used indifferently) governors of the Church of God, & placed in that room & high function by the Holy Ghost.\f* Holy Ghost hath placed you bishops, to rule the Church of God which he hath purchased with his own blood.
\v 29 I know that after my departure there will ravening wolves enter in among you, not sparing the flock. \ef - \fr 20.29 \fq Ravening wolves. \ft ¹ The governors of the Church are foretold of the great danger that should fall to the people by wolves, that is to say, by Heretics, whose cruelty toward the Catholics is noted by this term. They be known by the forsaking the unity of the Church whereof they were before, by going out and drawing many disciples after them, and by their perverse doctrine. Such wolves came afterward indeed in diverse ages, Arius, Macedonius, Nestorius, Eutyches, Luther, Calvin, great blood-sucking wolves, and wasters of the flock of Christ. \fq ¹ \ft Ravening wolves are the Heretics of all ages.\ef*
\v 30 And out of your own selves shall arise men speaking perverse things, to draw away Disciples after themselves.
\v 31 For the which cause be vigilant, keeping in memory that for three years night & day I ceased not with tears to admonish every one of you.
\v 32 And now I commend you to God and to the word of his grace, who is able to edify, and to give inheritance in all the sanctified.
\v 33 No man's silver and gold or garment have I coveted.
\v 34 Yourselves know that for such things as were needful for me and them that are with me, these hands have ministered.
\v 35 I have shewed you all things, that so labouring, you must receive the weak, and remember the word of our Lord \sc Jesus\sc*, because he said: It is a more blessed thing to give rather than to take. \ef - \fr 20.35 \fq More blessed to give. \ft ¹ Among many other infinite goodly things and speeches which Christ spake and be not written in the Gospels, this sentence is one: which S. Paul heard of some of the Apostles daily conversant with him, or else learned of Christ himself, or of the Holy Ghost. And it signifieth, that whereas the world commonly counteth him happy that receiveth any benefit, as alms either temporal or spiritual, ² yet indeed he that giveth or bestoweth, is more happy. Which if the world did well consider, men would give alms faster than they do, if it were but for their own benefit. \fq ¹ \ft Christ's speeches not written in the Gospel. \fq ² \ft Great almsmen blessed.\ef*
\v 36 And when he had said these things, falling on his knees he prayed with all them.
\v 37 And there was great weeping made of all; and falling upon the neck of Paul, they kissed him,
\v 38 being sorry most of all for the word which he had said, that they should see his face no more. And they brought him going unto the ship.
\c 21
\cd From Miletum going on his journey, 4. he cannot be dissuaded neither at Tyre, 8. nor at Cæsarea (in both which places the Holy Ghost revealed how he should be handled in Hierusalem, 10. the Prophet Agabus expressly foretelling that the Jews there should deliver him to the Gentiles) 15. but to Hierusalem he cometh: where being welcome to the Christians, and namely to James the Bishop, and to the Priests, while he goeth about to satisfy the Christian Jews there, who had been misinformed of him as if he had taught it to be unlawful for the Jews to keep Moyses' Law: 27. he is invaded by the infidel Jews, \it and ready to be murdered by them, until the Roman soldiers do rescue him.\it*
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* when it came to pass that we sailed, being carried from them, with a straight course we came to Coos, and the day following to Rhodes, and from thence to Patara.
\v 2 And when we had found a ship that passed over to Phoenice, going up into it we sailed.
\v 3 And when we were in the sight of Cypres, leaving it on the left hand, we sailed into Syria, and came to Tyre: for there the ship was to discharge her load.
\v 4 And finding Disciples, we tarried there seven days: who said to Paul by the Spirit, that he should not go up to Jerusalem.
\v 5 And the days being expired, departing we went forward, all bringing us on the way, with their wives and children, till we were out of the city: and falling upon our knees on the shore, we prayed.
\v 6 And when we had bid one another farewell, we went up into the ship; and they returned unto their own.
\v 7 But we having ended the navigation, from Tyre came down to Ptolomais: and saluting the brethren, we tarried one day with them.
\v 8 And the next day departing, we came to Cæsarea. And entering into the house of \x - \xt Act. 6, 5.\x* Philip the Evangelist, who was one of the seven, we tarried with him.
\v 9 And he had \f 1 \fr 21.9 \ft As S. Peter had a wife, but used her not after his calling, as it is noted elsewhere out of S. Hierom \+it Luc. 4, 38:\+it* so may it be said of S. Philip being Deacon.\f* four daughters virgins, that did prophesy. \ef - \fr 21.9 \fq Virgins. \ft ¹ S. Luke noteth specially that his daughters were Virgins: meaning (no doubt) that they were of the state, profession, or purpose of perpetual virginity, not only that they were young maids unmarried: and that they were the rather for that, endued with the gift of prophecy, as S. Hierom saith \+it li. 1. adv. Iovin. c. 24. See Oecum. c. 29. in hunc locum.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Virgins.\ef*
\v 10 And as we abode there for certain days, there came a certain Prophet from Jewry, named Agabus.
\v 11 He, when he was come to us, took Paul's girdle: and binding his own feet & hands, he said: Thus saith the Holy Ghost: The man whose girdle this is, so shall the Jews bind in Jerusalem, and shall deliver him into the hands of the Gentiles.
\v 12 Which when we had heard, we and they that were of the same place, desired him that he would not go up to Jerusalem.
\v 13 Then Paul answered, and said: What do you, weeping and afflicting my heart? for I am ready not only to be bound, but to die also in Jerusalem for the name of our Lord \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 14 And when we could not persuade him, we ceased, saying: The will of our Lord be done.
\v 15 And after these days, being prepared, we went up to Jerusalem.
\v 16 And there came also of the Disciples from Cæsarea with us, bringing with them one Jason a Cyprian (with whom we should lodge) an old Disciple.
\v 17 And when we were come to Jerusalem, the brethren received us gladly.
\v 18 And the day following Paul went in with us to James, and all the Ancients were assembled.
\v 19 Whom when he had saluted, he told particularly what God had done among the Gentiles by his ministry.
\v 20 But they hearing it, magnified God, and said to him: Thou seest (brother) how many thousands there are among the Jews that have believed: and all are zelatours of the Law.
\v 21 But they have heard of thee that thou doest teach those Jews that are among the Gentiles, to depart from Moyses: saying that they ought not to circumcise their children, nor walk according to the custom.
\v 22 What is it then? needs must the multitude assemble: for they will hear that thou art come.
\v 23 Do this therefore which we tell thee. There are with us four men, that have a vow on them.
\v 24 Taking these unto thee, sanctify thyself with them; and bestow on them, that they may \x - \xt Nu. 6, 18.\x* shave their heads: and all shall know that the things which they heard of thee, are false: but that thyself also walkest keeping the Law. \ef - \fr 21.24 \fq Keeping the law. \ft ¹ All the observations of the Law were now in themselves dead and unprofitable, yet till further propagation of the Gospel, they were not damnable to the keepers, nor offensive to God, but might be observed even of the Christian Jews. And for fear of scandalizing the weak of that nation, newly converted, or prone to receive the faith, the Apostles by God's suggestion did think it good to observe them as occasion required. \fq ¹ \ft Avoiding of scandal in things not unlawful.\ef*
\v 25 But concerning them that believe of the Gentiles, \x - \xt Act. 15, 20.\x* we have written, decreeing that they should refrain themselves from the immolated to idols, and blood, and suffocated, and fornication.
\v 26 Then Paul taking the men unto him, the next day being purified with them entered into the temple, shewing the accomplishment of the \x - \xt Nu. 6, 13.\x* days of the purification, until an oblation was offered for every one of them.
\v 27 But whiles the seven days were a finishing, those Jews that were of Asia, when they had seen him in the temple, stirred up all the people, and laid hands upon him,
\v 28 crying: Ye men of Israel, help: this is the man that against the people and the Law and this place teaching all men every-where, hath also moreover brought in Gentiles into the temple, and hath violated this holy place.
\v 29 (For they had seen Trophimus the Ephesian in the city with him, whom they supposed that Paul had brought into the temple.)
\v 30 And the whole city was in an uproar: and there was made a concourse of the people. And apprehending Paul, they drew him forth of the temple: and immediately the doors were shut.
\v 31 And as they sought to kill him, it was told the Tribune of the band, That all Jerusalem is in a confusion.
\v 32 Who forthwith taking unto him soldiers and Centurions, ran down to them. Who, when they had seen the Tribune and the soldiers, ceased to strike Paul.
\v 33 Then the Tribune coming near apprehended him, and commanded him to be bound with two chains: and he demanded who he was, and what he had done.
\v 34 And some cried one thing, some another, in the multitude. And whereas he could not know the certainty for the tumult, he commanded him to be led \f 1 \fr 21.34 \ft c In castra. \+it So in the places following.\+it*\f* into the castle.
\v 35 And when he was come to the stairs, it chanced that he was carried of the soldiers because of the violence of the people.
\v 36 For the multitude of the people followed, crying: Away with him.
\v 37 And when Paul began to be brought into the castle, he saith to the Tribune: is it lawful for me to speak something to thee? Who said: Canst thou speak Greek?
\v 38 Art not thou the Ægyptian that before these days did raise a tumult, and didst lead forth into the desert four thousand men that were murderers?
\v 39 And Paul said to him: \x - \xt Act. 22, 3.\x* I am a man truly a Jew of Tarsus, a citizen not of an obscure city of Cilicia. And I desire thee, permit me to speak to the people.
\v 40 And when he had permitted him, Paul standing on the stairs, beckoned with his hand to the people. And great silence being made, he spake unto them in the Hebrew tongue, saying.
\c 22
\cd Being licensed by the Tribune to speak to the people, he sheweth them that he was once as earnest on that side as they now be: 6. and how strange and miraculous his conversion was. 17. They hear him quietly, until he began to make mention of a vision that sent him away from them to the Gentiles. 22. Then they cry out upon him so, 23. that for their crying the Tribune commandeth him to be scourged. 25. which yet by his wisdom he escapeth.
\p
\v 1 \sc Men\sc* brethren and fathers, hear what account I do render now unto you.
\v 2 (And when they had heard that he spake to them in the Hebrew tongue, they did the more keep silence.
\v 3 And he saith:) \x - \xt Act. 21, 39.\x* I am a man a Jew, born at Tarsus in Cilicia, but brought up in this city, at the feet of Gamaliel instructed according to the verity of the law of the fathers, an emulator of the Law as also all you are this day:
\v 4 who \x - \xt Act. 8, 3.\x* persecuted this way unto death, binding & delivering into custodies men & women,
\v 5 as the high Priest doth give me testimony, and all the Ancients. Of whom \x - \xt Act. 9, 2.\x* receiving letters also to the brethren, I went to Damascus, that I might bring them thence bound to Jerusalem, to be punished.
\v 6 And it came to pass as I was going, and drawing nigh to Damascus at mid-day, suddenly from Heaven there shone round about me much light:
\v 7 and falling on the ground, I heard a voice saying to me: Saul, Saul, why persecutest thou me?
\v 8 And I answered: Who are thou Lord? And he said to me: I am \sc Jesus\sc* of Nazareth, whom thou persecutest.
\v 9 And they that were with me, saw the light indeed, but the voice they heard not of him that spake with me.
\v 10 And I said: What shall I do Lord? And our Lord said to me: Arise and go to Damascus; and there it shall be told thee of all things that thou must do.
\v 11 And whereas I did not see for the brightness of that light, being led of my companions by the hand, I came to Damascus.
\v 12 And one Ananias, a man according to the Law, having testimony of all the Jews' inhabitants,
\v 13 coming to me, and standing by me, said to me: Brother Saul, look up. And I the self-same hour looked upon him.
\v 14 But he said: The God of our fathers hath preordained thee, that thou shouldest know his will, and see the just one, and hear a voice from his mouth:
\v 15 because thou shalt be his witness to all men, of those things which thou hast seen and heard.
\v 16 And now what tarriest thou? Rise up, and be baptized, and \f 1 \fr 22.16 \ft The Sacrament of Baptism doth itself wash away sins as here is plain, & therefore doth not only signify (as the Heretics affirm) that our sins be forgiven before, or otherwise by faith only remitted. Whereby the Church's doctrine is proved to be fully agreeable to the Scriptures, that the Sacraments give grace \+it ex opere operato,\+it* that is, by the force & virtue of the work and word, done & said in the Sacrament.\f* wash away thy sins invocating his name.
\v 17 And it befell me returning into Jerusalem, and praying in the temple, that I was in a trance,
\v 18 and saw him saying unto me: Make haste, and depart quickly out of Jerusalem: because they will not receive thy testimony of me.
\v 19 And I said, Lord, they know that I did cast into prison and beat in every Synagogue them that believed in thee.
\v 20 And when the blood of Steven thy \f 1 \fr 22.20 \ft \+it c μάρτυρος. Act. 7, 38.\+it*\f* witness was shed, \f 2 \fr 22.20 \ft Not only the Principals, but all that consent to the death or vexation of Christian men for the Catholic faith, do highly offend: which the Apostle confesseth here, that God's mercy may be more notoriously glorified in him hereby.\f* I stood by and consented, and kept the garments of them that killed him.
\v 21 And he said to me: Go, for into the Gentiles a far will I send thee.
\v 22 And they heard him until this word, and they lifted up their voice, saying: Away with such an one from the earth: for it is not meet he should live.
\v 23 And when they cried out, and threw off their garments, and cast dust into the air,
\v 24 the Tribune commanded him to be carried into the castle, and to be beaten with whips, & that he should be tormented: to know for what cause they did so cry at him.
\v 25 And when they had bound him very straight with thongs, Paul saith to the Centurion standing by him: Is it lawful for you to whip a man that is a Roman and uncondemned?
\v 26 Which the Centurion hearing, went to the Tribune, and told him, saying: What wilt thou do? for this man is a citizen of Rome.
\v 27 And the Tribune coming, said to him: Tell me, art thou a Roman? But he said: Yea.
\v 28 And the Tribune answered: I obtained this city with a great sum. And Paul said: But I was also born to it.
\v 29 Immediately therefore they departed from him that were to torment him. The Tribune also feared after he understood that he was a citizen of Rome, and because he had bound him.
\v 30 But the next day meaning to know more diligently for what cause he was accused of the Jews, he loosed him, and commanded the \f 1 \fr 22.30 \ft c ἀρχιερεῖς.\f* Priests to come together and all the Council: & bringing forth Paul, he set him among them.
\c 23
\cd As the people in the tumult, so also the very chief of the Jews in their Councel shew themselves obstinate, and willful persecutors of the truth in S. Paul's person. Whose behaviour towards them is full of constancy, modesty, and wisdom. 11 (Christ also by a vision encouraging him, and foretelling that he shall to Rome.) 12. yea they conspire with 40 men to kill him treacherously. 16. But the matter being detected, the Roman Tribune conveyeth him strongly to Cæsarea.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* Paul looking upon the Council, said: Men brethren, I with all good conscience have conversed before God, until this present day.
\v 2 And the high Priest Ananias commanded them that stood by him, to smite him on the mouth.
\v 3 Then Paul said to him: \f 1 \fr 23.3 \ft He said not this through perturbation of mind or of a passion, but by way of prophecy, that this figurative high priesthood then trimmed like a whited wall, was to be destroyed, whereas now the true priesthood of Christ was come. \+it Beda in hunc lo.\+it*\f* God shall strike thee, thou whited wall. And thou sitting judgest me according to the law, and contrary to law doest thou command me to be smitten?
\v 4 And they that stood by, said: Doest thou revile the high Priest of God?
\v 5 And Paul said: I knew not, brethren, that he is the high Priest. For it is written: \x - \xt Exo. 22, 28.\x* \it The prince of thy people thou shalt not misspeak.\it* \ef - \fr 23.5 \fq I knew not. \ft ¹ \+it Our Lord\+it* (saith S. Cyprian ² ) \+it in the Gospel, when it was said to him, Answerest thou the high Priest so? Teaching that the honour of Priesthood must be kept, said nothing to the high Priest, but only purging his innocency, said: If I have spoken evil, bear witness of evil: but if well, why smitest thou me? Also the blessed Apostle when it was said to him, Doest thou assail the high Priest so with ill words? Speak not any thing contumeliously against the Priest, whereas he might have put forth himself stoutly against them which had both crucified our Lord, and which had now also lost their God and Christ, Temple and Priesthood. But though in false and spoiled Priests, yet considering the very bare shadow of the name of Priests, he said, I knew not brethren that he was high Priest\+it*. By which words of the Apostle, either it may be thought he knew not indeed that he was in that function, because he had not been of long time in those parts: or else that he so said in respect of the abrogation of the high Priesthood of the Jews, whereby he knew this man not to be truly any Priest, as also because at this time they came not orderly to it by succession of Aaron and Law of Moyses, but by the Roman Emperor's favour, ³ as is said before: though (as it is lawful in such a case) the less to irritate them, he frameth his speech so as they might not take occasion of further accusation against him. \fq ¹ \ft The honour of Priesthood. \fq ² \ft \+it Cypr. ep. 65. 59. nu. 2.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it See Annot. Io. c. 11, 51.\+it*\ef*
\v 6 And Paul knowing that the one part was of Sadducees, and the other of Pharisees, \x - \xt Phil. 3, 5.\x* he cried out in the Council: Men brethren, \f 2 \fr 23.6 \ft Such prudent evasions from danger are lawful. Which S. Chrysostom calleth (specially in this Apostle) the wisdom of the serpent, as otherwise in his teaching and preaching & patience he used the simplicity of a dove.\f* I am a Pharisee, the son of Pharisees: of the hope and resurrection of the dead am I judged.
\v 7 And when he had said these things, there rose dissension between the Pharisees and Sadducees; and the multitude was divided.
\v 8 For the Sadducees say \x - \xt Mt. 22, 23.\x* there is no resurrection, nor Angel, nor spirit: but the Pharisees confess both. \ef - \fr 23.8 \fq The Sadducees. \ft ¹ This was the worst Heresy among the Jews, denying that there be any Angels, or spirits, the Resurrection also of the bodies: & consequently (as it may very well be gathered by the book of the Machabees ² ) they denied prayer for the dead. For to offer or pray for the dead, & to think rightly & religiously of the Resurrection, are made there sequels one of another. Of this sect of Sadducees was (as Eusebius writeth \+it li. 2. c. 22. Ec. hist.\+it* ) this Ananias the High Priest, that caused Paul to be smitten. For their Priesthood had now no more the protection of God to preserve it in truth and right judgement, the Christian Priesthood being then established. \fq ¹ \ft The Sadducees (as it seemeth) denied prayer for the dead. \fq ² \ft \+it Mac. li. 2. c. 12, 43.\+it*\ef*
\v 9 And there was made a great cry. And certain of the Pharisees rising up, strove saying: We find no evil in this man. What if a spirit hath spoken to him, or an Angel?
\v 10 And when there was risen great dissension, the Tribune fearing lest Paul should be torn in pieces by them, commanded the soldiers to go down, and to take him out of the midst of them, and to bring him into the castle.
\v 11 And the night following our Lord standing by him, said: Be constant; for as thou hast testified of me in Jerusalem, so \f 1 \fr 23.11 \ft Though God who could not lie, had promised Paul that he should go to Rome; yet the Apostle omitted not human means to defend himself from his enemies and otherwise. Neither said he as the Heretics called Predestinates, Let them do what they will, they cannot hurt me, for I am predestinate to go to Rome. See his doings and sayings to save himself, in the chap. following.\f* must thou testify at Rome also.
\v 12 And when day was come, certain of the Jews gathered themselves together, & vowed themselves, saying: that they would neither eat nor drink till they killed Paul. \ef - \fr 23.12 \fq Vowed themselves. \ft ¹ Such vows, oaths, or execrations as this, bind no man before God, yea they must in nowise be observed. It is a great offence either to vow voluntarily, or to take any such thing upon a man, for fear or by commandment. For example, if thou have rashly by promise, vow, or oath, appointed to be revenged upon any man, thou bindest not thyself thereby, neither must thou keep thy promise. If thou be put to an oath to accuse Catholics for serving God as they ought to do, or to utter any innocent man to God's enemies and his, thou oughtest first to refuse such unlawful oaths: but if thou have not constancy and courage so to do, yet know thou that such oaths bind not at all in conscience and Law of God, but may and must be broken under pain of damnation. For to make or take such vows or oaths, is one sin, and to keep them, is another far greater, as when Herod, to keep his oath, killed John Baptist ². And such vows and oaths to God as these, are unlawful & must be broken: and not the vows of Chastity and Religion, as our new Ministers teach by their words and works. \fq ¹ \ft Unlawful oaths & vows must not be kept. \fq ² \ft \+it Mat. 14, 9.\+it*\ef*
\v 13 And they were more than forty men that had made this conspiracy:
\v 14 who came to the chief priests and the ancients, and said: By execration we have vowed ourselves, that we will eat nothing, till we kill Paul.
\v 15 Now therefore give you knowledge to the Tribune with the Council, that he bring him forth to you, as if you meant to know some more certainty touching him. But we, before he come near, are ready for to kill him.
\v 16 Which when Paul's sister's son had heard, of their lying in wait, he came and entered into the castle and told Paul.
\v 17 And Paul calling to him one of the Centurions, said: Bring this young man to the Tribune, for he hath something to tell him.
\v 18 \f 1 \fr 23.18 \ft c See the courtesy & equity of Heathen Officers toward their prisoners, to save them from all injury and villainy.\f* And he taking him, brought him to the Tribune, and said: The prisoner Paul desired me to bring this young man unto thee, having something to say to thee.
\v 19 And the Tribune taking him by the hand, went aside with him apart, and asked him: What is it that thou hast to tell me?
\v 20 And he said: The Jews have agreed to desire thee, that tomorrow thou wilt bring forth Paul into the Council, as though they meant to inquire some more certainty touching him.
\v 21 But do not thou credit them; for there lie in wait for him more than forty men of them, which have vowed neither to eat nor to drink, till they kill him: and they are now ready, expecting thy promise.
\v 22 The Tribune therefore dismissed the young man, commanding that he should speak to no man that he had notified these things unto him.
\v 23 And calling two Centurions, he said to them: Make ready two hundred soldiers, to go as far as Cæsarea, and seventy horsemen, and lances two hundred, from the third hour of the night:
\v 24 and prepare beasts: that setting Paul on, they might bring him safe to Felix the President.
\v 25 (For he feared lest perhaps the Jews might take him away, and kill him, and himself afterward should sustain reproach, as though he would have taken money)
\v 26 writing a letter containing this much: \it Claudius Lysias to the most excellent President Felix, greeting.\it*
\v 27 This man being apprehended of the Jews, and ready to be killed of them, I coming in with the band delivered him, understanding that he is a Roman:
\v 28 and meaning to know the cause that they objected unto him, I brought him down into their Council.
\v 29 Whom I found to be accused concerning questions of their law: but having no crime worthy of death or of bands.
\v 30 And when it was told me of ambushments that they had prepared against him, I sent him to thee, signifying also to the accusers, to speak before thee. Fare-well.
\v 31 And the soldiers according as it was commanded them, taking Paul, brought him by night to Antipatris.
\v 32 And the next day sending away the horse-men to go with him, they returned to the castle.
\v 33 Who when they were come to Cæsarea, and had delivered the letter to the President, they did set Paul also before him.
\v 34 And when he had read, and had asked of what province he was: and understanding that of Cilicia:
\v 35 I will hear thee, said he, when thy accusers are come. And he commanded him to be kept in Herod's palace.
\c 24
\cd They prosecute him to Cæsarea, bringing with them an orator, who before the President Felix accuseth him. 10. He answereth, defending himself from the crimes they charged him with, but confessing his religion plainly. 22. The Judge perceiving his religion to be irreprehensible, yieldeth not to condemn him at their pleasure, 24. yea he offentimes with his wife heareth his preaching, 27. but yet doth not his duty to deliver him out of prison.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* after five days the high priest Ananias descended, with certain ancients and one Tertullus an orator, who went to the President against Paul.
\v 2 And Paul being cited, Tertullus began to accuse, saying: Whereas we live in much peace by thee, & many things are corrected by thy providence,
\v 3 we do always & in all places receive it, most excellent Felix, with all thanks-giving.
\v 4 But lest I hinder thee any longer, I desire thee of thy clemency briefly to hear us.
\v 5 We have found this man pestiferous, & raising seditions to all the Jews in the whole world, and author of the sedition \f 1 \fr 24.5 \ft \+it c τῆς αἱρέσεως.\+it*\f* of the sect of the Nazarenes.
\v 6 Who also hath attempted to violate the temple, whom also being apprehended we would have judged according to our law.
\v 7 But Lysias the Tribune coming in, with great force took him away out of our hands,
\v 8 commanding his accusers to come to thee, of whom thou mayest thyself judging, understand of all these things, whereof we accuse him.
\v 9 And the Jews also added, saying that these things were so.
\v 10 But Paul answered: (the President making a sign unto him for to speak) Knowing that of many years thou art judge over this nation, I will with good courage answer for myself.
\v 11 For thou mayest understand that it is not above twelve days to me, since I went up to adore in Jerusalem.
\v 12 And neither in the temple did they find me disputing with any man, or causing concourse of the multitude, neither in the synagogues, nor in the city:
\v 13 neither can they prove unto thee the things whereof they now accuse me.
\v 14 But this I confess to thee, that \f 1 \fr 24.14 \ft \+it c κατὰ τὴν ὁδὸν\+it*\f* according to the \f 2 \fr 24.14 \ft Because Tertullus the Jews' orator called Christian religion the sect or (as it is there vers. 5. in the Greek) the heresy of the Nazarens: S. Paul answereth and sheweth that it is no heresy. And as for the word, \+it Sect,\+it* in this place: it is in the Greek, \+it According to the way, which they call heresy,\+it* as also Act. 9, 2. 24, 22. And therefore the word sect here is so taken. See \+it Annot. c. 28, 22.\+it*\f* sect, which they call heresy, I do so serve \f 3 \fr 24.14 \fq the Father my God \ft \+it the God of my Fathers, (or) the Father, and my God,\+it*\f* the Father my God, believing all things that are written in the Law & the Prophets:
\v 15 having hope in God, the which these also themselves expect, that there shall be a resurrection of just and unjust.
\v 16 In this myself also do study to have a conscience without offence toward God and toward men always.
\v 17 And after many years \x - \xt Ro. 15, 28.\x* I came to bestow alms upon my nation, & oblations, and vows.
\v 18 In the which they found me \x - \xt Act. 21, 26.\x* purified in the temple: not with multitude nor with tumult.
\v 19 But certain Jews of Asia, who ought to be present before thee and to accuse, if they had any thing against me:
\v 20 or let these men themselves say, if they have found in me any iniquity, forasmuch as I stand in the Council,
\v 21 but of this one voice only that I cried standing among them, That \x - \xt Act. 23, 6.\x* of the resurrection of the dead am I judged this day of you.
\v 22 And Felix deferred them, knowing most certainly of this way, saying: When Lysias the Tribune is come down, I will hear you.
\v 23 And he commanded the Centurion to keep him, and that he should have rest, neither to prohibit any of his to minister unto him.
\v 24 And after some days, Felix coming with Drusilla his wife, which was a Jew, called Paul, and heard of him the faith that is in Christ \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 25 And he disputing of \f 1 \fr 24.25 \ft The Apostolic teaching was not of only or special faith, but of justice, & chastity, & judgement, that is to say, of the terror of Hell and other God's judgments in the next life answerable to our deeds in this world: by which the hearers were first terrified, and so induced to penance. How say Heretics then that such things make men hypocrites?\f* justice and chastity, and of the judgement to come, Felix being terrified, answered: For this time, go thy way: but in time convenient I will send for thee.
\v 26 hoping also withal, that money would be given him of Paul; for the which cause also oftentimes sending for him, he spake with him.
\v 27 But when two years were ended, Felix had a Successor Portius Festus. And Felix being willing to shew the Jews a pleasure, left Paul in prison.
\c 25
\cd After two years imprisonment the Jews continue their suit against him, soliciting the new President Festus, 6. first at Hierusalem, then at Cæsarea: 9. where through the Jews' partiality he is fain to appeal unto the Emperor: 13. and is in the mean time brought forth by Festus (giving him good testimony, notwithstanding the exclamations of the Jews against him) unto king Agrippa and his Queen Bernice.
\p
\v 1 \sc Festus\sc* therefore when he was come into the province, after three days went up to Jerusalem from Cæsarea.
\v 2 And the chief Priests, and principal men of the Jews went unto him against Paul: and they desired him,
\v 3 requesting favour against him, that he would command him to be brought to Jerusalem, laying wait for to kill him in the way.
\v 4 But Festus answered, that Paul is kept in Cæsarea: and that he would very shortly go thither.
\v 5 They therefore, saith he, that are of ability among you, going down with me, if there be any crime in the man, let them accuse him.
\v 6 And having tarried among them not above eight or ten days, he went down to Cæsarea, and the next day he sat in the judgement seat: and he commanded Paul to be brought.
\v 7 Who being brought, there stood about him the Jews that were come down from Jerusalem, objecting many and grievous \f 1 \fr 25.7 \ft \+it c crimes as v. 27.\+it*\f* causes which they could not prove;
\v 8 Paul making answer, That neither against the law of the Jews, nor against the temple, nor against Cæsar have I any thing offended.
\v 9 But Festus willing to shew the Jews a pleasure, answering Paul, said: Wilt thou go up to Jerusalem, and there be judged of these things before me?
\v 10 And Paul said: At Cæsar's judgement seat do I stand, where I ought to be judged: the Jews I have not hurt, as thou very well knowest.
\v 11 For if I have hurt them, or done any thing worthy of death, I refuse not to die. But if none of those things be, whereof these accuse me, no man can give me to them. \f 1 \fr 25.11 \ft If S. Paul both to save himself from whipping and from death sought by the Jews, doubted not to cry for succour of the Roman laws, and to appeal to Cæsar the Prince of the Romans not yet Christened: how much more may we call for aid of Christian Princes and their laws, for the punishment of Heretics, and for the Church's defence against them? \+it S. Augustine ep. 50.\+it*\f* I appeal to Cæsar.
\v 12 Then Festus having conferred with the Council, answered: Hast thou appealed to Cæsar? to Cæsar shalt thou go.
\v 13 And when certain days were passed, king Agrippa & Bernice came down to Cæsarea to salute Festus.
\v 14 And as they tarried there a good many days, Festus signified to the king, of Paul, saying: A certain person was left prisoner by Felix,
\v 15 concerning whom, when I was at Jerusalem, the chief Priests and the Ancients of the Jews came unto me, desiring condemnation against him.
\v 16 To whom I answered: That it is not the Romans' custom to yield up any man before that he which is accused have his accusers present, and take place to make his answer for to clear himself of the crimes.
\v 17 When they therefore were assembled hither, without any delay, the day following, sitting in the judgement seat, I commanded the man to be brought.
\v 18 Of whom, when the accusers stood up, they brought no cause which I thought ill of:
\v 19 but certain questions of their own superstition they had against him, and of \f 1 \fr 25.19 \ft This whom he termeth by contempt, one JESUS, hath now made all the Roman Emperors and Princes of the world to know him, and hath given the seat of the Cæsars to his poor servants, Peter & his successors.\f* one \sc Jesus\sc* deceased, whom Paul affirmed to live.
\v 20 Doubting therefore of this kind of question, I said, whether he would go to Jerusalem, & there be judged of these things.
\v 21 But Paul appealing to be kept unto the knowledge of Augustus, I commanded him to be kept, till I send him to Cæsar.
\v 22 And Agrippa said to Festus: Myself also would hear the man. Tomorrow, said he, thou shalt hear him.
\v 23 And the next day when Agrippa and Bernice were come with great pomp, and had entered into the hall of audience with the Tribunes and principal men of the city, at Festus' commandment Paul was brought.
\v 24 And Festus saith: King Agrippa, and all ye men that are present together with us, you see this man, concerning whom all the multitude of the Jews called upon me at Jerusalem, requesting and crying out that he ought not to live any longer.
\v 25 Yet have I found nothing that he hath committed worthy of death. But forasmuch as he himself appealed to Augustus, I have determined to send him.
\v 26 Of whom what to write for certainty to my lord, I have not. For the which cause I have brought him forth to you, and especially to thee, king Agrippa, that examination being made, I may have what to write.
\v 27 For it seemeth to me without reason, to send a prisoner, & not to signify \f 1 \fr 25.27 \ft \+it c τὰς κατ ἀυτοῦ αἰτίας.\+it*\f* his causes.
\c 26
\cd In that honorable Audience being permitted to speak, 2. he declareth to the king what he first was, 12. and how miraculously he was converted, 19. and that he hath preached since, as he was commanded from heaven, and as the Prophets had foretold of Christ. 24. Which strange tale Festus the Heathen President hearing, saith that he is mad. 25. But he answereth, and exhorteth them all to be Christians as he is. 30. They finally pronounce that he might be set at liberty, but only for his appeal.
\p
\v 1 \sc But\sc* Agrippa said to Paul: Thou art permitted to speak for thyself. Then Paul stretching forth his hand, began to make his answer:
\v 2 Touching all things whereof I am accused of the Jews, king Agrippa, I account myself happy for that I am to defend myself this day before thee,
\v 3 especially whereas thou knowest all things that are among the Jews, customs and questions: for the which cause I beseech thee, hear me patiently.
\v 4 And my life truly from my youth, which was from the beginning in my nation in Jerusalem, all the Jews do know:
\v 5 knowing me before from the beginning (if they will give testimony) that according to the most sure \f 1 \fr 26.5 \ft \+it c ἁίρεσιν\+it*\f* sect of our religion I lived a Pharisee.
\v 6 And now for the hope of the promise that was made of God to our fathers, do I stand subject to judgement:
\v 7 \f 1 \fr 26.7 \ft \+it b in quam\+it*\f* the which, our twelve tribes \f 2 \fr 26.7 \ft \+it c λατρεῦον\+it*\f* serving night and day, hope to come unto. Of the which hope, o king, I am accused of the Jews.
\v 8 What incredible thing is it judged with you, if God raise the dead?
\v 9 And myself truly had thought that I ought to do against the name of \sc Jesus\sc* of Nazareth many contrary things.
\v 10 Which also I \x - \xt Act. 8, 3.\x* did at Jerusalem, and many of the saints did I shut up in prisons, having received authority of the chief priests: and when they were put to death, \f 2 \fr 26.10 \ft \+it c detuli sententiam.\+it*\f* I brought the sentence.
\v 11 And throughout all the synagogues oftentimes punishing them, I compelled them to blaspheme: and yet more mad against them, I persecuted them even unto foreign cities.
\v 12 Among which things whiles \x - \xt Act. 9, 3.\x* I went to Damascus with authority and permission of the chief priests,
\v 13 at mid-day, in the way, I saw (o king) from Heaven a light to have shined round about me and them that were in company with me, above the brightness of the sun.
\v 14 And when all we were fallen down on the ground, I heard a voice speaking to me in the Hebrew tongue: Saul, Saul, why persecutest thou me? It is hard for thee to kick against the prick.
\v 15 And I said: Who are thou Lord? And our Lord answered: I am \sc Jesus\sc* whom thou doest persecute.
\v 16 But rise up and stand upon thy feet: for to this end have I appeared to thee, that I may ordain thee a minister and witness of those things which thou hast seen, and of those things wherein I will appear to thee,
\v 17 delivering thee out of the peoples and nations unto the which now I send thee,
\v 18 to open their eyes, that they may be converted from darkness to light, and from the power of Satan to God, that they may receive remission of sins, and lot among the saints by the faith that is in me.
\v 19 Whereupon, king Agrippa, I was not incredulous to the heavenly vision:
\v 20 but to them first that are at Damascus, and at Jerusalem, and unto all the country of Jewry, and to the Gentiles \x - \xt Act. 9, 20.\x* did I preach that they should do \f 2 \fr 26.20 \ft Penance often inculcated, and works agreeable to the same.\f* penance, & turn to God, doing works worthy of penance.
\v 21 For this cause the Jews, when I was in the temple, \x - \xt Act. 21, 30.\x* apprehending me, attempted meaning to kill me.
\v 22 But aided by the help of God, I stand until this day, testifying to small & to great, saying nothing beside those things which the Prophets did speak should come to pass, & Moyses;
\v 23 if \sc Christ\sc* were passible, if the first of the resurrection from the dead, he were to shew light to the people and to the Gentiles.
\v 24 As he spake these things and made his answer, Festus with a loud voice said: Thou art mad, Paul; much learning turneth thee to madness.
\v 25 And Paul said: I am not mad, most excellent Festus; but I speak words of verity & sobriety.
\v 26 For the king knoweth of these things, to whom also I speak constantly. For I think none of these things to be unknown to him. For neither was any of these things done in a corner.
\v 27 Believest thou the Prophets, king Agrippa? I know that thou believest.
\v 28 And Agrippa said to Paul: A little thou persuadest me to become a \sc Christian\sc*.
\v 29 And Paul said: I wish of God, both in little, and in much, not only thee, but also all that hear this day, to become such as I am also, except these bands.
\v 30 And the king rose up, and the President, and Bernice, and they that sat by them.
\v 31 And going aside, they spake among themselves, saying, That this man hath done nothing worthy of death or bands.
\v 32 And Agrippa said to Festus: This man might be released, if he had not appealed to Cæsar.
\c 27
\cd What a dangerous navigation he had toward Rome: and that by his prediction and counsel the ship might have been saved. And for his sake (as God also revealed to him before) all the company was preserved, being 276 persons.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* after it was decreed that he should sail into Italy, and that Paul with other prisoners should be delivered to a Centurion named Julius, of the band Augusta,
\v 2 we going up into a ship of Adrumetum, beginning to sail about the places of Asia, loosed from the land, Aristarchus the Macedonian of Thessalonica continuing with us.
\v 3 And the third day following we came to Sidon. And Julius entreating Paul courteously, permitted him to go to his friends, and to take care of himself.
\v 4 And when we had loosed thence we sailed under Cypres: because the winds were contrary.
\v 5 And sailing the sea of Cilicia and Pamphilia, we came to \f 1 \fr 27.5 \fq Lystra \ft \+it Myra\+it*\f* Lystra, which is in Lycia:
\v 6 and there the Centurion finding a ship of Alexandria sailing into Italy, removed us into it.
\v 7 And whereas many days we sailed slowly, and were scarce come over against Gnidus, the wind hindering us, we sailed near Crete by Salmone:
\v 8 and with much ado sailing by it, we came into a certain place that is called Good-havens, nigh to the which was a city Thalassa.
\v 9 And when much time was spent, and whereas now it was not safe sailing, because the \f 1 \fr 27.9 \ft It may signify the Jews' fast of the seventh month September, after which the navigation was perilous, winter approaching.\f* fast now was past, Paul comforted them,
\v 10 saying to them: Ye men, I see that the sailing beginneth to be with hurt and much damage, not only of the lading and the ship, but also of our lives.
\v 11 But the Centurion believed the governor and master of the ship, more than those things which were said of Paul.
\v 12 And whereas it was not a commodious haven to winter in, very many taking counsel appointed to sail thence, if by any means they might coming to Phoenice, winter there, a haven of Crete looking toward the \f 1 \fr 27.12 \ft c names of winds.\f* Afrike and the Chore.
\v 13 And the south-wind blowing, they thinking that they had obtained their purpose, when they had parted from Asson, sailed along by Crete.
\v 14 But not long after, a tempestuous wind that is called Euro-aquilo, drove against it.
\v 15 And when the ship was caught and could not make way against the wind, giving up the ship to the winds, we were driven,
\v 16 And running upon a certain Island, that is called \f 1 \fr 27.16 \ft c Græc. Clauda.\f* Cauda, we could scarce get the cock-boat.
\v 17 Which being taken up, they used helps, girding the ship, and fearing lest they should fall into \f 1 \fr 27.17 \ft c a place of quick sands.\f* the Syrt, letting down the vessel, so were they carried.
\v 18 And when we were mightily tossed with the tempest, the next day they cast forth.
\v 19 And the third day with their own hands they threw forth the tacklings of the ship.
\v 20 And neither sun, nor stars appearing for many days, and no small storm being toward, all hope was now taken away of our saving.
\v 21 And when there had been long fasting, then Paul standing in the midst of them, said: You should indeed, O ye men, have heard me, and not have parted from Crete, & have gained this hurt and loss.
\v 22 And now I exhort you to be of good cheer. For there shall be no loss of any soul among you, but of the ship.
\v 23 For an Angel of the God whose I am, and \f 1 \fr 27.23 \ft \+it c ᾧ λατρεύω.\+it*\f* whom I serve, stood by me this night, \ef - \fr 27.23 \fq An Angel. \ft ¹ S. Paul had many visions, specially to assure him that he should to Rome and stand before Cæsar, our Lord himself before (23, 11) appearing to him, and here an Angel, for that purpose. Whereby we plainly see the special providence of God toward that See, where his two principal Apostles were designed to preach, plant the faith, live, die, be buried, and honoured till the world's end. \fq ¹ \ft God's providence to the See of ROME.\ef*
\v 24 saying: Fear not Paul, thou must appear before Cæsar: and behold God hath \f 1 \fr 27.24 \ft Paul (saith S. Hierom) had so many souls in the ship given him, that is, so many men saved for his sake: and after he is with Christ, shall he shut his mouth, and not be able once to speak for them that have believed in his Gospel? \+it Hiero. adv. Vigil.\+it* Whereby he proveth that if God do much for the merits of saints in this life, much more at their intercession & prayer in Heaven.\f* given thee all that sail with thee.
\v 25 For the which cause be of good cheer ye men: for I believe God, that it shall so be, as it hath been said to me.
\v 26 And we must come unto a certain island.
\v 27 But after the fourteenth night was come on us, as we were sailing in Adria about mid-night, the ship-men deemed that there appeared some country to them.
\v 28 Who also sounding, found twenty fathoms: and being parted a little from thence, they found fifteen fathoms.
\v 29 And fearing lest we should fall into rough places, casting out of the stern four anchors, they wished that day were come.
\v 30 But as the ship-men sought to fly out of the ship, having let down the cock-boat into the sea, pretending as if they were about to cast out anchors out of the fore-part of the ship,
\v 31 Paul said to the Centurion and to the soldiers: unless these tarry in the ship you cannot be saved. \ef - \fr 27.31 \fq Unless these tarry. \ft ¹ When God revealeth to us any thing, or assureth us of any event to come, he dischargeth us not thereby of our requisite endeavours and labours for achieving the same, not executing ordinarily his designments towards men otherwise than by their own free-will and actions. S. Paul said not here, Let us do what we list: work we or sit we still, whether the mariners go out or tarry within, we are all sure to be saved, for so God hath revealed to me, and he cannot lie, neither can it fall otherwise. But contrarywise saith he, If these mariners leave the ship, you cannot be saved. So say all true Catholic preachers to Christian people, what providence, predestination, or foresight so ever God have of your salvation, you are not thereby constrained any way, you have free-will still, and cannot be saved (though you be predestinate) except you keep God's commandments, repent you of your sins, believe, live and die well. And if it were revealed to any man, that he were one of God's elect, & that he should finally die in grace and be saved, yet he were bound to work his salvation with fear and trembling, as ² S. Paul both did, and taught, lest he become reprobate: no less than the same Apostle here and his fellows, though they had their life promised to them of God, yet were bound to labour and use all possible diligence that they might not be drowned. \fq ¹ \ft God's predestination and appointment taketh not away man's free-will and endeavours. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Cor. 9, 27. Philip. 2, 12.\+it*\ef*
\v 32 Then the soldiers cut off the ropes of the cock-boat; and suffered it to fall away.
\v 33 And when it began to be light, Paul desired all to take meat, saying: This day is the fourteenth day that you expect and remain fasting, taking nothing.
\v 34 For the which cause I desire you to take meat for your health sake: for there shall not an hair of the head perish of any of you.
\v 35 And when he had said these things, taking bread he gave thanks to God in the sight of them all: and when he had broken it, he began to eat.
\v 36 And being all made of better cheer, they also took meat.
\v 37 And we were in all in the ship, souls two hundred seventy six.
\v 38 And being filled with meat, they lighted the ship, casting the wheat into the sea.
\v 39 And when day was come, they knew not the land: but they spied a certain creek that had a shore, into the which they minded, if they could, to cast aland the ship.
\v 40 And when they had taken up the anchors, they committed themselves to the sea, loosing withal the rudder bands: and hoisting up the main sail according as the wind blew, they went on toward the shore.
\v 41 And when we were fallen into a place between two seas, they graveled the ship: and the fore-part truly sticking fast remained unmoveable: but the hinder-part was broken by the violence of the sea.
\v 42 And the counsel of the soldiers was, that they should kill the prisoners: lest any swimming out, might run away.
\v 43 But the Centurion willing to save Paul, forbade it to be done: & he commanded them that could swim, to cast out themselves first, and escape, and go forth to land:
\v 44 and the rest, some they carried on boards, & some upon those things that were of the ship. And so it came to pass, that all the souls escaped to land.
\c 28
\cd After their shipwreck having wintered in the Island (now named Malta) where many miracles were wrought by Paul, they take ship again, and so by Sicily they come to Puteoli in Italy, the Christian Romans coming a great way to meet him, to his great joy. 16. Finally being come to Rome, in his lodging he declareth to the Jews his cause, 23. and on a day appointed preacheth JESUS unto them. 25. And seeing their incredulity, he sheweth how it was foretold by Esay: 28. but that the Gentiles will not be incredulous. 30. To whom he there preacheth two whole years without prohibition.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* when we had escaped, then we knew that the \f 1 \fr 28.1 \ft This island (now Malta) is the seat of the knights of the Rhodes: the inhabitants whereof have a special devotion to S. Paul: to whom both the chief Church (being the Bishop's Seat) is dedicated, and the whole Island (as they count it) consecrated. Where the people shew yet to strangers, his prison and other memories of his miracles.\f* island was called \f 2 \fr 28.1 \fq Mitilene \ft \+it Melita\+it*\f* Mitilene. But the Barbarous shewed us no small courtesy.
\v 2 For, kindling a fire they refreshed us all, because of the imminent rain and the cold.
\v 3 And when Paul had gathered together some number of sticks, and had laid them on the fire, a viper issuing out of the heat, invaded his hand.
\v 4 But as the Barbarous saw the beast hanging on his hand, they said one to another: Undoubtedly this man is a murderer, who being escaped out of the sea, \f 1 \fr 28.4 \ft \+it c ἡ δίκη\+it*\f* vengeance doth not suffer him to live.
\v 5 and he indeed shaking off the beast into the fire, suffered no harm. \ef - \fr 28.5 \fq Shaking off the beat. \ft The promise of Christ \+it (Marc. 16)\+it* that venomous serpents should not hurt them that believe in him, is fulfilled not in all believers, but in such as had the gift of miracles, as S. Paul had. Whom here a viper, by nature so venomous that the people thought he should have died out of hand, did no whit annoy: he extinguishing by the power of Christ all the poison of the beast. ¹ Yea and (as the Christian people there till this day believe) by S. Paul's prayers the Island was delivered for ever from all such venomous serpents ², in so much that children there play with scorpions ever since that time, and Pilgrims daily carry with them pieces of stones out of the place where S. Paul abode, by which they affirm that they heal them which in other countries adjoining are bitten of scorpions, the medicine therefore being called, \+it S. Paul's grace\+it*. The Heretics that know not the power of God, nor ³ the miraculous virtues given to his Saints, marvel and blaspheme, when they hear such things as be proper to certain countries, attributed sometimes to God's miracles done by his Saints: as though that were not possible, or were not as much to God's honour, and more, than things proceeding only of natural causes. Such profane men would not have attributed the wholesomeness of the waters of Jericho to Eliseus his virtue and miracles, amending them by casting salt into them ⁴, if the Scripture had not expressly testified the same. It is the part of all faithful men to refer such things to God, when any just occasion is given thereunto, rather than to nature: though the incredulous do always contrary, for fear of superstition & dishonouring God. As though this escape of drowning, might better and more to God's glory, be referred to chance and the mariners' industry, than to S. Paul's prayers and extraordinary working. \fq ¹ \ft Malta hath S. Paul's blessing and grace until this day. \fq ² \ft \+it Tho. Fazellus de rebus Siculis decad. 1. li. 1. c. 1.\+it* \fq ³ \ft God's miraculous virtue in certain countries and creatures, by his Saints. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it 4. Reg. 2, 19.\+it*\ef*
\v 6 But they supposed that he should be turned into a swelling, and that he would suddenly fall and die. But expecting long and seeing that there was no harm done on him, being changed they said, that he was a God.
\v 7 And in those places were lands of the prince of the isle, named Publius, who receiving us, for three days entreated us courteously.
\v 8 And it chanced that the father of Publius lay vexed with fevers and the bloody flux. Unto whom Paul entered: and when he had prayed, \f 1 \fr 28.8 \ft Miraculous healing by imposition of the Apostle's hands.\f* and imposed hands on him, he healed him.
\v 9 Which being done, all in the isle also that had infirmities, came, and were cured:
\v 10 who also honoured us with many honours, and when we were sailing away, laded us with necessaries.
\v 11 And after three months, we sailed in a ship of Alexandria, that had wintered in the island, whose sign was the Castors.
\v 12 And when we were come to Syracusa, we tarried there three days.
\v 13 Thence compassing by the shore, we came to Rhegium: and after one day the South-wind blowing, we came the second day to Puteoli,
\v 14 where finding brethren, we were desired to tarry with them seven days: and so we came to Rome.
\v 15 And from thence, when the Brethren had heard, they came to meet us unto \it Apij-forum,\it* and the Three-taverns, whom when Paul had seen, giving thanks to God, he took courage.
\v 16 And when we were come to Rome, Paul was permitted to remain to himself with a soldier that kept him.
\v 17 And after the third day he called together the chief of the Jews. And when they were assembled, he said to them: Men brethren, I doing nothing against the people, or the custom of the fathers, was delivered prisoner from Jerusalem into the hands of the Romans,
\v 18 who when they had examined me, would have released me, for that there was no cause of death in me.
\v 19 But the Jews contradicting it, I was compelled to appeal unto Cæsar, not as having any thing to accuse my nation.
\v 20 For this cause therefore I desired to see you and to speak to you. For, because of the hope of Israel, am I compassed with this chain. \ef - \fr 28.20 \fq Chain. \ft ¹ I would wish now (saith S. Chrysostome) to be for a time in the place where these chains remain, and to see the fetters which Devils fear, and Angels reverence. \+it homil. 5. ad populum Antiochenum\+it*. See also S. Gregory \+it lib. 3. episto. 30.\+it* of the miracles done by S. Paul's chains, and that he sendeth to the Empress Constantia some dust thereof filed off, for a great Relic and holy gift. \fq ¹ \ft S. Paul's chains honoured.\ef*
\v 21 But they said to him: We neither received letters concerning thee from Jewry, neither did any of the brethren that came hither, report or speak any evil of thee.
\v 22 But we desire of thee to hear what thou thinkest: for concerning this \f 1 \fr 28.22 \ft c αἱρέσεως.\f* sect, it is known to us that it is gainsaid every-where. \ef - \fr 28.22 \fq Concerning this sect. \ft The Heretics of all sorts comfort themselves much, when they find here or elsewhere the Christian faith called of the Jews or incredulous persons, a ¹ Sect or an Heresy, & sometimes in contempt of Christ's person the Master of the same, the Sect of the Nazarens: as though the Church of God might as well err in naming their doctrine Heresy, as the Jews and Pagans might and did miss in condemning Christian religion for an Heresy: or as though the Protestants' doctrine were as well proved and tried to be no Heresy, by the Prophets and other Scriptures, miracles, and consent of all Nations and ages, as Christ's blessed doctrine is. Whereas indeed the Protestants' doctrine is evidently convinced to be heretical, by the same arguments that Christ's religion is proved to be the only true doctrine of salvation, and not an heresy. And whosoever can deduce the Christian faith from Adam to this day, throughout all the Fathers, Patriarchs, Prophets, Priests, Apostles, and Bishops, by descent and succession of all laws and states of true worshippers and believers (which is the only or special way to prove that the Christian faith is no heresy) he shall by the same means all at once prove the Protestants' doctrine to be an heresy and a false sect. That the Jews therefore and ill men in all places contradicted the Christian religion, calling it an Heresy or a Sect, as though it had a beginning of some certain Sect-Master other than God himself, they were deceived: and the Church of God nevertheless calling the Protestants' doctrine Heresy in the worst part that can be, and in the worst sort that ever was, doth right and most justly. \fq ¹ \ft The name of Sect is well given to all Heresies, though the Christian religion at the first was falsely so called.\ef*
\v 23 And when they had appointed him a day, they came to him unto his lodging very many: to whom he expounded, testifying the Kingdom of God, and using persuasion to them of \sc Jesus\sc*, out of the Law of Moyses and the Prophets, from morning until evening.
\v 24 And certain believed those things that were said: but certain believed not.
\v 25 And whereas they did not agree among themselves, they departed, Paul saying one word: That well did the Holy Ghost speak by Esaie the Prophet to our fathers,
\v 26 saying: \x - \xt Esa. 6, 9. Mat. 13, 14. Mr. 4, 12. Lu. 8, 10. Io. 12, 40. Ro. 11, 8.\x* \it Go to this people, and say to them: With the ear you shall hear, and shall not understand: and seeing you shall see and shall not perceive.\it*
\v 27 \it For the heart of this people is waxen gross, and with their ears have they heavily heard, and their eyes \it* \f 1 \fr 28.27 \ft Here also \+it (as Mat. 13.)\+it* it is plain that they would not see, nor hear, & that their excecation is to be attributed to themselves & not to God. \+it See annota. Io. 12, 40.\+it*\f* \it they have shut: lest perhaps they may see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their heart, and be converted, and I heal them.\it*
\v 28 Be it known therefore to you, that this Salvation of God is sent to the Gentiles, and they will hear.
\v 29 And when he had said these things, the Jews went out from him, having much questioning among themselves.
\v 30 And he tarried full two years in his hired lodging: and he received all that came in to him,
\v 31 preaching the Kingdom of God, and teaching the things that concern our Lord \sc Jesus Christ\sc* with all confidence, without prohibition.
\s1 The end of the Acts of the Apostles.
\ip Whereunto we join for the reader's behalf, two Tables of the two chief Apostles, and a note of the rest, as an abridgement of the said book, and a supply of some things not there mentioned.
\ip The sum of the acts of the apostles, containing specially the gests of the two principal Apostles, SS. Peter and Paul, in such order of time and years of the Emperors, and from Christ's Nativity, and Ascension, as they were done: so far as by holy Scriptures or Ecclesiastical writers may be gathered. Wherein though it be not possible to set down the precise and undoubted time or year of every thing, because neither S. Luke nor others do note particularly and orderly the moments of every action of the said Apostles: yet we follow the most probable and plain plat that we find in the holy Scripture and ancient writers. Whereby the studious reader may easily discover the folly of the Protestants, that can find no time when Peter might possibly come to Rome, be Bishop, and die there: diverse things in S. Paul's acts being no less hard to reconcile to the course of S. Luke's narration, than any thing touching the history of S. Peter, namely his three years preaching in Arabia: all which must needs be true, whether we hit the very just time or no, and how so ever author's differ about the same.
\s1 OF THE OTHER APOSTLES.
\ip THE Acts of the rest of the twelve Apostles be not much written of in this book: but as \f - \ft Genebr. out of diverse authors.\f*other Ecclesiastical writers do testify, they preached specially in these nations, as followeth. \it Andrew in Achaia, John in Asia, Philip in Phrygia, James in Jewry, Bartholomew in Scythia, Thomas in India, Matthew in Aethiopia, Simon in Persia, Thaddaeus in Mesopotamia, the other James in Spain, Matthias in Palestine.\it* So distributing themselves throughout the world, to gather one Catholic Church of all Nations, according as Christ gave them commission \it Mat. 28, 19\it*: and as it was prophesied of them before, Psal. 18. \it Their sound is gone forth into every country, and their words into the ends of the whole world\it*. But before they departed one from another (the time whereof is not certainly known) \f - \ft Ruff. in expos. Symb. Apost. Ambr. ser. \+it 38.\+it* Hiero. ep. \+it 61. c. 9.\+it* advers. erro. Iov. Hieros.\f*all Twelve assembling together, & full of the Holy Ghost, each laying down his sentence, agreed upon twelve principal articles of the Christian faith, and appointed them for a rule to all believers: Which is therefore called and is THE APOSTLES' CREED: \it Not written in paper\it*, as the Scripture, \it but from the Apostles delivered by tradition\it*. Ruff. & Hiero. locis citatis. Which, as of old \it (Hiero. cont. Lucifer.)\it* so at this day all solemnly profess in their Baptism, either by themselves or by others: and all that be of age and capacity, are bound to know and believe every article of the same. Which are these that follow.
